(window.MAG=window.MAG||{}).d=(window.MAG.d||{});window.MAG.d["2-vienne"]=[["vol2/vienne/index.html","p1","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Clement, bishop, servant of the servants of God, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Clemens episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p2","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","A voice in high places has been heard of lamentation, weeping, and mourning, because the time has come, the time has come in which the Lord complains through the prophet: \"This house has become to me into fury and indignation.","Vox in excelso audita est lamentationis fletus et luctus quia venit tempus, tempus venit quo per prophetam conqueritur dominus: in furorem et indignationem mihi facta est domus haec."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p3","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","It shall be removed from before my sight on account of the wickedness of its sons, because they provoked me to wrath, turning their backs to me and not their faces, placing their idols in the house in which my name is invoked, that they might defile it.","Auferetur de conspectu meo propter malitiam filiorum suorum quia me ad iracundiam provocabant vertentes ad me terga et non facies ponentes idola sua in domo in qua invocatum est nomen meum ut polluerent ipsam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p4","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","They have built the high places of Baal to initiate and consecrate their sons to idols and demons.","Aedificaverunt excelsa Baal ut initiarent et consecrarent filios suos idolis atque daemoniis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p5","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","They have sinned deeply as in the days of Gibeah.\"","Profunde peccaverunt sicut in diebus Gabaa."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p6","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","At so horrendous a hearing and at such great horror of the widespread infamy—\"Who has ever heard such a thing? Who has seen anything like this?\"—I fell down when I heard, I was saddened when I saw, my heart was embittered, darknesses stupefied me.","Ad tam horrendum auditum tantum que horrorem vulgatae infamiae quod quis umquam audivit tale quis vidit huic simile corrui cum audirem contristatus sum cum viderem amaruit cor meum tenebrae exstupefecerunt me."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p7","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","For the voice of the people from the city, the voice from the temple, the voice of the Lord rendering retribution to his enemies.","Vox enim populi de civitate vox de templo vox domini reddentis retributionem inimicis suis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p8","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","The prophet is compelled to cry out: \"Give to them, O Lord, give to them a womb without children and dry breasts.\"","Exclamare propheta compellitur: da eis domine da eis vulvam sine liberis et ubera arentia."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p9","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Their iniquities have been revealed on account of their malice.","Nequitiae eorum revelatae sunt propter malitiam ipsorum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p10","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","\"Cast them out of your house, and let their root be dried up, let them by no means bear fruit, let this house no longer be an offense of bitterness and a thorn inflicting pain.\"","De domo tua eice illos et siccetur radix eorum fructum nequaquam faciant non sit ultra domus haec offendiculum amaritudinis et spina dolorem inferens."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p11","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","For her fornication is not small—she who sacrifices her sons, giving them and consecrating them to demons and not to God, to gods whom they did not know.","Non enim parva est fornicatio eius immolantis filios suos dantis illos et consecrantis daemoniis et non deo diis quos ignorabant."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p12","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Therefore \"this house shall be into solitude and reproach, into malediction and desert,\" greatly confounded and levelled with dust; its last things desolate, impassable, and dry; by the wrath of the Lord whom it despised, let it not be inhabited but be reduced into solitude, and let all be stupefied over it and hiss over all its plagues.","Propterea in solitudinem et opprobrium in maledictionem et in desertum erit domus haec confusa nimis et adaequata pulveri novissima deserta et invia et arens ab ira domini quem contempsit non habitetur sed redigatur in solitudinem et omnes super eam stupeant et sibilent super universis plagis eius."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p13","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","For the Lord chose the place not on account of the place but on account of the people.","Non enim propter locum gentem sed propter gentem locum elegit dominus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p14","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Therefore the place of the temple itself has been made a partaker of the people of evils, the Lord himself most openly preaching to Solomon building a temple for him, who was filled as if with a river with wisdom: \"If you shall have been turned away in aversion, you and your sons not following and worshiping me but going away and worshiping strange gods and adoring them, I will cast them away from my face and I will expel them from the land which I gave to them, and the temple which I sanctified to my name I will cast away from my face, and it shall be into a proverb and a fable and an example to the peoples.\"","Ideo et ipse locus templi particeps factus est populi malorum ipso domino ad Salomonem aedificantem sibi templum qui impletus est quasi flumine sapientia apertissime praedicante: si aversione aversi fueritis filii vestri non sequentes et colentes me sed abeuntes et colentes deos alienos et adorantes ipsos proiciam eos a facie mea et expellam de terra quam dedi eis et templum quod sanctificavi nomine meo a facie mea proiciam et erit in proverbium et in fabulam et populis in exemplum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p15","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","All passing by, seeing, shall be stupefied and shall hiss and shall say, \"Why has the Lord done thus to this temple and this house?\"","Omnes transeuntes videntes stupebunt et sibilabunt et dicent quare sic fecit dominus templo et domui huic."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p16","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","And they shall answer, \"Because they withdrew from the Lord their God who bought and redeemed them, and followed Baal and strange gods and adored them and worshiped them.\"","Et respondebunt quia recesserunt a domino deo suo qui emit et redemit eos et secuti sunt Baal et deos alienos et adoraverunt eos et coluerunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p17","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Therefore the Lord has brought upon them this great evil.","Idcirco induxit dominus super ipsos hoc malum grande."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p18","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Now, long ago around the beginning of our promotion to the apex of the supreme pontificate, even before we came to Lyons where we received the insignia of our coronation, and afterwards both there and elsewhere, the secret insinuation of certain persons intimated to us that the master, preceptors, and other brothers of the order of the military order of the Temple of Jerusalem, and even the order itself—which had been established in the overseas parts for the defense of the patrimony of our Lord Jesus Christ and were special champions of the catholic faith and principal defenders of the Holy Land, and seemed principally to carry out the business of that land, on account of which the sacrosanct Roman church, pursuing the same brothers and the order with the fullness of special favor, armed them against the enemies of Christ with the sign of the cross, exalted them with many honors, and fortified them with various liberties and privileges, and the hand of both itself and of all Christ's faithful, with manifold expenditure of goods, had been felt by them as helper in many ways and in many modes on account of this—had lapsed against the Lord Jesus Christ himself into the crime of nefarious apostasy, the detestable vice of idolatry, the execrable deed of the Sodomites, and various heresies.","Sane dudum circa nostrae promotionis ad apicem summi pontificatus initium etiam antequam Lugdunum ubi recepimus nostrae coronationis insignia veniremus et post tam ibi quam alibi secreta quorundam nobis insinuatio intimavit quod magister praeceptores et alii fratres ordinis militiae templi hierosolymitani et etiam ipse ordo qui ad defensionem patrimonii domini nostri Iesu Christi fuerant in transmarinis partibus constituti et speciales fidei catholicae pugiles et terrae sanctae praecipui defensores ipsius terrae negotium gerere principaliter videbantur propter quod sacrosancta Romana ecclesia eosdem fratres et ordinem specialis favoris plenitudine prosequens eos adversus Christi hostes crucis armavit signaculo multis exaltavit honoribus et diversis libertatibus et privilegiis communivit et tam ipsius quam cunctorum Christi fidelium manus cum multiplici erogatione bonorum sentiebant multifarie multis que modis propter hoc adiutrices contra ipsum dominum Iesum Christum in scelus apostasiae nefandae detestabile idolatriae vitium exsecrabile facinus Sodomorum et haereses varias erant lapsi."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p19","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","But because it was not likely nor did it seem credible that men so religious, who had often poured out their blood especially for the name of Christ and seemed frequently to expose their persons to the perils of death, who also often seemed frequently to display great signs of devotion both in divine offices and in fasts and other observances, were so unmindful of their salvation that they would perpetrate such things—especially since the same order had a good and holy beginning and had received the grace of approval from the apostolic see, and through the same see the rule of the order, as holy, reasonable, and just, merited to be approved—we were unwilling to incline our ear to such insinuation and denunciation, taught by the examples of the same our Lord and by the doctrines of canonical scripture.","Sed quia non erat verisimile nec credibile videbatur quod viri tam religiosi qui praecipue pro Christi nomine suum saepe sanguinem effuderunt ac personas suas mortis periculis frequenter exponere videbantur qui que magna tam in divinis officiis quam in ieiuniis et aliis observantiis devotionis signa frequentius praetendere videbantur suae sic essent salutis immemores quod talia perpetrarent praesertim cum idem ordo bonum et sanctum initium habuerit et a sede apostolica gratiam approbationis perceperit et per sedem eandem ipsius ordinis regula utpote sancta rationabilis atque iusta meruerit approbari eiusmodi insinuationi et delationi ipsorum eiusdem domini nostri exemplis et canonicae scripturae doctrinis edocti aurem noluimus inclinare."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p20","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Then, indeed, our dearest son in Christ, Philip, the illustrious king of the French, to whom the same crimes had been announced—not under the type of avarice, for he intended to claim or appropriate nothing for himself from the goods of the Templars, indeed he removed his hand from them entirely in his kingdom, but inflamed with the fervor of orthodox faith, following the illustrious footprints of his ancestors, informing himself as lawfully as he could concerning the premises—sent many and great informations to us through his nuncios and letters for instructing and informing us concerning these things.","Deinde vero carissimus in Christo filius noster Philippus rex Francorum illustris cui eadem fuerant facinora nuntiata non typo avaritiae cum de bonis templariorum nihil sibi vindicare aut appropriare intenderit immo ea in regno suo dimisit manum suam exinde totaliter amovendo sed fidei orthodoxae fervore suorum progenitorum vestigia clara sequens accensus de praemissis quantum licite potuit se informans ad instruendum et informandum nos super his multas et magnas nobis informationes per suos nuntios et litteras destinavit."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p21","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","But with infamy growing more strongly against the Templars themselves and their order concerning the aforesaid crimes, and because also a certain knight of the same order of great nobility and who was held as of no light opinion in the said order, having been sworn secretly before us, deposed that he in his reception, at the suggestion of the receiver, with certain other knights of the military order of the Temple present, denied Christ and spat upon the cross shown to him by the said receiver.","Infamia vero contra templarios ipsos et ordinem eorumdem increbrescente validius super sceleribus antedictis et quia etiam quidam miles eiusdem ordinis magnae nobilitatis et qui non levis opinionis in dicto ordine habebatur coram nobis secrete iuratus deposuit quod ipse in receptione sua ad recipientis suggestionem praesentibus quibusdam aliis militibus militiae templi negavit Christum et exspuit super crucem sibi a dicto recipiente ostensam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p22","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","He also said that he saw that the master of the military order of the Temple, who still lives, received in the convent of the said order overseas a certain knight in the same manner, namely with the denial of Christ and spitting upon the cross, about two hundred brothers of the same order being present, and he heard it said that thus was observed in the reception of the brothers of the said order that, at the suggestion of the receiver or of the one deputed for this, the one who was being received denied Jesus Christ and spat upon the cross shown to him in vituperation of Christ crucified, and the receiver and the received did certain other things which are not lawful nor agreeable to Christian honesty, just as he then confessed before us.","Dixit etiam se vidisse quod magister militiae templi qui vivit adhuc recepit in conventu dicti ordinis ultramarino quemdam militem eodem modo scilicet cum abnegatione Christi et exspuitione super crucem praesentibus bene ducentis fratribus eiusdem ordinis et audivit dici quod sic in receptione fratrum dicti ordinis servabatur quod ad recipientis vel ad hoc deputati suggestionem qui recipiebatur Iesum Christum negabat et super crucem sibi ostensam exspuebat in vituperium Christi crucifixi et quaedam alia faciebant recipiens et receptus quae non sunt licita nec christianae conveniunt honestati prout ipse tunc confessus exstitit coram nobis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p23","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","The debt of our office urging us to this, we were unable to avoid giving a hearing to so many and so great clamors.","Urgente nos ad id officii nostri debito vitare nequivimus quin tot et tantis clamoribus accomodaremus auditum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p24","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","But when finally, with public fame bearing witness and the clamorous insinuation of the said king and also of dukes, counts, and barons and other nobles, clerics also and people of the said kingdom of France coming to our presence on account of this both through themselves and through proctors and syndics, it had reached our hearing—which we report with sorrow—that the master, preceptors, and other brothers of the said order, and the order itself, were entangled with the aforesaid and many other crimes, and the premises seemed in a certain way to be proved through many confessions, attestations, and depositions of the aforesaid master, the visitor of France, and of many preceptors and brothers of the aforementioned order, made, had, and received before many prelates and the inquisitor of heretical depravity by preceding apostolic authority in the kingdom of France, and reduced into public writing, and shown to us and our brothers, and nevertheless the aforesaid fame and clamors had so much prevailed and also shown themselves both against the order itself and against individual persons of it, that it could not be passed over without grave scandal nor tolerated without imminent peril to the faith—we, following in the footsteps of Him whose vicar we bear on earth, although unworthily, for the purpose of inquiring concerning the premises, decreed that reason guiding, we should proceed, and we interrogated many of the preceptors, presbyters, knights, and other brothers of the said order of no small reputation, constituted in our presence, an oath having been administered to them nonetheless, and they having been adjured with no small affection through the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, under the ostentation of divine judgment and the intermination of eternal malediction, in virtue of holy obedience, that, then constituted in a safe and suitable place where they had no need to fear anything, notwithstanding the confessions made by them before others—through which we wished no prejudice to be made to the same confessors—they would tell us the pure and full truth concerning the premises concerning these things, and we examined them up to the number of seventy-two, many of our brothers assisting us diligently, and we caused their confessions, reduced into authentic writing through public hands, immediately in the presence of us and of the said our brothers, and then after the interposition of some days, in the consistory, to be read before them, and those [confessions] to be explained to each of them in their vernacular; and they, persevering in them, expressly and spontaneously approved them as they had been recited.","Sed cum demum fama publica deferente ac clamosa insinuatione dicti regis necnon et ducum comitum et baronum et aliorum nobilium clericorum quoque et populi dicti regni Francorum ad nostram propter hoc tam per se quam per procuratores et syndicos praesentiam venientium ad nostram quod dolenter referimus audientiam pervenisset quod magister praeceptores et alii fratres dicti ordinis et ipse ordo praefatis et pluribus aliis erant criminibus irretiti et praemissa per multas confessiones attestationes et depositiones praefati magistri visitatoris Franciae ac plurium praeceptorum et fratrum ordinis praelibati coram multis praelatis et haereticae pravitatis inquisitore auctoritate apostolica praecedente in regno Franciae factas habitas et receptas et in publicam scripturam redactas nobis que et fratribus nostris ostensas probari quodammodo viderentur ac nihilominus fama et clamores praedicti in tantum invaluissent et etiam ostendissent tam contra ipsum ordinem quam contra personas singulares eiusdem quod sine gravi scandalo praeteriri non poterat nec absque imminenti fidei periculo tolerari nos illius cuius vices licet immeriti in terris gerimus vestigiis inhaerentes ad inquirendum de praedictis ratione praevia duximus procedendum multos que de praeceptoribus presbyteris militibus et aliis fratribus dicti ordinis reputationis non modicae in nostra praesentia constitutos praestito ab eis nihilominus iuramento et eis cum affectione non modica per patrem et filium et spiritum sanctum sub ostentatione divini iudicii ac interminatione maledictionis aeternae in virtute sanctae oboedientiae adiuratis quod tunc in loco tuto et idoneo constituti ubi nihil eos timere oportebat non obstantibus confessionibus per eos coram aliis factis per quas eisdem confitentibus nullum fieri praeiudicium volebamus super praemissis meram et plenam nobis dicerent veritatem super his interrogavimus et usque ad numerum septuaginta duorum examinavimus multis ex fratribus nostris nobis assistentibus diligenter eorum que confessiones per publicas manus in authenticam scripturam redactas illico in nostra et dictorum fratrum nostrorum praesentia ac deinde interposito aliquorum dierum spatio in consistorio legi fecimus coram ipsis et illas in suo vulgari cuilibet eorum exponi qui perseverantes in illis eas expresse et sponte prout recitatae fuerant approbarunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p25","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","After which, intending to inquire concerning the premises by ourselves into the general master, the visitor of France, and the chief preceptors of the aforesaid order, we, being at Poitiers, commanded that the same general master and the visitor of France and the greater preceptors of the overseas land, Normandy, Aquitaine, and Poitou be presented to us.","Post quae cum generali magistro visitatore Franciae et praecipuis praeceptoribus praefati ordinis intendentes super praemissis inquirere per nos ipsos ipsum generalem magistrum et visitatorem Franciae ac terrae ultramarinae Normanniae Aquitaniae ac Pictaviae praeceptores maiores nobis Pictavis exsistentibus mandavimus praesentari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p26","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","But because some of them were so ill at that time that they could not ride nor be conveniently brought to our presence, we, wishing to know the truth concerning all the premises and whether those things which were contained in their confessions and depositions which they were said to have made before the inquisitor of heretical depravity in the aforesaid kingdom of France, certain public notaries and many other good men being present, were true—[these] having been exhibited and shown to us and our brothers by the same inquisitor under public hands—commissioned and commanded our beloved sons Berengar, then of the title of Nereus and Achilleus, now bishop of Tusculum, and Stephen, priest cardinal of the title of Saint Cyriacus in the Baths, and Landulf, deacon cardinal of the title of Saint Angelus, concerning whose prudence, experience, and fidelity we have indubitable trust, that they should inquire with diligence concerning the truth with the aforesaid general master, visitor, and preceptors, both against them and individual persons of the same order generally and against the order itself concerning the premises, and whatever they should find concerning these things they should refer to us, and they should take care to bring and present their confessions and depositions, reduced into writing by a public hand, to our apostolate, about to impart to the same master, visitor, and preceptors the benefit of absolution from the sentence of excommunication which they had incurred for the premises if they were true, if they should humbly and devotedly seek absolution as they ought, according to the form of the church.","Sed cum quidam ex eis sic infirmabantur tunc temporis quod aequitare non poterant nec ad nostram praesentiam commode adduci nos scire volentes de praemissis omnibus veritatem et an vera essent quae continebantur in eorum confessionibus et depositionibus quas coram inquisitore pravitatis haereticae in regno Franciae supradicto praesentibus quibusdam notariis publicis et multis aliis bonis viris dicebatur fecisse nobis et fratribus nostris per ipsum inquisitorem sub manibus publicis exhibitas et ostensas dilectis filiis nostris Berengario tunc tituli Nerei et Achillei nunc episcopo Tusculano et Stephano tituli sancti Cyriaci in thermis presbytero et Landulfo titulo sancti Angeli diacono cardinalibus de quorum prudentia experientia et fidelitate indubitatam fiduciam obtinemus commisimus et mandavimus ut ipsi cum praefatis magistro generali visitatore ac praeceptoribus inquirerent tam contra ipsos et singulares personas ipsius ordinis generaliter quam contra ipsum ordinem super praemissis cum diligentia veritatem et quidquid super his invenirent nobis referre ac eorum confessiones et depositiones per manum publicam in scriptis redactas nostro apostolatui deferre ac praesentare curarent eidem magistro ac visitatori et praeceptoribus beneficium absolutionis a sententia excommunicationis quam pro praemissis si vera essent incurrerant si absolutionem humiliter ac devote peterent ut debebant iuxta formam ecclesiae impensuri."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p27","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","The cardinals, personally approaching the same general master, visitor, and preceptors, explained to them the cause of their arrival.","Qui cardinales ad ipsos generalem magistrum visitatorem et praeceptores personaliter accedentes eis sui adventus causam exposerunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p28","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","And because the persons of them and of the other Templars residing in the kingdom of France had been handed over to us so that they would freely, without fear of anyone, fully and purely tell the truth concerning all the premises to the same cardinals, they enjoined upon them by apostolic authority.","Et quoniam personae ipsorum et aliorum templariorum in regno Franciae consistentium nobis traditae fuerant quod libere absque metu cuiusquam plene ac pure super praemissis omnibus ipsis cardinalibus dicerent veritatem eis auctoritate apostolica iniunxerunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p29","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","The master, visitor, and preceptors of the lands of Normandy, overseas, Aquitaine, and Poitou, before the same three cardinals, four public tabellions and many other good men being present, an oath having been administered to them that they would tell the pure and full truth concerning all the premises, placed upon the holy gospels of God touched corporally by them, deposed and confessed before them singularly, freely, and spontaneously, without any coercion or terror, among other things, the denial of Christ and the spitting upon the cross when they were received into the order of the Temple, and some of them [confessed] that they had also received many brothers under the same form, namely with the denial of Christ and spitting upon the cross.","Qui magister visitator et praeceptores terrae Normanniae ultramarinae Aquitaniae et Pictaviae coram ipsis tribus cardinalibus praesentibus quatuor tabellionibus publicis et multis aliis bonis viris ad sancta dei evangelia ab eis corporaliter tacta praestito iuramento quod super praemissis omnibus meram et plenam dicerent veritatem coram ipsis singulariter libere ac sponte absque coactione qualibet et terrore deposuerunt et confessi fuerunt inter cetera Christi abnegationem ac exspuitionem super crucem cum in ordine templi recepti fuerunt et quidam ex eis se sub eadem forma scilicet cum abnegatione Christi et exspuitione super crucem fratres multos etiam recepisse."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p30","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","There are also some of them who confessed certain other horrible and dishonorable things, which we suppress for the present.","Sunt etiam quidam ex eis quaedam alia horribilia et inhonesta confessi quae subticemus ad praesens."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p31","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Moreover, they said and confessed that those things which are contained in their confessions and depositions which they had previously made before the aforesaid inquisitor are true.","Dixerunt praeterea et confessi fuerunt ea vera esse quae in eorum confessionibus et depositionibus continentur quas dudum fecerant coram inquisitore praefato."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p32","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Which confessions and depositions of the said general master, visitor, and preceptors, reduced into public writing by four public tabellions, were read in the presence of the same master, visitor, and preceptors and of certain other good men, and afterwards, after the interposition of some days, before the same, by mandate and in the presence of the aforesaid cardinals, and were explained in their vernacular to each of them.","Quae confessiones et depositiones dictorum generalis magistri visitatoris et praeceptorum in scripturam publicam per quatuor tabelliones publicos redactae in ipsorum magistri visitatoris et praeceptorum et quorumdam aliorum bonorum virorum praesentia ac deinde interposito aliquorum dierum spatio coram ipsis eisdem lectae fuerunt de mandato et in praesentia cardinalium praedictorum et in suo vulgari expositae cuilibet eorumdem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p33","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Who, persevering in them, expressly and spontaneously approved them as they had been recited.","Qui perseverantes in illis eas expresse et sponte prout recitatae fuerant approbarunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p34","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","And after confessions and depositions of this kind, they sought absolution from the excommunication which they had incurred for the premises from the same cardinals, on bended knees and with clasped hands, humbly and devotedly, and with no small shedding of tears.","Et post confessiones et depositiones huiusmodi ab ipsis cardinalibus ab excommunicatione quam pro praemissis incurrerant absolutionem flexis genibus manibus que complexis humiliter et devote ac cum lacrimarum effusione non modica petierunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p35","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","The cardinals indeed, because the church does not close its bosom to the one returning, the heresy having been expressly abjured by the same master, visitor, and preceptors, imparted to them the benefit of absolution according to the form of the church by our authority, and then, returning to our presence, presented to us the confessions and depositions of the aforementioned master, visitor, and preceptors reduced into public writing by public hands as has been said, and reported what they had done with the said master, visitor, and preceptors.","Ipsi vero cardinales quia ecclesia non claudit gremium redeunti ab iisdem magistro visitatore et praeceptoribus haeresi abiurata expresse ipsis secundum formam ecclesiae auctoritate nostra absolutionis beneficium impenderunt ac deinde ad nostram praesentiam redeuntes confessiones et depositiones praelibatorum magistri visitatoris et praeceptorum in scripturam publicam redactas per manus publicas ut est dictum nobis praesentarunt et quae cum dictis magistro visitatore et praeceptoribus fecerant retulerunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p36","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","From which confessions and depositions and report we find that the oft-said master, visitor of France, [and] preceptors of the overseas land, Normandy, Aquitaine, and Poitou have gravely sinned in the premises and concerning the premises, although some of them in more things and others in fewer.","Ex quibus confessionibus et depositionibus et relatione invenimus saepe fatum magistrum visitatorem terrae ultramarinae Normanniae Aquitaniae et Pictaviae praeceptores in praemissis et circa praemissa licet quosdam ex eis in pluribus et alios in paucioribus graviter deliquisse."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p37","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Attending moreover that so horrendous crimes could not and ought not to pass uncorrected without injury to omnipotent God and to all catholics, we decreed by the counsel of our brothers that inquiry be made concerning the aforesaid crimes and excesses against the individual persons of the same order and also against the said order by the ordinaries of the places and by other faithful and wise men to be deputed for this by us.","Attendentes autem quod scelera tam horrenda transire incorrecta absque omnipotentis dei et omnium catholicorum iniuria non poterant nec debebant decrevimus de fratrum nostrorum consilio per ordinarios locorum ac per alios fideles ac sapientes viros ad hoc deputandos a nobis contra singulares personas ipsius ordinis necnon et contra dictum ordinem per certas discretas personas quas ad hoc duximus deputandas super praemissis criminibus et excessibus inquirendum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p38","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","After these things, inquests were made both by the ordinaries and by those deputed by us against the individual persons of the said order and by the inquisitors whom we decreed to be deputed for this against the order itself, through all the parts of the world in which the brothers of the said order are accustomed to dwell; and those inquests which had been made against the aforementioned order were remitted to our examination—some by us and our brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church, others by many very learned, prudent, faithful, God-fearing, and zealous for the catholic faith, and experienced men, both prelates and others, at Malausana in the diocese of Vaison, and were very diligently read and skillfully examined.","Post haec tam per ordinarios quam per deputatos a nobis contra singulares personas dicti ordinis et per inquisitores quos ad hoc duximus deputandos contra ipsum ordinem per universas mundi partes in quibus consueverint fratres dicti ordinis habitare inquisitiones factae fuerunt et illae quae factae contra ordinem praelibatum fuerant ad nostrum examen remissae quaedam per nos et fratres nostros sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinales aliae vero per multos viros valde litteratos prudentes fideles deum timentes et fidei catholicae zelatores et exercitatos tam praelatos quam alios apud Malausanam Vacionensis dioeceseos fuerunt valde diligenter lectae et examinatae solerter."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p39","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","After which, when we had come to Vienne and there were already very many patriarchs, archbishops, bishops, elect, exempt and non-exempt abbots, and other prelates of churches, and also the proctors of absent prelates and chapters, gathered there for the council convoked by us, we, after the first session which we held there with the said cardinals and with the aforesaid prelates and proctors—in which we deemed it necessary to explain to them the causes of the convocation of the council—because it was difficult, indeed almost impossible, for the aforesaid cardinals and all the prelates and proctors gathered in the present council to meet in our presence to treat concerning the mode of proceeding in and about the fact or business of the brothers of the aforesaid order, by our mandate certain patriarchs, archbishops, bishops, exempt and non-exempt abbots, and other prelates of churches and proctors from all parts of Christendom, of whatever languages, nations, and regions, who were believed to be the more expert, discreet, and suitable for counseling in such and so great a business and for treating together with us and with the aforesaid cardinals so solemn a fact or business, were concordantly elected and assumed.","Post quae dum venissemus Viennam et essent iam quamplures patriarchae archiepiscopi episcopi electi abbates exempti et non exempti et alii ecclesiarum praelati necnon et procuratores absentium praelatorum et capitulorum ibidem pro convocato a nobis concilio congregati nos post primam sessionem quam inibi cum dictis cardinalibus et cum praefatis praelatis et procuratoribus tenuimus in quo causas convocationis concilii eisdem duximus exponendas quia erat difficile immo fere impossibile praefatos cardinales et universos praelatos et procuratores in praesenti concilio congregatos ad tractandum de modo procedendi super et in facto seu negotio fratrum ordinis praedictorum in nostra praesentia convenire de mandato nostro ab universis praelatis et procuratoribus in hoc concilio exsistentibus certi patriarchae archiepiscopi episcopi abbates exempti et non exempti et alii ecclesiarum praelati et procuratores de universis christianitatis partibus quarumcumque linguarum nationum et regionum qui de peritioribus discretioribus et idoneioribus ad consulendum in tali et tanto negotio et ad tractandum una nobis cum et cum cardinalibus antedictis tam solemne factum sive negotium credebantur electi concorditer et assumpti fuerunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p40","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","After which, we caused the aforesaid attestations received upon the inquest of the aforementioned order to be publicly read before the same prelates and proctors for as many days and as much as they themselves wished to hear, in the place deputed for holding the council, namely in the cathedral church, and subsequently they were seen, read, and examined with great diligence and solicitude, not perfunctorily but with a deliberative treatment, by many of our venerable brothers—the patriarch of Aquileia, archbishops, and bishops, existing in the present sacred council, elected and deputed for this by those elected by the whole council.","Post quae praefatas attestationes super inquisitionem ordinis praelibati receptas coram ipsis praelatis et procuratoribus per plures dies et quantum ipsi voluerunt audire publice legi fecimus in loco ad tenendum concilium deputato videlicet in ecclesia cathedrali et subsequenter per multos venerabiles fratres nostros patriarcham Aquileiensem archiepiscopos et episcopos in praesenti sacro concilio exsistentes electos et deputatos ad hoc per electos a toto concilio cum magna diligentia et sollicitudine non perfunctorie sed moratoria tractatione dictae attestationes ac rubricae super his factae visae perlectae et examinatae fuerunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p41","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","With the aforesaid cardinals, patriarchs, archbishops, bishops, exempt and non-exempt abbots, and other prelates and proctors elected by others as premised, constituted in our presence on account of the aforementioned business, a proposition and secret consultation having been made by us as to how one ought to proceed in the same business—especially since certain Templars offered themselves for the defense of the same order—it seemed indubitable to the greater part of the said cardinals and to almost the whole council, namely to those who as premised were elected by the whole council and represent the place of the whole council in this matter, or to a much greater part, indeed to four or five parts of the same, of whatever nation existing in the council; and so the said prelates and proctors gave their counsels that defense ought to be given to the order itself, and that the order itself cannot be condemned concerning the heresies concerning which inquiry has been made against it, through those things which have been proved so far, without offense to God and injury of law—others certain, saying on the contrary that the said brothers are not to be admitted to the defense of the said order, nor ought we to give defense to the same; for if, as they said, the defense of the same order be admitted or given, from this the peril of the business itself and no small detriment to the aid of the Holy Land would follow, and altercation and retardation and delay of the decision of the business itself; and alleging many reasons and various ones.","Praefatis itaque cardinalibus patriarchis archiepiscopis et episcopis abbatibus exemptis et non exemptis et aliis praelatis et procuratoribus ab aliis ut praemittitur electis propter praemissum negotium in nostra praesentia constitutis facta per nos propositione et consultatione secreta qualiter esset in eodem negotio procedendum praesertim cum quidam templarii ad defensionem eiusdem ordinis se offerrent maiori parti cardinalium et toti fere concilio illis videlicet qui a toto concilio ut praemittitur sunt electi et quoad hoc vices totius concilii repraesentant vel parti multo maiori quinimo quatuor vel quinque partibus eorundem cuiuscumque nationis in concilio exsistentium indubitatum videbatur et ita dicti praelati et procuratores sua consilia dederunt quod ipsi ordini defensio dari deberet et quod ipse ordo de haeresibus de quibus inquisitum est contra ipsum per ea quae hactenus sunt probata absque offensa dei et iuris iniuria condemnari nequeat aliis quibusdam e contra dicentibus dictos fratres non esse ad defensionem dicti ordinis admittendos nec nos dare debere defensionem eidem si enim ut dicebant praemissi eiusdem ordinis defensio admittatur vel detur ex hoc ipsius negotii periculum et non modicum terrae sanctae subsidii detrimentum sequeretur et altercatio et retardatio ac decisionis ipsius negotii dilatio ad haec multas rationes et varias allegantes."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p42","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","But although from the processes had against the aforementioned order, it cannot be canonically condemned as heretical by a definitive sentence, nevertheless because the same order is very much defamed concerning those heresies which are imputed to it, and because almost infinite persons of that order, among whom are the general master, the visitor of France, and the greater preceptors of it, have been convicted through their spontaneous confessions of the aforesaid heresies, errors, and crimes, and because even the same confessions render the said order very suspect, and because the aforementioned infamy and suspicion render the said order too abominable and hateful to the holy church of God and to its prelates and to kings and other princes and to other catholics, and because it is also believed likely that henceforth no good person would be found who would wish to enter the said order, on account of which the order itself would be rendered useless to the church of God and to the prosecution of the business of the Holy Land—for whose service they had been deputed—since moreover from the delay of the decision or ordinance of the said business, to which a peremptory term had been assigned by us in the present council to the aforesaid order and brothers, the total loss, destruction, and dilapidation of the goods of the Temple, which have been given, bequeathed, and granted long ago by Christ's faithful for the aid of the Holy Land and the attack of the enemies of the Christian faith, would follow, as is believed likely—between those who say that from now on a sentence of condemnation is to be promulgated against the said order for the said crimes, and others who say that a sentence of condemnation cannot be lawfully pronounced from the processes previously had against the said order, a long and mature deliberation having been held beforehand, having only God before our eyes and having regard to the utility of the business of the Holy Land, not declining to the right or to the left, we decreed that the way of provision and ordinance be chosen by which scandals shall be removed, perils avoided, and goods preserved for the aid of the Holy Land.","Verum licet ex processibus habitis contra ordinem memoratum ipse ut haereticalis per diffinitivam sententiam canonice condemnari non possit quia tamen idem ordo de illis haeresibus quae imponuntur eidem est plurimum diffamatus et quia quasi infinitae personae illius ordinis inter quas sunt generalis magister visitator Franciae et maiores praeceptores ipsius per eorum confessiones spontaneas de praedictis haeresibus erroribus et sceleribus sunt convictae quia etiam ipsae confessiones dictum ordinem reddunt valde suspectum et quia infamia et suspicio praelibatae dictum ordinem reddunt ecclesiae sanctae dei et praelatis eiusdem ac regibus aliis que principibus et caeteris catholicis nimis abominabilem et exosum quia etiam verisimile creditur quod amodo bona non reperiretur persona quae dictum ordinem vellet intrare propter quae ipse ordo ecclesiae dei ac prosecutioni negotii terrae sanctae ad cuius servitium fuerant deputati inutilis redderetur quoniam insuper ex dilatione decisionis seu ordinationis dicti negotii ad quam faciendam vel sententiam promulgandam terminus peremptorius fuerat in praesenti concilio praefatis ordini et fratribus assignatus a nobis bonorum templi quae dudum ad subsidium terrae sanctae et impugnationem inimicorum fidei christianae a Christi fidelibus data legata et concessa fuerunt totalis amissio destructio et dilapidatio ut probabiliter creditur sequeretur inter eos qui dicunt ex nunc contra dictum ordinem pro dictis criminibus condemnationis sententiam promulgandam et alios qui dicunt ex processibus praehabitis contra dictum ordinem condemnationis sententiam iure ferri non posse longa et matura deliberatione praehabita solum deum habentes prae oculis et ad utilitatem negotii terrae sanctae respectum habentes non declinantes ad dexteram vel sinistram viam provisionis et ordinationis duximus eligendam per quam tollentur scandala vitabuntur pericula et bona conservabuntur subsidio terrae sanctae."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p43","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Considering therefore the infamy, suspicion, clamorous insinuation, and other things aforesaid which stand against the aforesaid order, and also the occult and clandestine reception of the brothers of that order, and the difference of many brothers of the same from the common conversation, life, and morals of other of Christ's faithful, especially in that those receiving some as brothers of their order caused those received in that reception to make a profession and to swear to reveal the mode of reception to no one nor to leave that religion—from which there is evident presumption against them—attending moreover to the grave scandal which has arisen against the aforementioned order from the aforesaid, which would not seem able to be calmed while the same order remains, and also the perils of faith and of souls and the many horrible deeds of very many brothers of the said order, and many other just reasons and causes which reasonably and fittingly were able to move our mind to the undersigned—because to the greater part of the said cardinals and of the aforesaid elected by the whole council, more than four or five parts of them, it seemed more decent and expedient and more useful for the honor of God and for the preservation of the Christian faith and the aid of the Holy Land, and for many other valid reasons, that the way of ordinance and provision of the apostolic see be followed rather than the observations of the right of defense and the prolongations of the negotiation, by abolishing the oft-said order and applying the goods to the use for which they were deputed, and by wholesome provision concerning the persons of the order itself who live—attending also that even apart from the fault of the brothers the Roman church has sometimes caused other solemn orders to cease for incomparably lesser causes than the premises—not without bitterness and sorrow of heart, not by way of a definitive sentence but by way of provision or apostolic ordinance, we abolish the aforesaid order of the Temple and its state, habit, and name by an irrevocable and perpetually valid sanction, and we subject it to perpetual prohibition, the sacred council approving, more strictly inhibiting that anyone presume henceforth to enter the said order or to receive or wear its habit or to bear himself as a Templar.","Considerantes itaque infamiam suspicionem clamosam insinuationem et alia supradicta quae contra ordinem faciunt supradictum necnon et occultam et clandestinam receptionem fratrum ipsius ordinis differentiam que multorum fratrum eiusdem a communi conversatione vita et moribus aliorum Christi fidelium in eo maxime quod recipientes aliquos in fratres sui ordinis receptos in ipsa receptione professionem emittere faciebant et iurare modum receptionis nemini revelare nec religionem illam exire ex quibus contra eos praesumitur evidenter attendentes insuper grave scandalum ex praedictis contra ordinem praelibatum subortum fuisse quod non videretur posse sedari eodem ordine remanente necnon et fidei et animarum pericula et quamplurimorum fratrum dicti ordinis horribilia multa facta et multas alias rationes iustas et causas quae nostrum ad infrascripta movere animum rationabiliter et debite potuerunt quia et maiori parti dictorum cardinalium et praedictorum a toto concilio electorum plus quam quatuor vel quinque partibus eorumdem visum est decentius et expedientius et utilius pro dei honore et pro conservatione fidei christianae ac subsidio terrae sanctae multis que aliis rationibus validis sequendam fore potius viam ordinationis et provisionis sedis apostolicae ordinem saepe fatum tollendo et bona ad usum ad quem deputata fuerant applicando de personis etiam ipsius ordinis quae vivunt salubriter providendo quam defensionis iuris observationes et negotii prorogationes animadvertentes quoque quod alias etiam sine culpa fratrum ecclesia Romana fecit interdum alios ordines solemnes ex causis incomparabiliter minoribus quam sint praemissae cessare non sine cordis amaritudine et dolore non per modum diffinitivae sententiae sed per modum provisionis seu ordinationis apostolicae praefatum templi ordinem et eius statum habitum atque nomen irrefragabili et perpetuo valitura tollimus sanctione ac perpetuae prohibitioni subicimus sacro concilio approbante districtius inhibentes ne quis dictum ordinem de cetero intrare vel eius habitum suscipere vel portare aut pro templario gerere se praesumat."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p44","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","But if anyone shall have acted against this, let him incur the sentence of excommunication ipso facto.","Quod si quis contra fecerit excommunicationis incurrat sententiam ipso facto."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p45","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Moreover, we reserve to the ordinance and disposition of us and the apostolic see the persons and the same goods—which, with divine favor assisting, we intend to do for the honor of God and the exaltation of the Christian faith and the prosperous state of the Holy Land before this present sacred council is ended—more strictly inhibiting that no one, of whatever condition or state he may exist, intermeddle at all with persons or goods of this kind or do, innovate, or attempt anything concerning them in prejudice of the ordinance or disposition to be made by us as premised; decreeing from now as invalid and void if it shall happen that anything to the contrary be attempted by anyone knowingly or ignorantly.","Porro nos personas et bona eadem nostrae ac apostolicae sedis ordinationi et dispositioni quam gratia divina favente ad dei honorem et exaltationem fidei christianae ac statum prosperum terrae sanctae facere intendimus antequam praesens sacrum terminetur concilium reservamus inhibentes districtius ne quis cuiuscumque conditionis vel status exsistat se de personis vel bonis huiusmodi aliquatenus intromittat vel circa ea in ordinationis sive dispositionis nostrae per nos ut praemittitur faciendae praeiudicium aliquod faciat innovet vel attentet decernentes exnunc irritum et inane si secus a quoquam scienter vel ignoranter contigerit attentari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p46","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Through this, however, we are unwilling to derogate from the processes made or to be made concerning the individual persons of the same Templars by the diocesan bishops and provincial councils, as was otherwise ordained by us.","Per hoc tamen processibus factis vel faciendis circa singulares personas ipsorum templariorum per dioecesanos episcopos et provincialia concilia prout per nos alias exstitit ordinatum nolumus derogari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p47","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ...","Nulli ergo ... Si quis ...."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p48","Council of Vienne A.d. 1311–1312","Given at Vienne, on the 11th of the Kalends of April [22 March], in the seventh year of our pontificate.","Datum Viennae xi kalendas aprilis pontificatus nostri anno septimo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p49","Ad Providam","For perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Ad perpetuam rei memoriam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p50","Ad Providam","It pertains to the provident circumspection of the vicar of Christ presiding in the watchtower of apostolic dignity to weigh the vicissitudes of times, to discuss the causes of emerging negotiations, and to attend to the qualities of persons, so that, directing the insight of necessary consideration to each thing and applying the hand of opportune operation, he may thus pluck out the thorns of vices from the field of the Lord as to amplify virtues; thus remove the thorns of the prevaricators as to plant more than he destroys by uprooting; and by transferring devout plantings into vacant places through the eradication of harmful thorns, he may provide greater joy concerning the provided and useful union and translation of such places than true justice, which has compassion for sorrow, has inflicted detriment upon the persons of such places through ruin. For thus, by tolerating what hinders and by subrogating what profits, he amplifies the progress of virtues and, the obstacle having been removed from the midst, restores by the subrogation of something better.","Ad providam Christi vicarii praesidentis in specula apostolicae dignitatis circumspectionem pertinet vices pensare temporum emergentium negotiorum causas discutere ac personarum attendere qualitates ut ad singula debitum dirigens necessariae considerationis intuitum et opportunae manum operationis apponens de agro domini sic vitiorum tribulos eruat ut virtutes amplificet sic praevaricantium spinas tollat ut evellendo plus plantet quam destruat et in loca vacua per eradicationem nocentium tribulorum devota deo plantaria transferendo potiorem praebeat de provisa et utili eorumdem locorum unione et translatione laetitiam quam vera iustitia quae compassionem habet doloris intulerit detrimentum personarum locorum huiusmodi per ruinam. Sic enim sufferendo quod officit et subrogando quod proficit virtutum profectus amplificat et sublata de medio meliori subrogatione restaurat."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p51","Ad Providam","Long ago indeed, the order of the military order of the Temple of Jerusalem—because of the master and brothers and other persons of the said order residing in any parts of the world, sprinkled with various and diverse—alas sorrowfully—not so much nefarious as unspeakable obscenities, depravities, stains, and blemishes of errors and crimes, which on account of their sad and filthy memory we suppress in the present [writings]—and its state, habit, and name, not without bitterness and sorrow of heart, with the sacred council approving, not by way of a definitive sentence (since we could not lawfully issue such a sentence concerning this according to the inquests and processes had concerning these things) but by way of provision or apostolic ordinance, we abolished by an irrevocable and perpetually valid sanction, subjecting it to perpetual prohibition, more strictly inhibiting that anyone presume henceforth to enter the said order or to receive or wear its habit or to bear himself as a Templar.","Dudum siquidem ordinem domus militiae templi ierosolimitani propter magistrum et fratres ceteras que personas dicti ordinis in quibuslibet mundi partibus consistentes variis et diversis non tam nefandis quam infandis proh dolor errorum et scelerum obscenitatibus pravitatibus maculis et labe respersos quae propter tristem et spurcidam eorum memoriam praesentibus subticemus eius que ordinis statum habitum atque nomen non sine cordis amaritudine et dolore sacro approbante concilio non per modum diffinitivae sententiae cum eam super hoc secundum inquisitiones et processus super hiis habitos non possemus ferre de iure sed per viam provisionis seu ordinationis apostolicae irrefragabili et perpetuo valitura substulimus sanctione ipsum prohibitioni perpetuae supponentes districtius inhibendo ne quis dictum ordinem de cetero intrare vel eius habitum suscipere vel portare aut pro templario gerere se praesumeret."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p52","Ad Providam","But if anyone should act against [this], let him incur the sentence of excommunication ipso facto.","Quod si quis contrafaceret excommunicationis incurreret sententiam ipso facto."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p53","Ad Providam","We also deemed all the goods of the aforementioned order to be reserved to the ordinance and disposition of the apostolic see by apostolic authority, more strictly inhibiting that no one, of whatever condition or state he might exist, intermeddle at all with persons or goods of this kind, or do, innovate, or also attempt anything concerning them in prejudice of the ordinance or apostolic disposition to be made by the same see as premised, decreeing from then as invalid and void if it should happen that anything to the contrary be attempted by anyone knowingly or ignorantly.","Universa etiam bona ordinis praelibati apostolicae sedis ordinationi et dispositioni auctoritate apostolica duximus reservanda inhibentes districtius ne quis cuiuscumque conditionis vel status existeret se de personis vel bonis huiusmodi aliquatenus intromitteret vel circa ea in praeiudicium ordinis seu dispositionis apostolicae per sedem eamdem ut praemittitur faciendae aliquid faceret innovaret vel etiam attentaret decernentes ex tunc irritum et inane si secus a quoquam scienter vel ignoranter contingeret attentari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p54","Ad Providam","And afterwards, lest the said goods—which long ago were given, bequeathed, granted, and acquired by the worshipers of Christ for the aid of the Holy Land and for the attack of the enemies of the Christian faith—lacking due governance, perish as vacant, or be converted into uses other than those for which they were deputed by the pious devotion of the faithful, or lest because of the slowness of such ordinance and disposition their destruction or dilapidation follow, we had arduous, lengthy, and various counsels and treatises with our brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church and also with patriarchs, archbishops, bishops, and prelates, and also with certain excellent and illustrious persons, and with the proctors of the remaining absent prelates and also of the chapters and convents of churches and monasteries constituted in the said council, so that by a preceding diligent deliberation of such counsels and treatises, the ordinance and disposition of the said goods for the honor of God, the increase of faith, the exaltation of the church, the aid of the said land, and also the salvation and quiet of the faithful might come forth as healthful and useful. After which, indeed, long premeditated, provided, and mature counsels persuading—with many just causes, our deliberation and counsel and that of the said fathers and also of the patriarchs, archbishops, bishops, and other prelates and the aforesaid excellent and illustrious persons then present in the said council—finally settled upon this, that the aforesaid goods be united in perpetuity to the order of the Hospital of Saint John of Jerusalem and to the Hospital itself and to our beloved sons, the master and brothers of the same Hospital, in the name of the Hospital and their order, who, as athletes of the Lord, strenuously exposing themselves to the perils of death for the defense of the faith, bear very burdensome and perilous expenses continually in the overseas parts.","Ac postmodum ne dicta bona quae dudum ad subsidium terrae sanctae et impugnationem inimicorum fidei christianae a Christi cultoribus data legata concessa et acquisita fuerunt debita gubernatione carentia tamquam vacantia deperirent vel converterentur in usus alios quam in illos ad quos fuerant pia devotione fidelium deputata vel propter tarditatem ordinationis et dispositionis huiusmodi eorum destructio vel dilapidatio sequeretur cum fratribus nostris sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalibus necnon patriarchis archiepiscopis episcopis et praelatis ac etiam cum nonnullis excellentibus et illustribus personis cum reliquorum quoque absentium praelatorum et etiam capitulorum et conventuum ecclesiarum et monasteriorum procuratoribus in dicto concilio constitutis habuimus ardua morosa et diversa consilia et tractatus ut per huiusmodi consiliorum et tractatuum deliberationem praehabitam diligentem dictorum bonorum ordinatio et dispositio ad honorem dei augmentum fidei exaltationem ecclesiae dictae terrae subsidium salutem quoque fidelium et quietem salubris et utilis proveniret post que utique longa praemeditata provisa et matura consilia suadentibus plurimis iustis causis nostra et dictorum patrum necnon patriarcharum archiepiscoporum episcoporum et aliorum praelatorum ac excellentium et illustrium personarum praedictorum in dicto concilio tunc praesentium deliberationes et consilia in hoc finaliter resederunt ut praedicta bona ordini hospitalis sancti Ioannis ierosolimitani et ipsi hospitali ac dilectis filiis magistro et fratribus hospitalis eiusdem nomine hospitalis et ordinis eorumdem qui tamquam athletae domini pro defensione fidei se periculis mortis iugiter exponentes onerosa nimis et periculosa dispendia continue perferunt in partibus transmarinis in perpetuum unirentur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p55","Ad Providam","We therefore, among other places of the world in which the regular observance is known to flourish, pursuing the said order of the Hospital and the Hospital itself with the fullness of sincere charity, and attending that—as the evidence of the fact teaches—in it divine services are fervently insisted upon, the works of piety and mercy are exercised with watchful studies, the brothers of the same Hospital, spurned the alluring temptations of the world, devote devoted service to the Most High, and, as intrepid champions of Christ, intending with fervent studies and desires for the recovery of the aforesaid land, hold any human perils in contempt; considering also that from this the strength of spirit of the same master and brothers of the said order and Hospital will grow, their fervor will be increased, and their fortitude will be strengthened for repelling the injuries of our Redeemer and for crushing the enemies of the same faith, as much as their power, augmented in more opulent resources, will be able more lightly and easily to support the burdens which the necessity of the prosecution of so great a negotiation requires; and therefore not undeservedly rendered watchful and excited with solicitous studies so that we may impart aid and work for their state—with the same sacred council approving, we give, grant, unite, incorporate, apply, and annex in perpetuity, from the plenitude of apostolic power, to the same order of the Hospital of Saint John of Jerusalem and to the Hospital itself, the very house of the military order of the Temple, and the other houses, churches, chapels, oratories, cities, castles, villages, lands, granges, and places, possessions, jurisdictions, revenues, and rights, and all other goods, immovable and movable or self-moving, with all their members, rights, and appurtenances, existing overseas and on this side of the sea, and in all and any parts of the world, which the same order and the said master and brothers of the order itself of the military order of the Temple at the time when the same master and some of the same brothers of the military order of the Temple were commonly captured in the kingdom of France, namely in the year of the Lord 1308 in the month of October, through themselves or through any others, had, held, and possessed, or pertained in any way to the same house and order of the military order of the Temple and to the said master and brothers of the order itself of the military order of the Temple; and also the names, actions, and rights which at the said time of their capture pertained or could pertain in any way to the same house, order, or persons of the order itself of the military order of the Temple against anyone, of whatever dignity, state, or condition they might exist, with all the privileges, indulgences, immunities, and liberties with which the aforesaid master and brothers of the said house and order of the military order of the Temple and the house and order itself were legitimately fortified by the apostolic see or by catholic emperors, kings, and princes and other faithful, or in any other way—except the goods of the said order itself of the military order of the Temple existing in the kingdoms and lands of our dearest sons in Christ, the illustrious kings of Castile, Aragon, Portugal, and Majorca, outside the kingdom of France, which we deemed to be specially excepted and also excluded from the aforesaid donation, concession, union, application, incorporation, and annexation, nonetheless reserving them to the disposition and ordinance of the apostolic see.","Nos igitur inter cetera mundi loca in quibus vigere dinoscitur observantia regularis dictum ordinem hospitalis et ipsum hospitale sincerae caritatis plenitudine prosequentes ac attendentes quod sicut evidentia facti docet in eo divinis obsequiis ferventer insistitur pietatis et misericordiae opera vigilantibus studiis exercentur fratres hospitalis ipsius mundanis spretis illecebris devotum impendentes altissimo famulatum ac pro recuperatione terrae praedictae tamquam intrepidi Christi pugiles ferventibus studiis et desideriis intendentes quaelibet ducunt humana pericula in contemptum considerantes quoque quod ex hoc tanto eorumdem magistri et fratrum dictorum ordinis et hospitalis crescet strenuitas animorum fervor augebitur et ipsorum roborabitur fortitudo ad propulsandas nostri redemptoris iniurias et hostes eiusdem fidei conterendos quanto ipsorum potentia in opulentioribus facultatibus augmentata onera quae prosecutionis tanti negotii necessitas exigit levius et facilius poterunt supportare et propterea non indigne vigiles redditi studiis que sollicitis excitati ut ad sui status opem et operam impendamus eodem sacro approbante concilio ipsam domum militiae templi ceteras que domos ecclesias capellas oratoria civitates castra villas terras grangias et loca possessiones iurisdictiones redditus atque iura omnia que alia bona immobilia et mobilia vel se moventia cum omnibus membris iuribus et pertinentiis suis ultra et citra mare ac in universis et quibuslibet mundi partibus consistentia quae ipse ordo et dicti magister et fratres ipsius ordinis militiae templi tempore quo ipse magister et nonnulli ex eisdem fratribus militiae templi in regno Franciae communiter capti fuerunt videlicet anno domini millesimo trecentesimo octavo mense octobris per se vel quoscunque alios habebant tenebant et possidebant vel ad eosdem domum et ordinem militiae templi et dictos magistrum et fratres ipsius ordinis militiae templi quomodolibet pertinebant necnon nomina actiones et iura quae praedicto tempore captionis ipsorum eisdem domui ordini vel personis ipsius ordinis militiae templi quocumque modo competebant vel competere poterant contra quoscumque cuiuscunque dignitatis status vel conditionis exsisterent cum omnibus privilegiis indulgentiis immunitatibus et libertatibus quibus praefati magister et fratres dictorum domus et ordinis militiae templi et ipsa domus et ordo per sedem apostolicam vel per catholicos imperatores reges et principes et fideles alios vel quocumque alio modo erant legitime communiti eidem ordini hospitalis sancti Ioannis ierosolimitani et ipsi hospitali donamus concedimus unimus incorporamus applicamus et annectimus in perpetuum de apostolicae plenitudine potestatis exceptis bonis quondam dicti ordinis ipsius militiae templi consistentibus in regnis et terris carissimorum in Christo filiorum nostrorum Castellae Aragoniae Portugaliae et Maioricarum regum illustrium extra regnum Franciae quae a donatione concessione unione applicatione incorporatione et annexione praedictis specialiter excipienda duximus et etiam excludenda ex nihilominus dispositioni et ordinationi sedis apostolicae reservantes"],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p56","Ad Providam","Wishing the inhibition recently made through our other processes—namely, that no one, of whatever condition or state he might exist, intermeddle at all with persons and goods of this kind, or do anything concerning them in prejudice of the ordinance or disposition to be made by the same see concerning them—and also the interposition of our decree concerning the persons and goods existing in the said kingdoms and lands of the same kings next expressed to remain wholly in full force of firmness, until concerning the aforesaid goods and things existing in the same kingdoms and lands it be otherwise ordained by the disposition of the same see.","inhibitionem dudum per alios processus nostros factam ne quis videlicet cuiuscumque conditionis vel status existeret se de personis et bonis huiusmodi aliquatenus intromitteret vel circa ea in praeiudicium ordinationis seu dispositionis sedis eiusdem faciendae de illis necnon decreti nostri interpositionem quoad personas et bona in dictis regnis et terris eorumdem regum proxime expressorum consistentia omnino manere volentes in pleno robore firmitatis quousque de bonis et rebus praedictis in eisdem regnis et terris consistentibus per dispositionem sedis eiusdem fuerit aliter ordinatum"],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p57","Ad Providam","Moreover, we decree that the occupiers of the said goods or illicit detainers, of whatever state, condition, excellence, or dignity they may exist—even if they shine with pontifical, imperial, or royal dignity—unless within the space of one month after they shall have been requested concerning this by the said master and brothers of the Hospital itself or by any of them or by their proctor or proctors, they shall have relinquished the said goods and fully and freely restored them to the order of the Hospital itself and to the same Hospital or to the master or priors or preceptors or brothers of the same Hospital constituted in any parts and provinces, and to each of them, or to their proctor or proctors in the name of the same order of the Hospital itself—even if the said priors, preceptors, and brothers of the Hospital itself and their proctors do not have a special mandate from the said master of the Hospital itself concerning this, provided that the aforesaid proctors have or show a special mandate concerning this from the said priors and preceptors or from each of them in the provinces and parts in which such priors and preceptors have been deputed—be subject to the sentences of excommunication.","occupatores quoque dictorum bonorum aut illicitos detentores cuiuscumque status conditionis excellentiae vel dignitatis exstiterint etiam si pontificali imperiali vel regali praefulgeant dignitate nisi infra unius mensis spatium postquam super hoc per dictos magistrum et fratres ipsius hospitalis vel ipsorum quemlibet aut procuratorem seu procuratores eorum fuerint requisiti dicta bona dimiserint illa que plene et libere restituerint ordini ipsius hospitalis et eidem hospitali aut magistro seu prioribus vel praeceptoribus aut fratribus hospitalis eiusdem in quibuscunque partibus et provinciis constitutis eorum que singulis vel procuratori seu procurationibus eorumdem eiusdem ordinis ipsius hospitalis nomine etiamsi dicti priores praeceptores et fratres ipsius hospitalis et procuratores ipsorum et eorum quilibet a dicto magistro ipsius hospitalis mandatum super hoc specialiter non haberent dummodo procuratores praedicti a dictis prioribus et praeceptoribus vel eorum singulis in provinciis et partibus in quibus huiusmodi priores et praeceptores exstiterint deputati mandatum super hoc habuerint vel ostenderint speciale. Qui omnes et singuli videlicet priores et praeceptores et fratres dicto magistro procuratores vero praedicti eisdem prioribus et praeceptoribus eorum que singulis a quibus super hiis fuerint deputati plenum super omnibus gestis actis receptis et procuratis per eos quomodolibet in hac parte computum et rationem ponere et reddere teneantur necnon omnes qui scienter occupatoribus et detentoribus praelibatis in occupatione vel detentione huiusmodi dederint consilium auxilium vel favorem publice vel occulte excommunicationis capitula vero collegia seu conventus ecclesiarum et monasteriorum necnon universitates civitatum castrorum villarum et aliorum locorum et ipsas civitates castra villas et loca quae in hiis culpabilia exstiterint ac etiam civitates castra et loca in quibus detentores et occupatores huiusmodi dominium obtinuerint temporale si huiusmodi domini temporales in dimittendo bona praedicta et restituendo illa magistro et fratribus ordinis et hospitalis eiusdem nomine hospitalis ipsius obstaculum adhibebunt et infra dictum mensem ab huiusmodi praemissis non destiterint postquam super hoc ut praemittitur fuerint requisiti ipso facto interdicti sententiis decernimus subiacere a quibus absolvi non possint donec super hiis plenam et debitam satisfactionem curaverint exhibere et nihilominus occupatores et detentores huiusmodi vel praestantes eisdem ut praemittitur auxilium consilium vel favorem sive singulares personae sive capitula collegia seu conventus ecclesiarum et monasteriorum ac universitates civitatum castrorum terrarum vel aliorum locorum exstiterint praeter poenas praescriptas omnibus quae a Romana vel aliis ecclesiis quibuscumque tenent in feudum ipso facto decernimus fore privatos sive privata ita quod ad ecclesias ad quas spectant illa libere sine contradictione aliqua revertantur earum que ecclesiarum praelati sive rectores de ipsis pro sua voluntate disponant sicut utilitati ecclesiarum ipsarum viderint expedire."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p58","Ad Providam","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ...","Nulli ergo ... Si quis ...."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p59","Ad Providam","Given at Vienne, on the 6th of the Nones of May [2 May], in the seventh year of our pontificate.","Dat(um) Viennae vi nonas maii pontificatus nostri anno septimo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p60","Ad Providam","Wherefore we command your discretion by apostolic writings that you, or two or one of you, through yourselves or through another or others, induce into corporal possession of the said house of the military order of the Temple and of the other houses, churches, chapels, oratories, cities, castles, villages, lands, granges, and places, possessions, jurisdictions, revenues, and rights, and all other goods, movable and immovable or self-moving, with all their members, rights, and appurtenances, existing overseas and on this side of the sea and in all and any parts of the world, which the same order and the said master and brothers of the order itself of the military order of the Temple at the said time of such their capture, through themselves or through any others, had and possessed or pertained in any way to the same house and order of the military order of the Temple and to the said master and brothers of the order itself of the military order of the Temple, the aforesaid master or priors or preceptors or brothers of the same Hospital or any of them, or their proctor or proctors in the name of the Hospital itself, by our authority, and to defend them when induced, the aforesaid occupiers and detainers and administrators and conservators deputed in the same goods by apostolic authority and by any other authority, and any others, having been removed; and cause full account to be made to them concerning their fruits, revenues, proceeds, rights, and all obventions.","Quocirca discretioni vestrae per apostolica scripta mandamus quatenus vos vel duo aut unus vestrum per vos vel per alium seu alios praefatos magistrum seu priores vel praeceptores aut fratres hospitalis eiusdem vel ipsorum quemlibet aut procuratorem seu procuratores eorum nomine hospitalis ipsius in corporalem possessionem dictorum domus militiae templi ceterarum que domorum ecclesiarum capellarum oratorium civitatum castrorum villarum terrarum grangiarum et locorum possessionum iurisdictionum reddituum atque iurium omnium aliorum bonorum mobilium et immobilium vel se moventium cum omnibus membris iuribus et pertinentiis suis ultra et citra mare ac in universis et quibuslibet mundi partibus consistentium quae ipse ordo et dicti magister et fratres ipsius ordinis militiae templi praedicto tempore huiusmodi captionis eorum per se vel quoscumque alios habebant et possidebant vel ad eosdem domum et ordinem militiae templi et dictos magistrum et fratres ipsius ordinis militiae templi quomodolibet pertinebant inducatis auctoritate nostra et defendatis inductos amotis praedictis occupatoribus et detentoribus ac administratoribus et conservatoribus in bonis ipsis auctoritate apostolica et quavis alia deputatis et quibuslibet aliis ab eisdem eis que faciatis de illorum fructibus redditibus proventibus iuribus et obventionibus universis integre responderi"],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p61","Ad Providam","You shall cause the occupiers or detainers, administrators, conservators, and the aforesaid others, unless within the said term as premised they shall have relinquished the aforesaid goods and fully and freely restored them to the order of the Hospital itself and to the same Hospital or to the master or priors or preceptors or brothers of the same Hospital constituted in the parts and provinces where such goods are located, and to each of them, or to their proctor or proctors in the name of the Hospital itself as is premised above, and those who have given aid, counsel, or favor to such occupiers or detainers and administrators and conservators—if single persons, to be subject to excommunication; if chapters, colleges, or convents or universities, and the cities, castles, villages, and places which have been culpable in these matters, and also the cities, castles, and places in which such detainers and occupiers have obtained temporal dominion, if such temporal lords shall have interposed an obstacle in relinquishing the aforesaid goods and restoring them to the master and brothers of the same Hospital in the name of the Hospital itself, and shall not have desisted from such premises within the said month after having been requested as premised—to lie under sentences of interdict to be pronounced by you; and nonetheless you shall denounce as deprived of all goods which they hold in fief from the Roman or any other churches, and cause them to be denounced by others, and cause the said excommunicated persons to be more strictly avoided until they shall have suitably satisfied concerning these things and shall have deserved to obtain the benefit of absolution; not obstructed if any have been granted indulgence by the aforesaid see that they cannot be interdicted, suspended, or excommunicated by apostolic letters not making full and express mention word for word, etc.; restraining also other contradictors, if any exist, by ecclesiastical censure, appeal having been postponed.","occupatores seu detentores administratores conservatores et alios praedictos nisi infra dictum terminum ut praemittitur bona et redditus praedicta dimiserint illa que libere et plene restituerint ordini ipsius hospitalis et eidem hospitali aut magistro seu prioribus vel praeceptoribus aut fratribus hospitalis eiusdem in partibus et provinciis ubi bona huiusmodi fuerint constitutis eorum que singulis vel procuratori seu procuratoribus eorumdem ipsius hospitalis nomine ut superius est praemissum ac praestantes occupatoribus seu detentoribus ac administratoribus et conservatoribus huiusmodi auxilium consilium vel favorem si singulares personae excommunicationis si vero capitula collegia seu conventus seu universitates exstiterint et ipsas civitates castra villas et loca quae in hiis culpabilia fuerunt ac etiam civitates castra et loca in quibus detentores et occupatores huiusmodi dominium obtinuerint temporale si huiusmodi domini temporales in dimittendo bona praedicta et restituendo illa magistro et fratribus hospitalis eiusdem ipsius hospitalis nomine obstaculum adhibebunt et infra dictum mensem ab huiusmodi praemissis non duxerint ut praedicitur desistendum tamdiu interdicti per vos latis sententiis subiacere et nihilominus omnibus bonis quae a Romana vel aliis ecclesiis quibuscumque tenent in feudum fore privatos seu privata per omnia loca in quibus expedire videritis denuntietis et faciatis ab aliis nuntiari dictos que excommunicatos arctius evitari donec super hiis congrue satisfecerint et absolutionis meruerint beneficium obtinere non obstante si aliquibus a praedicta sit sede indultum quod interdici suspendi vel excommunicari non possint per litteras apostolicas non facientes plenam et expressam ac de verbo et cetera contradictores etiam alios si qui fuerint per censuram ecclesiasticam appellatione postposita compescendo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p62","Ad Providam","We wish, however, and by apostolic authority we decree that from the date of the presents power and jurisdiction be attributed to you and to each of you in all and singular the premises, so that from now on you may be able to proceed so freely in them and in each of them as if the same jurisdiction had existed perpetuated by citation or another legitimate mode, and as if the aforesaid jurisdiction were considered perpetuated as if the matter were not whole; and that each one of you may freely prosecute an article begun by any of his colleagues, the contrary constitution of our predecessor Pope Boniface VIII of happy memory ceasing, even with the same colleague living and not impeded by any impediment, as often and whenever it shall be opportune.","Volumus autem et apostolica auctoritate decernimus quod a data praesentium sit vobis et cuilibet vestrum in praedictis omnibus et singulis potestas et iurisdictio attributa ut ex nunc in eis et eorum quolibet ita libere procedere valeatis ac si eadem iurisdictio per citationem vel modum alium perpetuata legittimum exstitisset et quasi re non integra perpetuata praefata iurisdictio censeatur quod que unusquisque vestrum articulum per collegarum suorum aliquem inchoatum felicis recordationis Bonifatii papae viii praedecessoris nostri contraria constitutione cessante prosequi libere valeat etiam eodem collega vivente nec impedimento aliquo praepedito quotiens et quando fuerit opportunum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p63","Ad Providam","Given at Vienne, on the 6th of the Nones of May [2 May], in the seventh year of our pontificate.","Datum Viennae vi nonas maii pontificatus nostri anno septimo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p64","Considerantes","Clement, bishop, servant of the servants of God, for the certainty of the presents and the remembrance of the future.","Clemens episcopus servus servorum dei ad certitudinem praesentium et memoriam futurorum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p65","Considerantes","Considering long ago the various inquests and processes had or made by mandate of the apostolic see through all the parts of Christendom against the former order of the military order of the Temple and against the individual persons of it, concerning the heresies of which they were gravely defamed, and especially concerning the fact that the brothers of the same former order, when they were received into that order and sometimes after their reception, were said to deny Christ and, to his opprobrium, to spit upon the cross shown to them and sometimes to trample it underfoot; and that the general master of that order, the visitor of France, and the greater preceptors of it, and also very many brothers of it, confessed in judgment concerning the aforesaid heresies; and that the same confessions rendered the said order very suspect; attending moreover to the widespread infamy, vehement suspicion, and also the clamorous insinuation of the prelates, dukes, communes, barons, and counts of the kingdom of France, and the grave scandal arising from the aforesaid against the aforementioned order which would not seem able to be calmed while the same order remains; and considering many other just reasons and causes which moved our mind to this, about which is contained in the process had concerning this—with grave bitterness and sorrow of heart, not by way of a definitive sentence (since we could not lawfully issue such a sentence concerning this according to the aforesaid inquests and processes) but by way of provision and apostolic ordinance, we abolished, removed, and annulled the aforesaid former order of the Temple and its state, habit, and name, and subjected it to perpetual prohibition, the sacred council approving, reserving the persons and goods of the same order to the ordinance and disposition of the apostolic see, wishing by this, however, not to derogate from the processes made or to be made concerning the individual persons or brothers of the same former order by the diocesan bishops and provincial councils, as was otherwise ordained by us.","Considerantes dudum inquisitiones et processus varios de mandato sedis apostolicae per universas partes christianitatis contra ordinem quondam militiae templi et ipsius singulas personas habitos sive factos super haeresibus de quibus ipsi erant graviter infamati et specialiter super eo quod fratres eiusdem quondam ordinis dum in ipso recipiebantur ordine ac interdum post receptionem eorum Christum negare et in eius opprobrium super crucem sibi ostensam spuere et eam interdum conculcare pedibus dicebantur quod que generalis magister ipsius ordinis visitator Franciae ac maiores ipsius ordinis praeceptores necnon et quamplures fratres eiusdem in iudicio confessi fuerunt de haeresibus supradictis quod que ipsae confessiones dictum ordinem valde suspectum reddebant attendentes insuper infamiam divulgatam suspicionem vehementem necnon praelatorum ducum communitatum baronum ac comitum regni Franciae insinuationem clamosam grave quoque scandalum ex praedictis contra ordinem praelibatum subortum quod non videbatur posse sedari eodem ordine remanente animadvertentes que multas alias iustas rationes et causas quae ad id nostrum moverunt animum de quibus in processu super hoc habito continetur cum gravi cordis amaritudine ac dolore non per modum diffinitivae sententiae cum eam super hoc secundum inquisitiones et processus praedictos non possemus ferre de iure sed per viam provisionis et ordinationis apostolicae praefatum quondam templi ordinem ac eius statum habitum atque nomen substulimus removimus et cassavimus ac perpetuae prohibitioni subiecimus sacro concilio approbante personas et bona eiusdem ordinis ordinationi et dispositioni sedis apostolicae reservantes per hoc tamen processibus factis vel faciendis circa singulares personas aut fratres eiusdem quondam ordinis per dioecesanos episcopos et provincialia concilia prout per nos alias ordinatum exstitit noluimus derogari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p66","Considerantes","Now therefore, wishing to provide more fully concerning the same individual persons or brothers as is expedient, we decreed that all the same brothers themselves, except the former master of the said order, the visitor of France and of the Holy Land, and the greater preceptors of Normandy and Aquitaine and Poitou and the province of Provence—whom we long ago specially reserved to our disposition—and brother Oliver of Penna, knight of the said former order, whom we from now on reserve to the disposition of the apostolic see, are to be left to the judgment and disposition of the provincial councils, just as we have also done up to now, wishing that according to the diversity of their conditions, it be proceeded with them by the same councils; namely, that to those who have already been sententially absolved from the aforesaid errors or in the future, justice demanding, shall be absolved, provision be made from the goods of the aforesaid former order whereby they may be able to be supported according to the decency of their state; concerning those indeed who have confessed to the aforesaid errors, their conditions having been considered and the mode of their confession having been weighed, we wish by the aforesaid councils, as shall seem to their circumspection, the rigor of justice to be mitigated with flowing mercy; concerning the impenitent and relapsed, if any—which God forbid—shall have been found among them, canonical justice or censure is to be observed; concerning those indeed who, even subjected to questioning, have denied themselves to be involved in the aforesaid errors, we wish what is just and what equity of the canons will persuade to be observed and done by the same councils.","Nunc igitur volentes circa singulares easdem personas aut fratres plenius sicut expedit providere fratres ipsos omnes praeter magistrum quondam dicti ordinis visitatorem Franciae et terrae sanctae Normanniae et Aquitaniae ac Pictaviae et provinciae Provinciae magnos praeceptores quos dudum dispositioni nostrae specialiter reservavimus et fratrem Oliverium de Penna dicti quondam ordinis militem quem ex nunc dispositioni sedis apostolicae reservemus iudicio et dispositioni conciliorum provincialium sicut et hactenus fecimus duximus relinquendos volentes iuxta diversitatem conditionum ipsorum per eadem concilia cum eis procedi videlicet quod illis qui sunt iam supradictis erroribus sententialiter absoluti vel in posterum exigente iustitia absolventur de bonis praefati quondam ordinis unde iuxta status sui decentiam substentari valeant ministretur circa eos autem qui de praefatis erroribus sunt confessi consideratis eorum conditionibus modo que confessionis eorum pensato volumus a praefatis conciliis prout eorum circumspectioni videbitur rigorem iustitiae cum affluenti misericordia mitigari circa impenitentes et relapsos si qui quod deus avertat inventi fuerint inter eos iustitia aut censura canonica observanda quoad illos vero qui etiam suppositi quaestionibus se praedictis esse involutos erroribus negaverint per eadem concilia servari et fieri volumus quod iustum fuerit et aequitas canonum suadebit."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p67","Considerantes","We also, by the approval of the sacred council, by the tenor of the presents, cite those with whom inquiry has not yet been made concerning the said errors and who are not held under the hand or power of the church but are perhaps fugitives, that from the present day within one year—which we assign to them for this as a precise and peremptory term—they take care to appear personally before their diocesans, about to undergo their examination as justice will persuade, and according to the judgment of the aforesaid councils about to receive according to their merits—great mercy nonetheless having been applied and preserved both concerning them and concerning the others expressed above, except the relapsed and impenitent; and with this always provided, that from the goods of the said former order provision be made in necessary things for both these and those and also for all brothers of the same former order, whenever they shall have come to the obedience of the church and as long as they shall persist in the same obedience, according to the conditions and decency of their state—all of them to be placed in the houses of the aforesaid former order or in monasteries of other religious persons, at the expenses however of the former order, according to the arbitration of the said councils, yet in such a way that many be by no means placed together in one house or monastery.","Eos autem cum quibus adhuc non est super dictis erroribus inquisitum et qui sub manu vel potestate ecclesiae non habentur sed sunt forsitan fugitivi sacri approbatione concilii praesentium tenore citamus ut a die praesenti infra annum quem ad hoc eis pro termino praeciso et peremptorio assignamus coram dioecesanis suis curent personaliter comparere subituri eorum examen prout iustitia suadebit ac secundum praedictorum conciliorum iudicium pro meritis recepturi magna tamen tam circa eos quam circa alios supra expressos praeterquam contra relapsos et impenitentes misericordia adhibita et servata et eo semper proviso quod de bonis dicti quondam ordinis provideatur in necessariis tam istis quam illis et etiam omnibus eiusdem quondam ordinis fratribus quandocumque ad ecclesiae oboedientiam venerint et quamdiu in oboedientia eadem persisterint iuxta status sui conditiones et decentiam eorumdem ipsis omnibus in domibus praefati quondam ordinis aut in religiosorum aliorum monasteriis ad expensas tamen ipsius quondam ordinis iuxta dictorum conciliorum arbitrium collocandis ita tamen quod in una domo uno ve monasterio nullatenus multi simul ponantur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p68","Considerantes","We also command and strictly enjoin upon all with whom and through whom the brothers of the said former order are detained that they freely restore and release them whenever they shall have been requested concerning this by the metropolitans and ordinaries of the same brothers.","Mandamus etiam et districte praecipimus omnibus apud quos et per quos fratres dicti quondam ordinis detinentur ut eos libere restituant et dimittant quandocumque per metropolitanos et ordinarios fratrum ipsorum fuerint super hoc requisiti."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p69","Considerantes","But if, having been cited in the aforesaid manner, they shall not have taken care to appear before their aforesaid diocesans within the aforesaid year as premised, let them incur the sentence of excommunication ipso facto; and because in a cause, especially of faith, contumacy adds vehement presumption to suspicion, if they, thus contumacious, shall have sustained the aforesaid excommunication with a pertinacious spirit for one year, from then on let them be condemned as heretics.","Quod si infra praefatum annum coram dioecesanis praedictis praemisso modo citati non curaverint ut praemittitur comparere eo ipso sententiam excommunicationis incurrant et quia in causa praesertim fidei contumacia suspicioni praesumptionem adicit vehementem si sic contumaces excommunicationem praedictam per annum animo sustinuerint pertinaci ex tunc velut haeretici condemnentur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p70","Considerantes","But we make this edict of our citation from certain knowledge, and we wish the aforesaid brothers to be bound by it just as if they had been apprehended by special personal citations, because, as vagabonds, they could not be found at all or at least not easily; so that all occasion of calumny may be removed against the process of the same citation, we publish it in the present sacred council.","Verum huiusmodi nostrae citationis edictum quod fit ideo ex certa scientia facimus et eo fratres praedictos arctari volumus ac si essent per speciales citationes personaliter apprehensi quia ut vagabundi nullatenus possent aut saltem non faciliter inveniri ut contra citationis eiusdem processum omnis calumniae tollatur occasio in praesenti sacro concilio publicamus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p71","Considerantes","And so that such citation may be more certainly brought to the notice of the same brothers and of all in common, we shall cause charters or membranes containing the process of such citation, sealed with our bull, to be affixed or attached to the doors of the greater church of Vienne, which citation, as if with a sonorous herald and open judgment, they will publish, so that the aforesaid brothers, whom the citation itself touches, may be able to pretend no excuse that such citation did not reach them or that they were ignorant of it, since it is not likely that it remain unknown or hidden among them which is so openly published to all.","Et ut ipsa talis citatio certius ad fratrum ipsorum et communem omnium notitiam deducatur cartas sive membranas processum citationis huiusmodi continentes bulla que nostra bullatas in maioris ecclesiae Viennensis appendi vel affigi ostiis faciemus quae citationem huiusmodi suo quasi sonoro praeconio et patulo iudicio publicabunt ita quod fratres praedicti quos citatio ipsa contingit nullam possint excusationem praetendere quod ad eos ipsa citatio non pervenerit vel quod ignorarint eamdem cum non sit verisimile remanere apud eos incognitum vel occultum quod tam patenter omnibus publicatur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p72","Considerantes","Moreover, so that fuller caution may be observed concerning this, we command the diocesans of the places that in their cathedral churches and in the churches of notable places of their dioceses they cause the edict of such our citation to be published as soon as they can conveniently.","Ceterum ut circa hoc cautela plenior observetur dioecesanis locorum praecipimus ut in suis cathedralibus ac locorum insignium dioecesium suarum ecclesiis huiusmodi nostrae citationis edictum cum primum commode poterunt faciant publicari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p73","Considerantes","Given at Vienne, on the 2nd of the Nones of May [6 May], in the seventh year.","Dat(um) Viennae Ii nonas maii anno septimo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p74","Nuper in Generali Concilio","To all administrators and curators deputed by apostolic and any other authority to administer and conserve the goods of the former house and order of the military order of the Temple, existing anywhere.","Universis administratoribus et curatoribus ad administrandum et conservandum bona quondam domus et ordinis militiae templi apostolica et quacumque auctoritate alia deputatis ubicumque consistentia."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p75","Nuper in Generali Concilio","Recently in the general council celebrated by us at Vienne, the Lord disposing, after long and mature deliberation held beforehand, believing it to be more acceptable to the Most High, more honorable to the worshipers of orthodox faith, and judging it more useful for the aid of the Holy Land to concede the goods of the former house and order of the military order of the Temple to the order of the Hospital of Saint John of Jerusalem than to unite them to a newly created order or also to apply them, the counsel of our deliberation provided [for this]. But because then some, asserting that it would be more useful to confer the same goods to a newly created order than to apply them to the said order of the Hospital, our affection was unable to obtain the hoped-for effect concerning this.","Nuper in generali concilio per nos Viennae disponente domino celebrato post longam deliberationem praehabitam et maturam acceptabilius fore credentes altissimo magis honorabile fidei orthodoxae cultoribus ac subventioni terrae sanctae utilius exstimantes bona quondam domus et ordinis militiae templi ordini hospitalis sancti Ioannis ierosolimitani concedere quam ordini de novo creando unire aut etiam applicare consilium deliberationis nostrae providit sed quia tunc aliquibus asserentibus utilius fore bona ipsa ordini noviter creando conferre quam dicti hospitalis ordini applicare tunc noster affectus speratum effectum super hoc obtinere nequivit."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p76","Nuper in Generali Concilio","Finally, however, it is known to have been brought about by the grace of God that on the 6th of the Nones of the present month of May [2 May], with the same sacred council approving, we deemed that the aforesaid goods be conceded and applied and also united to the aforesaid Hospital or to its order—the goods of the same order of the military order of the Temple existing in the kingdoms and lands of our dearest sons in Christ, the illustrious kings of Castile, Aragon, Portugal, and Majorca, outside the kingdom of France, alone excepted, which we deemed to be excepted and also excluded from such union, concession, and application for certain causes, specially reserving them to our disposition and that of the apostolic see, until concerning them it be otherwise ordained for the aid of the said land by the providence of us and the said see.","Tandem vero sic per dei gratiam actum fore dinoscitur quod vi nonas praesentis mensis maii eodem sacro approbante concilio hospitali seu ipsius hospitalis ordini supradictis praefata bona concedenda et applicanda duximus et etiam unienda bonis eiusdem ordinis militiae templi in regnis et terris carissimorum in Christo filiorum nostrorum ... Castellae ... Aragonum ... Portugaliae et Maioricarum regum illustrium extra regnum Franciae consistentibus dumtaxat exceptis quae ab unione concessione et applicatione huiusmodi ex certis causis excipienda duximus et etiam excludenda ipsa dispositioni nostrae et sedis apostolicae specialiter reservantes quousque de illis aliter pro dictae terrae subsidio per nostram et dictae sedis providentiam exstiterit ordinatum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p77","Nuper in Generali Concilio","Wherefore we strictly command your university by apostolic writings that you take care integrally to restore and assign the aforesaid goods, with the revenues received therefrom, necessary expenses having been deducted, to the same master and brothers or to each of them, to the said priors or preceptors of the provinces or cities or dioceses or places of the said Hospital in which the same goods are located, or to their proctor or proctors, or to any of them, according as they were entrusted to the care and administration of each of you, when you shall have been requested concerning this by them or any of them, within one month from the time of such request, in the name of the said Hospital and order, so that the master and brothers and the aforesaid priors and preceptors or proctor or proctors may be able worthily to commend themselves concerning you in this matter, and we may deservedly acknowledge in you the promptness of devoted obedience.","Quocirca universitati vestrae per apostolica scripta districte mandamus quatenus bona praedicta cum redditibus inde perceptis deductis deducendis expensis eisdem magistro et fratribus aut singula eorum singulis dicti hospitalis prioribus seu praeceptoribus provinciarum aut civitatum vel dioecesium seu locorum in quibus bona ipsa consistunt seu procuratori vel procuratoribus eorumdem sive ipsorum alicuius prout cuiuslibet vestrum curae et administrationi commissa fuerunt cum ab eis vel eorum aliquo super hoc fueritis requisiti infra unum mensem a tempore requisitionis huiusmodi nomine dictorum hospitalis et ordinis integraliter restituere et assignare curetis ita quod magister et fratres ac priores et praeceptores seu procurator vel procuratores praedicti de vobis in hac parte digne se valeant commendare nos que in vobis devotae oboedientiae promptitudinem merito cognoscamus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p78","Nuper in Generali Concilio","Given at Liverone, in the diocese of Valence, on the 17th of the Kalends of June [16 May], in the seventh year.","Dat(um) Liverone Valentin(ensis) dioecesis xvii kal(endas) iunii anno septimo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p79","Redemptor Noster","Our Redeemer, the only-begotten Son of God, the Lord Jesus Christ, so loved the daughter of Zion—namely the Holy Land—that He pre-elected it for Himself as an inheritance and a proper patrimony. This, therefore, He Himself, clothed with the garment of our flesh, adorned with His presence and consecrated with the sprinkling of His most precious blood.","Redemptor noster unigenitus dei filius dominus Iesus Christus in tantum Syon filiam videlicet terram sanctam dilexit quod eam sibi in hereditatem et proprium patrimonium praeelegit hanc itaque ipse nostrae Carnis indumento vestitus sua decoravit praesentia sui que pretiosissimi aspersione sanguinis consecravit."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p80","Redemptor Noster","But it is to be mourned—alas sorrowfully—and to be lamented bitterly that so noble an inheritance of our Redeemer has been turned over to strangers and, confounded by the fury of the Babylonian persecutor, lies trodden under the feet of defilers, and is miserably held occupied, polluted by the most filthy handling of the Saracens, perfidious enemies of the Christian name; and the Christian people having been savagely slaughtered therein, to the greater contumely of the Creator and the injury and sorrow of all Christendom, the name of Christ is horribly blasphemed there by the abominable filth of those enemies.","Sed lugendum est proh dolor et amare dolendum quod tam praeclara nostri redemptoris hereditas versa est ad alienos et Babilonici persecutoris furore confusa iacet coinquinatorum pedibus conculcata et per immundissimos Sarracenos hostes perfidos nominis christiani vilissima ipsorum contrectatione polluta occupata miserabiliter detinetur et trucidato in ea immaniter populo christiano ad maiorem contumeliam creatoris totius que christianitatis iniuriam et dolorem ab hostium ipsorum abhominanda spurcitia blasfematur ibidem horribiliter nomen Christi."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p81","Redemptor Noster","Therefore that region itself, afflicted with dire scourges, sad, laments and, multiplying laments concerning the intolerable persecution which it suffers, cries out to the vicar of Christ, calls upon Christian princes and the catholic people, wounded with reproaches, and also reveals its wounds to them from whom it expects the work of a healer; it seeks their avenging right hand for whose salvation the Author of salvation bore the passion of the cross in it.","Lamentatur itaque diris afflicta flagellis regio ipsa tristis et de intolerabili persecutione quam patitur lamenta multiplicans clamat ad Christi vicarium christianos principes et plebem catholicam interpellat opprobriis sauciata eis quoque sua vulnera detegit a quibus exspectat operam medicantis illorum dexteram vindicem expetit pro quorum salute salutis auctor in ea crucis pertulit passionem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p82","Redemptor Noster","Therefore, these and other things concerning these matters which the mind is unable fully to conceive nor the tongue to relate rising up to the heart and exciting our mind—we, who were first called to the apex of apostolic dignity, although unworthily, by divine condescension, beholding the mournful state of the said land with the eye of pious consideration, have striven to think of remedies by which, with the aid of heavenly help, that land itself, having been liberated from the hand of the wicked enemy, might be able to have serene times of desired quiet for itself after such darkness of tribulations.","Hiis igitur et aliis circa haec quae animus nequit plene concipere nec lingua referre ad cor ascendentibus et excitantibus mentem nostram quam primo ad apostolicae dignitatis apicem fuimus licet indigni divina dignatione vocati statum lugubrem dictae terrae piae considerationis oculo intuentes studuimus cogitare remedia quibus ope comite caelestis auxilii terra ipsa de scelestis hostis manibus liberata sibi post tantarum tribulationum tenebras possent serena quietis optatae tempora provenire."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p83","Redemptor Noster","To which [end] and for other pious works acceptable to God, to be advanced with the aid of His omnipotence, we caused a general council to be convoked at the city of Vienne.","Ad quod et alia pia et deo accepta opera ope suae omnipotentiae promovenda fecimus apud civitatem Viennensem generale concilium convocari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p84","Redemptor Noster","Afterwards, indeed, with our brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church, and also patriarchs, archbishops, bishops, prelates, and also our dearest sons in Christ, Philip of France and Louis of Navarre, illustrious [kings], who exhibited their presence in the aforesaid council, and with certain other excellent men and the proctors of the remaining absent prelates and also of the chapters, convents of churches and monasteries gathered in the same council, we had a long, mature, and diligent treatise concerning aiding the business of the said land, and finally with the approval of the same council we deliberated that aid be given to the same land through a general passage.","Postmodum vero cum fratribus nostris sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinalibus patriarchis quoque archiepiscopis et episcopis praelatis necnon carissimis in Christo filiis nostris Philippo Francorum et Ludovico Navarrae illustribus qui suam exhibuerunt in concilio memorato praesentiam et nonnullis aliis excellentibus viris ac reliquorum absentium praelatorum et etiam capitulorum conventuum ecclesiarum et monasteriorum procuratoribus in ipso concilio congregatis longum maturum et diligentem tractatum habuimus super iuvando negotio dictae terrae et tandem de ipsius approbatione concilii deliberavimus ut eidem terrae per subsidium generalis passagii succurratur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p85","Redemptor Noster","To which, intending to apply the hand of apostolic power with solicitous studies, all the premises having been weighed with worthy consideration, we deemed that a tenth of all ecclesiastical rents and proceeds through all the parts of the world—the persons and places of the Hospital of Saint John of Jerusalem and of other military orders alone excepted—be imposed for up to six years, to be counted from the next preceding Kalends of January [January 1], by apostolic authority, with the same sacred council approving, for this purpose, to be collected and paid at certain terms as would seem expedient to us, and to be converted into the aid of the said land and otherwise against the enemies and infidels of the catholic faith.","Ad quod nos manum apostolicae potestatis apponere sollicitis studiis intendentes praemissis omnibus digna consideratione pensatis decimam omnium ecclesiasticorum reddituum et proventuum per universas mundi partes personis et locis hospitalis sancti Ioannis ierosolimitani et aliorum militarium ordinum dumtaxat exceptis usque ad sex annos a kalendis ianuarii proximo praeteritis computandos auctoritate apostolica eodem sacro approbante concilio duximus propterea imponendam per certos sicut nobis videretur expediens colligendam terminos et solvendam ac in dictae terrae subsidium et alias contra inimicos et infideles catholicae fidei convertendam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p86","Redemptor Noster","But we, recently considering more attentively that our letters concerning such imposition, collection, and payment of the tenth had not reached you before the aforesaid Kalends, nor could they conveniently reach you in a short time on account of the long distance of those parts from the Roman curia, and therefore wishing to provide benignly for your revelations and conveniences in this matter, we decreed that the aforesaid six years in those your parts begin on the coming Kalends of October [October 1].","Verum nos nuper considerantes attentius quod litterae nostrae super huiusmodi impositione collectione et solutione decimae ad vos infra kalendas non pervenerant supradictas nec etiam in brevi propter longam a Romana curia partium illarum distantiam commode pervenire valebant et propterea revelationibus et commoditatibus vestris in hac parte benigne prospicere cupientes praedictos sex annos in eisdem partibus vestris in kalendis octobris futuri decrevimus inchoandos."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p87","Redemptor Noster","Wherefore we ask, admonish, and more attentively exhort your university, and nonetheless by apostolic writings more strictly enjoin upon you in virtue of obedience, that you and each of you, for the same six years to begin from the said Kalends of October as premised, according to customary practice, at the terms written below—namely, for the half of the first year of the said six years on the same Kalends of October, and for the remaining half of that year on the following Kalends of April [April 1], and thus for each of the remaining five years at similar terms—pay the aforesaid tenth from your ecclesiastical rents and proceeds without any diminution, without the obstacle of difficulty.","Quare universitatem vestram rogamus monemus et hortamur attentius vobis nihilominus per apostolica scripta in virtute oboedientiae districtius iniungentes quatenus praefatam decimam per eosdem sex annos a dictis kalendis octobris ut praedicitur inchoandos iuxta morem solitum per infrascriptos terminos videlicet pro medietate primi anni dictorum sex annorum in eisdem kalendis octobris et pro reliqua medietate ipsius anni in kalendis aprilis ex tunc proxime secuturi et sic per singulos reliquos quinque annos per similes terminos absque difficultatis obstaculo vos et quilibet vestrum de redditibus et proventibus vestris ecclesiasticis sine diminutione qualibet persolvatis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p88","Redemptor Noster","But if perhaps you shall have failed in the same terms in payment of the aforesaid tenth, we wish you and each of you to incur the same sentences ipso facto which it shall happen to be pronounced by you or by another or by other suitable and faithful persons to be deputed or to be deputed by you for collecting the same tenth in your cities and dioceses, upon those not paying it within the aforesaid terms.","Quod si forsan in eisdem terminis defeceritis in solutione decimae supradictae vos et quemlibet vestrum similes sententias quas per vos vel alium seu alias personas idoneas et fideles deputandam seu deputandas a vobis ad eandem decimam in vestris civitatibus et dioecesibus colligendam in non solventes illam in terminis supradictis proferri contigerit incurrere volumus ipso facto."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p89","Redemptor Noster","And nonetheless you, that is each of you in your same cities and dioceses, by yourselves or by such other or other suitable and faithful persons whom you shall have deemed to assume for the ministry of such collection in each of your same cities and dioceses—which we altogether wish and command to be deputed by you for this, and to whom by the tenor of the presents we commit and command—from our beloved sons the abbots, priors, deans, provosts, archdeacons, archpriests, and other prelates of churches, chapters, colleges, and convents of the Cistercian, Cluniac, Premonstratensian, Benedictine and Augustinian, Carthusian, Grandmontine, and of other orders, and other ecclesiastical persons, secular and regular, non-exempt, constituted through your cities and dioceses—except only the same priors, preceptors, masters, persons, and places of the Hospital of Saint John of Jerusalem and of the other military orders—by our authority integrally exact and collect the aforesaid tenth according to the aforesaid custom for the years and terms mentioned, from their ecclesiastical rents and proceeds; and the same assumed persons shall faithfully and integrally hand over and assign the tenth which it shall happen to be exacted and collected from them, both exempt and non-exempt, as premised, to each of you by whom they shall have been thus assumed, within the same terms or promptly for them, as soon as they conveniently can, all delay and dilation having been postponed; and you shall compel them by ecclesiastical censure, appeal ceasing, to render to you an account of the things exacted and collected from the aforesaid non-exempt, and to make such handing over and assignment of the tenth exacted and collected from the same exempt and non-exempt as premised, public instruments having been drawn up concerning such handing over and assignment, and other due cautions having been applied, by which, when necessary, it may be able to be established how much and from whom and when and for what time the aforesaid assumed persons have received the tenth itself, and how much of it, when, and for what time they have handed over and assigned to each of you; take care.","Et vos nihilominus a dilectis filiis abbatibus prioribus decanis archidiaconis praepositis archipresbyteris et aliis ecclesiarum praelatis capitulis collegiis et conventibus Cisterciensibus Cluniacensibus praemonstratensibus sanctorum Benedicti et Augustini Cartusiensibus Grandimontensibus et aliorum ordinum ceteris que personis ecclesiasticis saecularibus et regularibus non exemptis per vestras civitates et dioeceses constitutis videlicet singuli vestrum in singulis eisdem civitatibus et dioecesibus prioribus et praeceptoribus magistris et aliis personis ac locis hospitalis sancti Ioannis ierosolimitani et aliorum militarium ordinum eorundem dumtaxat exceptis per vos vel huiusmodi aliam seu alias personas idoneas et fideles quam vel quas ad huiusmodi collectionis ministerium in singulis eisdem vestris civitatibus et dioecesibus assumendam duxeritis vel etiam assumendas ipse que et earum singulae quas omnino per vos ad hoc volumus et praecipimus deputari quibus ve id tenore praesentium committimus et mandamus in singulis praedictis civitatibus et dioecesibus ubi taliter assumptae fuerint a dilectis filiis abbatibus prioribus decanis praepositis archidiaconis archipresbyteris et aliis ecclesiarum praelatis capitulis collegiis et conventibus eorundem ordinum exemptis dictarum vestrarum civitatum et dioecesium praeterquam ab eisdem prioribus praeceptoribus magistris personis et locis hospitalis sancti Ioannis et aliorum militarium ordinum praedictorum huiusmodi decimam iuxta praedictum morem per annos et terminos memoratos de redditibus et proventibus suis ecclesiasticis auctoritate nostra integraliter exigere et colligere dictae que etiam assumptae personae decimam quam ut praedicitur ab ipsis tam exemptis quam non exemptis exigi et colligi contigerit per easdem singulis videlicet vestrum a quibus taliter assumptae fuerint per eosdem terminos seu pro eis quam cito commode poterunt omni mora et dilatione postpositis fideliter et integre tradere et assignare quas ad reddendum vobis computum de exactis et collectis a praedictis non exemptis ac traditionem et assignationem huiusmodi decimae per eas ab eisdem exemptis et non exemptis exactae et collectae ut praemittitur faciendam per censuram ecclesiasticam appellatione cessante cogatis de ipsarum traditione et assignatione huiusmodi confectis publicis instrumentis aliis que debitis cautelis adhibitis per quae cum opus fuerit constare valeat quantum et a quibus quando et pro quo tempore prefatae assumptae personae de decima ipsa receperint quantum que de illa quando ac pro quo tempore singulis vestrum tradiderint et assignaverint studeatis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p90","Redemptor Noster","Which tenth, both when it shall have been exacted and collected by you and by the same persons from others exempt and non-exempt as premised, and when it shall have been handed over and assigned to you—that which also from your said rents and proceeds you shall pay, each of you, together with the chapter of his cathedral church, shall cause to be diligently and faithfully kept stored within that church itself or also elsewhere as they shall have known more expedient, in some more decent and safer place, at the expenses of themselves and of the same chapter; to be assigned by the same each of you to him or them whom or which we shall have deemed to be deputed for this, as and when it shall seem to us, for the business of such land and of the aforesaid faith.","Quam quidem decimam tam per vos et easdem personas ab aliis exemptis et non exemptis ut praemittitur exigendam et colligendam cum exacta et collecta exstiterit vobis que illa etiam quam per dictas personas a praefatis exemptis exigi et colligi contigerit tradita et assignata fuerit ut superius est expressum quam illa quam de dictis vestris redditibus et proventibus persolvetis singuli vestrum una cum capitulo suae cathedralis ecclesiae infra ecclesiam ipsam aut etiam alibi prout magis expedire cognoverint in aliquo decentiori et tuitiori loco repositam suis et ipsius capituli expensis faciant diligenter et fideliter custodiri per eosdem singulos vestrum illi vel illis quem vel quos ad id deputandum seu deputandos duxerimus prout et quando nobis videbitur pro huiusmodi terrae ac fidei praedictarum negotiis assignandam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p91","Redemptor Noster","So that a due effect may more easily and efficaciously follow upon the exaction and collection of such tenth, we grant to you and to each of you full and free power by the tenor of the presents to restrain abbots, priors, deans, provosts, and the aforesaid others non-exempt in your same cities and dioceses, by yourselves or by the aforesaid persons to be assumed by you as premised, and to the same persons [to restrain] the aforesaid exempt abbots, priors, deans, provosts, and other prelates in each of the same cities and dioceses in which they shall have been deputed, and also any other contradictors and rebels, by ecclesiastical censure, appeal having been postponed; and moreover to impart the benefit of absolution according to the form of the church to you and to each of you in your same cities and dioceses, to those among the aforesaid non-exempt, and to the aforesaid persons to be assumed and to each of them, to those among the aforesaid exempt who shall have been bound by sentences of excommunication, suspension, or interdict on occasion of the aforesaid tenth not paid at the due time, after satisfaction has been exhibited concerning it, and to dispense with those who, having been bound by the said sentences or any of them by celebrating divine [offices] or mixing with them as before, shall have contracted a stain of irregularity.","Ut igitur facilius et efficacius super exactione et collectione huiusmodi decimae debitus subsequatur effectus vobis et singulis vestrum abbates priores decanos praepositos et alios supradictos non exemptos in eisdem vestris civitatibus et dioecesibus per vos vel praedictas personas per vos ut praemittitur assumendas eisdem vero personis abbates priores decanos praepositos et alios praelibatos exemptos in singulis eisdem civitatibus et dioecesibus in quibus deputatae fuerint necnon quoscunque alios contradictores et rebelles per censuram ecclesiasticam appellatione postposita compescendi et insuper vobis et singulis vestrum in eisdem vestris civitatibus et dioecesibus illis ex non exemptis praedictis ac praefatis assumendis personis et earum cuilibet illis ex praedictis exemptis qui occasione praedictae decimae tempore debito non solutae excommunicationum suspensionum vel interdicti fuerint ligati sententiis post satisfactionem de illa exhibitam iuxta formam ecclesiae absolutionis beneficium impendendi et dispensandi cum eis qui dictis ligati sententiis vel earum aliqua celebrando divina vel immiscendo se illis sicut prius maculam irregularitatis contraxerint plenam et liberam concedimus tenore praesentium potestatem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p92","Redemptor Noster","And so that both you and the said persons to be deputed for collecting the said tenth by you as premised may obtain fruit from your labors, we enjoin the premises upon you and them for the remission of sins.","Et ut tam vos quam dictae personae ad dictam colligendam decimam per vos ut praemittitur deputandae fructum de vestris laboribus reportetis praemissa vobis et eis in remissionem iniungimus peccatorum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p93","Redemptor Noster","Notwithstanding if it has been granted to you or to any of you or to the abbots, priors, and other aforesaid exempt and non-exempt or to any others by the same see that you are held to the payment of any least tenth and to be compelled to it, or that you cannot be interdicted, suspended, or excommunicated, by apostolic letters which do not make full and express mention of such indulgence and its whole tenor word for word and of the proper names of your orders, places, and persons; or any privileges, indulgences, exemptions, and apostolic letters whatsoever granted generally or specially to dignities, orders, places, or persons under any form or conception of words by the aforesaid see, of which, whose whole tenors word for word special, full, and express mention must be had in our letters.","Non obstantibus si vobis vel vestrum aliquibus aut abbatibus prioribus et aliis supradictis exemptis et non exemptis vel quibuscunque aliis ab eadem sit sede indultum quod ad solutionem alicuius decimae minimae teneamini et ad id compelli aut quod interdici suspendi vel excommunicari non possitis per litteras apostolicas quae de indulto huiusmodi et toto eius tenore de verbo ad verbum ac de propriis ordinum locorum et personarum vestrarum nominibus plenam et expressam non fecerint mentionem seu quibuslibet privilegiis indulgentiis exemptionibus et litteris apostolicis quibuscunque dignitatibus ordinibus locis vel personis generaliter vel specialiter sub quacunque forma vel conceptione verborum a sede memorata concessis de quibus quorum que totis tenoribus de verbo ad verbum in nostris litteris specialis plena et expressa mentio sit habenda."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p94","Redemptor Noster","Moreover, considering that in the premises you are pursuing the business of God and acting in the sight of Him who discerns all things, and that you will be held to render an account to Him and also to us who intend to apply all diligence concerning this, about to receive according to your merits—therefore take care so to conduct yourselves prudently and solicitously in them, and so diligently and circumspectly to have yourselves, that you not only avoid the dangers of penalty and confusion, but also may be able to obtain the glory of praise and the reward of condign recompense.","Praeterea considerantes quod in praemissis negotium dei persequimini et in conspectu agitis eius qui cuncta discernit sibi que necnon et nobis qui circa hoc omnem diligentiam intendimus adhibere tenebimini reddere rationem ab utroque pro meritis recepturi sic prudenter in illis et sollicite gerere sic que diligenter et consulte vos habere curetis quod non solum vitetis penae confusionis que discrimina sed laudis gloriam ac retributionis condignae mercedem consequi valeatis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p95","Redemptor Noster","We wish moreover that each of you cause the same persons deputed by him for collecting the said tenth to swear diligently and solicitously to prosecute the premises in this form: \"I swear, I, . . . , by you, lord . . . , deputed collector by authority of the apostolic see and also by the same see, to exact, collect, and receive the tenth of all ecclesiastical rents and proceeds from all ecclesiastical persons, exempt and non-exempt, constituted in your city and diocese—the priors, preceptors, masters, and other persons and places of the Hospital of Saint John of Jerusalem and of the other military orders alone excepted—imposed by the same see for the business of the Holy Land and of the catholic faith, that I will faithfully exact, collect, and receive and keep the same tenth, not deferring in these matters to any person of whatever dignity, state, condition, or dignity he may exist, by prayer, fear, favor, or grace or for any cause, and I will integrally restore and assign to you when I shall have received it from you in mandate, and concerning all and singular—namely from the aforesaid non-exempt to you, from the same exempt by me exacted, collected, and received—I will render final and entire account to him or them whom the aforesaid see shall have deemed to be deputed for this, or also about to be deputed, and if it happen that you leave the office which you bear in the premises, I will do these same things according to the mandate of him who shall be substituted in the same office. So help me God and these holy gospels of God.\"","Volumus insuper quod quilibet vestrum de praemissis diligenter et sollicite prosequendis personas easdem per eum ad dictam colligendam decimam deputatas iurare faciat in hac forma: iuro ego ... a vobis domino ... auctoritate sedis apostolicae ac etiam ab eadem sede deputatus collector ad exigendum colligendum et recipiendum decimam omnium reddituum et proventuum ecclesiasticorum ab omnibus personis ecclesiasticis exemptis et non exemptis in civitate et dioecesi vestris prioribus praeceptoribus magistris et aliis personis ac locis hospitalis sancti Ioannis ierosolimitani et aliorum militarium ordinum dumtaxat exceptis constitutis pro terrae sanctae ac fidei catholicae negotiis ab eadem sede impositam quod fideliter exigam colligam et recipiam atque custodiam ipsam decimam non deferendo in hiis alicui personae cuiusvis dignitatis status conditionis aut dignitatis exsistat prece timore gratia vel favore seu quacunque de causa et integre vobis restituam et assignabo cum id a vobis recepero in mandatis et super omnibus et singulis videlicet a praedictis non exemptis vobis ab eisdem vero exemptis per me exactis collectis et receptis illi vel illis quem vel quos praefata sedes ad id deputandum duxerit seu etiam deputandos reddam finalem et integram rationem et si contingat vos officium quod in praemissis geritis dimittere haec eadem faciam iuxta mandatum illius qui substituetur in eodem officio sic me deus adiuvet et haec sancta dei evangelia."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p96","Redemptor Noster","Given at Avignon, on the Kalends of December [December 1], in the eighth year.","Dat(um) Avinione kal(endis) decembris anno octavo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p97","Licet Dudum","For the remembrance of the future.","Ad memoriam futurorum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p98","Licet Dudum","Although recently at Vienne, constituted in a general council, the goods of the former order of the Temple, with their rights, privileges, indulgences, immunities, and liberties having been first transferred by us, with the sacred council approving, into the order of the Hospital of Saint John of Jerusalem, for the purpose of establishing peace and concord more readily and firmly among the prelates of churches and other ecclesiastical men and the brothers of the said order of the Hospital, and for other probable causes also concurring, we deemed that all the privileges formerly granted to the aforesaid order of the Hospital by the apostolic see, and consequently without doubt the privileges of the former Temple which ought to be considered [as belonging] to the Hospital and ought [to be considered] as having been transferred into the order of the Hospital itself—except, however, the privilege of exemption if they had any—be altogether suspended in the last session of the same council, and we wished them to remain suspended until the good pleasure of our will.","Licet dudum Viennae in generali concilio constituti bonis ordinis quondam templi cum suis iuribus privilegiis indulgentiis immunitatibus et libertatibus prius in ordinem hospitalis sancti Ioannis ierosolimitani per nos sacro concilio approbante translatis pro pace et concordia inter ecclesiarum praelatos ceteros que viros ecclesiasticos et fratres ordinis dicti hospitalis stabiliendis promptius et firmandis certis ad hoc aliis nihilominus probabilibus concurrentibus causis omnia privilegia praefato ordini hospitalis per sedem apostolicam dudum concessa et per consequens procul dubio privilegia quondam templi quae censeri debebant hospitalis et debent velut iam in ipsum ordinem hospitalis translata excepto tamen exemptionis privilegio si quod habebant in ultima eiusdem concilii sessione omnino duxerimus suspendenda ea que voluerimus usque ad voluntatis nostrae beneplacitum manere suspensa"],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p99","Licet Dudum","Certain persons, however, drawing the premises less fully into the examination of reason, do not fear to assert that such suspension of the privileges of the aforesaid Hospital does not extend to the privileges of the former order of the Temple.","quidam tamen minus plene praemissa in rationis examine deducentes asserere non verentur suspensionem huiusmodi ipsorum privilegiorum hospitalis praedicti ad ipsius ordinis templi quondam privilegia non extendi."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p100","Licet Dudum","Although, however, not even the color of any probable reason supports the assertion of such persons, we nevertheless, wishing to remove every matter of doubt from the hearts of the same, declare by apostolic authority that it was our intention through the aforesaid suspension of the privileges of the order of the Hospital to suspend the privileges of the former Temple which had already been affected by the aforesaid transfer to the order of the Hospital itself, and we decree that they are and remain suspended just as the other privileges of the Hospital.","Quamquam autem assertioni talium non suffragetur etiam color alicuius probabilis rationis nos tamen a cordibus eorundem volentes cuiusvis dubii materiam submovere nostrae fuisse intentionis per suspensionem praedictam privilegiorum ordinis hospitalis privilegia quondam templi suspendere quae iam erant per translationem praedictam ipsius ordinis hospitalis affecta auctoritate apostolica declaramus ac illa esse decernimus sicut et alia privilegia hospitalis et remanere suspensa."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p101","Licet Dudum","Now, although before the aforesaid suspension it was said in common by some of our brothers the cardinals of the holy Roman church to several prelates who had convened to the general council that the suspension itself of the privileges of the Hospital would be made to last until all things concerning concord, causes, and lawsuits which were between the said prelates and other ecclesiastical men were completely finished and perfectly completed, we nevertheless, attending that if the end of such causes and lawsuits ought to be awaited for all, grave prejudice could be generated to the order of the Hospitallers themselves by one moderate cause and their privileges could remain suspended for a long time not without their great loss—considering also that this could supply matter for many calumnies—in the said last session, wishing to meet such perils, we decreed it to be declared by living voice clearly and openly, repeating this even a third time so that it could be fully understood by all and singular, that we wish the said suspension of the privileges of the aforesaid order of the Hospital to endure until our good pleasure, intending, the Lord cooperating, so to dispose our good pleasure of will—the utilities of both sides having been weighed—that provision will be made for the prelates and other ecclesiastical persons and for the Hospitallers themselves in the premises such that neither will have matter for complaint, but rather both may be able to be worthily contented.","Sane licet per aliquos fratres nostros sanctae Romanae ecclesiae cardinales pluribus ex praelatis qui ad concilium generale convenerant ante suspensionem praefatam dictum fuerit in communi quod ipsa privilegiorum hospitalis suspensio fieret quousque omnia circa concordiam causas et lites quae inter dictos praelatos et alios viros ecclesiasticos erant dubia essent completa et perfecta integraliter duratura nos tamen attendentes quod si finis causarum et litium huiusmodi deberet omnium expectari posset per unam modicam causam ipsi ordini hospitalariorum grave praeiudicium generari et sua privilegia diutius non absque eorum grandi dispendio remanere suspensa considerantes nihilominus quod istud posset materiam multis ministrare calumniis in dicta sessione ultima volentes tantis periculis obviare viva voce clare et aperte promendum duximus hoc etiam tertio ut ab omnibus et singulis plene posset intelligi repetendo nos velle dictam suspensionem privilegiorum ordinis hospitalis praedicti usque ad nostrum beneplacitum perdurare intendentes cooperante domino sic utrorumque pensatis utilitatibus nostrae disponere beneplacitum voluntatis quod et praelatis et personis aliis ecclesiasticis ac ipsis hospitalariis sic providebitur in praemissis quod neutri supererit conquerendi materia quin immo potius poterunt utrique merito contentari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p102","Licet Dudum","Let no one therefore ....","Nulli ergo ...."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p103","Licet Dudum","Given at Avignon, on the 15th of the Kalends of January [December 18], in the eighth year.","Dat(um) Avinione xv kal(endas) ianuarii anno octavo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p104","Fidei Catholicae","For perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Ad perpetuam rei memoriam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p105","Fidei Catholicae","Firmly adhering to the foundation of the catholic faith, besides which, as the apostle testifies, no one is able to place another, we confess openly with holy mother church that the only-begotten Son of God, in all those things in which God the Father exists, subsisting aeternally with the Father, assumed parts of our nature united at the same time—namely, a passible human body and an intellective or rational soul truly informing that body by itself and essentially—from which He Himself, existing as true God, might become true man, in the virginal chamber at the time, into the unity of His hypostasis and person.","Fidei catholicae fundamento praeter quod teste apostolo nemo potest aliud ponere firmiter inhaerentes aperte cum sancta matre ecclesia confitemur unigenitum dei filium in his omnibus in quibus deus pater exsistit una cum patre aeternaliter subsistentem partes nostrae naturae simul unitas ex quibus ipse in se verus deus exsistens fieret verus homo humanum videlicet corpus passibile et animam intellectivam seu rationalem ipsum corpus vere per se et essentialiter informantem assumpsisse ex tempore in virginali thalamo ad unitatem suae hypostasis et personae."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p106","Fidei Catholicae","And that in this assumed nature the Word of God Himself, for the purpose of working the salvation of all, willed not only to be affixed to the cross and to die on it, but also, after He had already given up His spirit, He permitted His side to be pierced by a lance, so that from it, with the flowing forth of the waves of water and blood, the one and immaculate and holy virgin mother church, the spouse of Christ, might be formed, just as from the side of the first man when he was put to sleep, Eve was formed for him into marriage, so that thus the truth might correspond to the figure of the first and old Adam—who according to the apostle is the form of the future—in our newest Adam, that is Christ.","Et quod in hac assumpta natura ipsum dei verbum pro omnium operanda salute non solum affigi cruci et in ea mori voluit sed etiam emisso iam spiritu perforari lancea sustinuit latus suum ut exinde profluentibus undis aquae et sanguinis formaretur unica et immaculata ac virgo sancta mater ecclesia coniunx Christi sicut de latere primi hominis soporati Eva sibi in conjugium est formata ut sic certae figurae primi et veteris Adae qui secundum apostolum est forma futuri in nostro novissimo Adam id est Christo veritas responderet."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p107","Fidei Catholicae","This is, I say, the truth of that very great eagle fortified with testimony, whom the prophet Ezekiel saw flying above the other evangelical animals—namely, blessed John, apostle and evangelist, who narrating the deed and the order of this sacrament in his gospel said: \"But when they came to Jesus, as they saw that He was already dead, they did not break His legs, but one of the soldiers opened His side with a lance, and immediately there came out blood and water. And he who saw it has borne witness, and his witness is true, and he knows that he speaks truth, so that you also may believe.\"","Haec est inquam veritas illius praegrandis aquilae vallata testimonio quam propheta vidit Ezechiel animalibus ceteris evangelicis transvolantem beati Ioannis videlicet apostoli et evangelistae qui sacramenti huius rem gestam narrans et ordinem in evangelio suo dixit: ad Iesum autem cum venissent ut viderunt eum iam mortuum non fregerunt eius crura sed unus militum lancea latus eius aperuit et continuo exivit sanguis et aqua. Et qui vidit testimonium perhibuit et verum est testimonium eius et ille scit quia vera dicit ut et vos credatis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p108","Fidei Catholicae","We therefore, converting the point of apostolic consideration—to which alone the declaration of these things pertains—to so distinguished a testimony and to the common sentence of the holy fathers and doctors, with the sacred council approving, declare that the aforesaid apostle and evangelist John held the right order of the deed in the premises in narrating that when Christ was already dead, one of the soldiers opened His side with a lance.","Nos igitur ad tam praeclarum testimonium ac sanctorum patrum et doctorum communem sententiam apostolicae considerationis ad quam dumtaxat haec declarare pertinet aciem convertentes sacro approbante concilio declaramus praedictum apostolum et evangelistam Ioannem rectum in praemissis factae rei ordinem tenuisse narrando quod Christo iam mortuo unus militum lancea latus eius aperuit."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p109","Fidei Catholicae","Moreover, every doctrine or position rashly asserting or turning into doubt that the substance of the rational or intellective soul is not truly and by itself the form of the human body, as erroneous and inimical to the truth of the catholic faith, with the aforesaid sacred council approving, we reprobate, defining—so that the truth of sincere faith may be known to all and the approach may be closed to all errors lest they enter—that whoever from now on shall have presumed to assert, defend, or pertinaciously hold that the rational or intellective soul is not the form of the human body by itself and essentially, be held as a heretic.","Porro doctrinam omnem seu positionem temere asserentem aut vertentem in dubium quod substantia animae rationalis seu intellectivae vere ac per se humani corporis non sit forma velut erroneam ac veritati catholicae fidei inimicam praedicto sacro approbante concilio reprobamus diffinientes ut cunctis nota sit fidei sincerae veritas ac praecludatur universis erroribus aditus ne subintrent quod quisquis deinceps asserere defendere seu tenere pertinaciter praesumpserit quod anima rationalis seu intellectiva non sit forma corporis humani per se essentialiter tanquam haereticus sit censendus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p110","Fidei Catholicae","To this: that baptism is one, regenerating all the baptized in Christ, just as there is one God and one faith, is to be faithfully confessed by all—that we believe baptism celebrated in water in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit to be a perfect remedy for salvation commonly for both adults and infants.","Ad hoc baptisma unicum baptizatos omnes in Christo regenerans est sicut unus deus ac fides unica ab omnibus fideliter confitendum quod celebratum in aqua in nomine patris et filii et spiritus sancti credimus esse tam adultis quam parvulis communiter perfectum remedium ad salutem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p111","Fidei Catholicae","But because concerning the effect of baptism in infants certain theological doctors are found to have had contrary opinions—some of them saying that by the power of baptism guilt is indeed remitted to infants but grace is not conferred, others on the contrary asserting that both guilt and virtues and informing grace are infused in the same infants in baptism as to habit, even if not at that time as to use—we, attending to the general efficacy of the death of Christ, which is applied through baptism equally to all the baptized, decree, with the sacred council approving, that the second opinion, which says that informing grace and virtues are conferred in baptism on both infants and adults, as more probable and more consonant with the sayings of the saints and modern doctors of theology, is to be chosen.","Verum quia quantum ad effectum baptismi in parvulis reperiuntur doctores quidam theologi opiniones contrarias habuisse quibusdam ex ipsis dicentibus per virtutem baptismi parvulis quidem culpam remitti sed gratiam non conferri aliis e contra asserentibus quod et culpa eisdem in baptismo et virtutes ac informans gratia infunduntur quoad habitum etsi non pro illo tempore quoad usum nos autem attendentes generalem efficaciam mortis Christi quae per baptisma applicatur pariter omnibus baptizatis opinionem Secundam quae dicit tam parvulis quam adultis conferri in baptismo informantem gratiam et virtutes tanquam probabiliorem et dictis sanctorum ac doctorum modernorum theologiae magis consonam et concordem sacro approbante concilio duximus eligendam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p112","Constitution Concerning Abbots and Religious","Abbots or other religious persons presiding over any administrations by reason of priories or other places subject to them, may not by any means convene by authority of the apostolic see or of its legates except where and before whom it would be lawful for the priors or other governors of those priories and places.","Abbates aut alii religiosi quibuslibet administrationibus praesidentes occasione prioratuum seu aliorum locorum subditorum eisdem nullum auctoritate sedis apostolicae vel legatorum ipsius quomodolibet valeant convenire nisi ubi et coram quibus prioratuum et locorum ipsorum prioribus aut aliis gubernatoribus hoc liceret."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p113","Constitution Concerning Abbots and Religious","Nor by occasion of places pertaining to their tables may anything else be lawful to them concerning this than if the places themselves thus premised had special governors under them.","Nec occasione locorum ad mensas pertinentium eorundem aliud ipsis super hoc liceat quam si loca ipsa sic praemissa speciales sub eis gubernatores haberent."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p114","Constitution Concerning Abbots and Religious","But if anyone shall have presumed to vex anyone against these things, let him be condemned in his own expenses and interest by the judge before whom such vexation will be done, and every process against the premises shall be invalid ipso iure.","Si quis autem aliquem contra haec vexare praesumpserit in expensis sibi et interesse per iudicem coram quo vexatio fiet huiusmodi condemnetur omnis que processus contra praemissa sit irritus ipso iure."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p115","Constitution Concerning Abbots and Religious","Although the principal official of a bishop or a religious obtaining a conventual priory—even though the prior of the same priory has not been accustomed to be assumed by election—may be given as delegated by the apostolic see or its legate, we by no means wish this to be observed concerning a foreign official or concerning the cloistral prior of his monastery.","Etsi principalis officialis episcopi aut religiosus conventualem obtinens prioratum quamvis ad prioratum eundem prior non consueverit per electionem assumi a sede apostolica vel legato eiusdem dari valeat delegatus in officiali tamen foraneo aut religioso monasterii sui priore claustrali nequaquam hoc volumus observari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p116","Constitution Concerning the Negligence of Prelates","Because regular prelates sometimes neglect to commit or confer priories, churches, administrations, or any other benefices pertaining to their disposition when they become vacant, within the time established in the Lateran council, the diocesans of the places in non-exempt [churches] by their own authority, but in exempt [churches] by apostolic authority, shall supply their negligence concerning this; such priories, churches, administrations, and benefices—namely, those which have been accustomed to be governed by secular clerics [shall be conferred] on secular clerics, but those which have been accustomed to be committed or conferred only to religious [shall be conferred] on the religious of the monasteries whose prelates have been thus negligent in conferring.","Quia regulares praelati prioratus ecclesias administrationes aut quaevis alia beneficia ad eorum dispositionem spectantia cum vacant interdum committere negligunt vel conferre infra tempus in Lateranensi concilio constitutum dioecesani locorum in non exemptis sua in exemptis vero apostolica auctoritate negligentiam super hoc suppleant eorundem prioratus ecclesias administrationes et beneficia huiusmodi illa videlicet quae consueverunt per saeculares clericos gubernari saecularibus clericis alia vero quae religiosis dumtaxat committi sunt solita vel conferri religiosis monasteriorum quorum praelati huiusmodi negligentes fuerint conferendo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p117","Constitution Concerning the Negligence of Prelates","Supported also by the same authority, let the diocesans by no means permit that the same prelates apply such priories, churches, administrations, or benefices to their tables, or impose new pensions on them, or increase old ones, or that what have been newly imposed or increased be paid to them.","Eadem quoque auctoritate dioecesani suffulti nullo modo permittant quod iidem praelati prioratus ecclesias administrationes aut beneficia huiusmodi applicent mensis suis pensiones ve novas eis imponant aut veteres augeant sive quae ipsis de novo impositae sive auctae solvantur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p118","Constitution Concerning the Negligence of Prelates","Concerning priories, churches, administrations, and benefices we understand the premises which are not of the table of the prelates themselves but have been accustomed to have special priors, administrators, or rectors, although such priors or administrators may be able freely to be recalled to the cloister when necessary.","Praemissa vero de prioratibus ecclesiis administrationibus et beneficiis intelligimus quae non sunt de mensa praelatorum ipsorum sed speciales priores administratores seu rectores consueverunt habere licet priores seu administratores huiusmodi libere possint ad claustrum cum oportuerit revocari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p119","Constitution Concerning the Negligence of Prelates","In which also the constitution of Pope Boniface VIII, our predecessor, prohibiting goods of vacant benefices to be occupied by prelates or others, and nonetheless in such priories and churches the statute of the same Boniface which prohibits any religious person to preside over several priories or churches having the care of souls, even if that care is exercised not by him but by another, we decree to claim a place for itself, even though the care does not impend over the same priories as above said.","In quibus etiam constitutionem Bonifacii papae viii praedecessoris nostri bona beneficiorum vacantium occupari a praelatis vel aliis prohibentem ac nihilominus in prioratibus huiusmodi et ecclesiis illud Bonifacii eiusdem statutum quod religiosum quemcunque pluribus praeesse prohibet prioratibus aut ecclesiis curam animarum habentibus etiamsi cura ipsa non per eum sed per alium habeat exerceri locum sibi censuimus vendicare licet etiam eisdem prioratibus cura non immineat supra. dicta"],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p120","Constitution Concerning Clerics Not Promoted to Sacred Orders","So that those who are or will be in the future attached to divine offices in cathedral or collegiate secular churches may be more strongly induced to receive sacred orders, we decree that no one henceforth have a voice in the chapter in such churches, even if this be freely granted to him by others, unless he shall have been at least constituted in the order of subdeacon.","Ut ii qui divinis in cathedralibus vel collegiatis saecularibus ecclesiis sunt mancipati officiis vel mancipabuntur in posterum ad suscipiendos sacros ordines propensius inducantur statuimus ut nullus de cetero in huiusmodi ecclesiis vocem in capitulo habeat etiamsi hoc sibi ab aliis libere concedatur nisi saltem in subdiaconatus ordine fuerit constitutus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p121","Constitution Concerning Clerics Not Promoted to Sacred Orders","But those who now peacefully obtain dignities, personates, offices, or prebends to which certain orders are annexed in the same churches, or shall obtain them in the future, unless, a just impediment ceasing, they shall have caused themselves to be promoted to such orders within one year, from then on until they shall have been promoted to them, by no means have a voice in the chapter of the same.","Illi vero qui dignitates personatus officia vel praebendas quibus certi ordines sunt annexi pacifice nunc obtinent in eisdem ecclesiis vel obtinuerint in futurum nisi iusto impedimento cessante ad huiusmodi ordines se promoveri fecerint infra annum extunc donec ad eos promoti fuerint nullo modo vocem in capitulo habeant earumdem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p122","Constitution Concerning Clerics Not Promoted to Sacred Orders","Moreover, let half of the distributions which are given to those who attend at certain hours be subtracted from them, notwithstanding any customs or statutes whatever, other penalties which are established by law against those refusing to be promoted to such orders nonetheless remaining in their force.","Ipsis que distributionum quae dantur his qui certis horis intersunt pars dimidia subtrahatur non obstantibus quibuslibet consuetudinibus vel statutis poenis aliis quae contra tales promoveri ad ordines recusantes statuuntur in iure nihilominus in suo robore permansuris."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p123","Constitution Concerning the Avoidance of Expenses of Lawsuits","Wishing to restrict the disadvantageous prolongation of lawsuits, which sometimes experience shows to arise from the subtle observation of the judicial order in the following cases, we decree that in causes concerning elections, postulations, or provisions, or concerning dignities, personates, offices, canonries, or prebends, or any ecclesiastical benefices whatsoever, or concerning tithes—to the payment of which even those who are held to them may be coerced by ecclesiastical censure after being warned—and also concerning marriages or usuries and those touching them in any way, it may be valid to proceed henceforth simply and plainly and without the noise and figure of judgment, wishing this to be extended not only to future negotiations but also to present ones and even to those pending by appeal.","Dispendiosam prorogationem litium quam interdum ex subtili ordinis iudiciarii observatione causarum docet experientiam provenire restringere in subscriptis casibus cupientes statuimus ut in causis super electionibus postulationibus vel provisionibus aut super dignitatibus personatibus officiis canonicatibus vel prebendis seu quibusvis beneficiis ecclesiasticis aut super decimis ad quarum etiam praestationem possunt qui tenentur ad eas praemoniti censura ecclesiastica coerceri nec non super matrimoniis vel usuris et eas quoquo modo tangentibus ventilandis procedi valeat de cetero simpliciter et de plano ac sine strepitu iudicii et figura volentes non solum ad futura negotia sed ad praesentia et adhuc etiam per appellationem pendentia hoc extendi."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p124","Constitution Concerning Bishops Who Have Been Expelled","Although it is generally forbidden by the sacred canons that any bishop exercise jurisdiction in a foreign diocese, we nevertheless have deemed to grant to bishops who, expelled from their sees by the protervia of impious men, do not dare, on account of fear of their persecutors, to reside in their cities or dioceses or any part of them, nor to exercise the jurisdiction pertaining to them there through themselves or through another, lest the injury inflicted upon their churches in their expulsion on this pretext remain unpunished, that in foreign dioceses—namely in cities or notable places near their churches, in which they may be able to dwell securely and freely exercise their jurisdiction—they may be able to proceed freely against their expellers and their counselors and supporters in this matter, provided that they have been cited in the cities or places of this kind which are safe for the same expellers, counselors, and supporters, either personally or to the home if this can be done safely, otherwise publicly in the cathedral church of the place or of their dwelling, as justice will persuade.","Quamvis sacris canonibus sit generaliter interdictum ne quis episcopus iurisdictionem in dioecesi exerceat aliena nos tamen episcopis qui a suis sedibus protervia impiorum expulsi non audent propter metum persequentium in suis civitatibus vel dioecesibus aut earum parte aliqua residere nec iurisdictionem ad se spectantem per se vel per alium inibi exercere ne iniuria in eorum expulsione ipsorum irrogata ecclesiis hoc praetextu remaneat impunita duximus indulgendum ut in dioecesibus alienis in civitatibus videlicet vel locis insignibus suis ecclesiis vicinioribus in quibus poterunt secure morari et iurisdictionem suam libere exercere possint contra suos expulsores et eorum in hac parte consiliarios et fautores dummodo civitates ve loca huiusmodi eisdem expulsoribus consiliariis et fautoribus sint secura et ipsi personaliter vel ad domum si hoc tute fieri valeat alioquin publice in ecclesia cathedrali loci vel domicilii eorumdem citati fuerint libere procedere prout iustitia suadebit."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p125","Constitution Concerning Bishops Who Have Been Expelled","But if the said bishops shall not have dared to cite their expellers or their counselors or supporters in the said manner, or they [the expellers] have maliciously interposed an impediment so that they could not be thus cited, it shall be lawful for the same bishops to cite or cause to be cited the same expellers, counselors, and supporters on certain Lord's days or feast days when the people have assembled for divine [offices], so that such citation may be more likely presumed to reach them, publicly in the churches of the aforesaid cities or notable places, and thereafter to proceed against them if they, thus cited, shall have been unwilling to appear before the same bishops within a peremptory term fixed for them, just as if they had been otherwise legitimately cited.","Si vero dicti episcopi ausi non fuerint huiusmodi suos expulsores vel eorum consiliarios aut fautores dicto modo citare vel ipsi malitiose impedimentum praestiterint ne potuerint sic citari licitum sit eisdem episcopis ut expulsores consiliarios et fautores eosdem diebus aliquibus dominicis vel festivis cum populus convenerit ad divina ut sic facta citatio ad eos pervenire verisimilius praesumatur citent vel citari faciant publice in ecclesiis civitatum vel locorum insignium praedictorum et procedant deinde contra ipsos si citati taliter statuto ipsis peremptorio termino competenti coram eisdem episcopis noluerint comparere ac si citati legitime alias exstitissent."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p126","Constitution Concerning Bishops Who Have Been Expelled","We also grant to the same bishops that they may be able to exercise their omnimodal jurisdiction among their subjects, lest they suffer any loss on account of a defect of justice, in the foreign dioceses in which they have stayed, provided that they do not dare or are unable to do this through themselves or through another in their own dioceses—yet in such a way that subjects who have not been expellers or their supporters or counselors be not drawn beyond two journeys from the boundary of their diocese.","Eisdem etiam episcopis indulgemus ut inter suos subditos ne ob defectum iustitiae dispendium aliquod patiantur possint in dioecesibus alienis in quibus moram traxerint dummodo per se vel per alium hoc in suis propriis dioecesibus facere non audeant vel non possint iurisdictionem suam omnimodam exercere ita tamen quod subditi qui expulsores vel eorum fautores aut consiliarii non fuerint ultra duas diaetas a fine suae dioecesis non trahantur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p127","Constitution Concerning Bishops Who Have Been Expelled","We wish moreover that before all things they seek license from the diocesans of the places in which they have stayed concerning the premises, which if they shall not have been able to obtain, they may nevertheless proceed as is expressed above.","Volumus insuper ut ante omnia a dioecesanis locorum in quibus moram traxerint petant licentiam in praemissis quam si obtinere nequiverint procedant nihilominus prout superius est expressum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p128","Constitution Concerning Bishops Who Have Been Expelled","Now, if the aforesaid bishops shall have asserted that they did not dare to cite the aforesaid expellers, counselors, or supporters personally or to the home or publicly in their cathedral church as premised, or that they have maliciously interposed an impediment so that they could not be thus cited by them, or that they are unable or do not dare to exercise jurisdiction against them in their own dioceses through themselves or through another—we wish to be stood by the assertions of them concerning these things, more strictly enjoining upon them under the intermination of divine judgment that they assert nothing mendaciously or deceitfully concerning the premises against conscience.","Sane si praedicti episcopi asseruerint se non fuisse ausos praefatos expulsores consiliarios vel fautores personaliter vel ad domum aut publice ut praemittitur in eorum ecclesia cathedrali citare vel ipsos malitiose praestitisse impedimentum ne per eos potuerint sic citari aut quod in suis dioecesibus iurisdictionem per se vel alium contra ipsos exercere nequeunt vel non audent stari super his volumus assertionibus eorundem ipsis districtius iniungentes sub interminatione divini iudicii ne circa praemissa contra conscientiam quicquam mendaciter aut dolose asserant vel confingant."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p129","Constitution Concerning Bishops Who Have Been Expelled","Moreover, let the neighboring bishops, when they shall have been requested by them, denounce or cause to be denounced through their dioceses the sentences and processes of the same bishops which they shall have deemed to be made in the aforesaid cases in other dioceses.","Eorundem autem episcoporum sententias et processus quos in praemissis casibus in aliis dioecesibus duxerint faciendos denuncient seu denunciari faciant per suas dioeceses convicini episcopi cum ab eis fuerint requisiti."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p130","Constitution Concerning Bishops Who Have Been Expelled","Through the premises also, let no prejudice be generated to the diocesans of the places or to any others having jurisdiction in the aforesaid places in which the said thus expelled bishops will exercise jurisdiction as premised, by the passage of time however long or otherwise in any way.","Per praemissa quoque nullum ex transcursu temporis quanticunque vel alias quoquo modo locorum dioecesanis vel aliis quibuscunque iurisdictionem habentibus in locis praemissis in quibus praedicti episcopi sic expulsi iurisdictionem ut praedicitur exercebunt praeiudicium generetur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p131","Constitution Concerning Clerics Who Exercise the Office of Butcher, Tavern-keepe","We strictly command the diocesans of places that they admonish by name and a third time clerics who publicly and personally exercise the office of butchers or of meat-sellers or of tavern-keepers, that they desist from such offices within a convenient term to be moderated at the discretion of those admonishing, and that they never reassume them at any time.","Dioecesanis locorum districte praecipimus ut clericos carnificum seu macellariorum aut tabernariorum officium publice et personaliter exercentes nominatim et tertio moneant ut sic ab huiusmodi officiis infra convenientem terminum monentium ipsorum arbitrio moderandum desistant et quod ipsa nullo unquam tempore reassumant."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p132","Constitution Concerning Clerics Who Exercise the Office of Butcher, Tavern-keepe","Who, if thus admonished, shall not have desisted from these things, or shall have reassumed them by exercising as premised, if married, let them altogether [lose the clerical privilege?]; but if not married, in things and if they proceed wholly as laypersons in their persons, let them lose the clerical privilege as long as they shall have insisted on the premises, ipso facto.","Qui si taliter moniti ab his non destiterint aut ipsa ut praemissum est exercendo resumpserint quandocunque coniugati omnino non coniugati vero in rebus et si omnino incedant ut laici in personis privilegium clericale quamdiu praemissis institerint eo ipso amittant."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p133","Constitution Concerning Clerics Who Exercise the Office of Butcher, Tavern-keepe","But against other clerics publicly insisting upon secular negotiations or trades or offices not suitable to the clerical purpose, or bearing arms, let them take care so to observe the canonical institutes that both they may be restrained from such excesses and they themselves may not be able to be reprehended concerning damnable negligence in these matters.","Adversus vero alios clericos negotiationibus vel commerciis saecularibus vel officiis non convenientibus clericali proposito publice insistentes vel arma portantes sic canonica servare studeant instituta quod et illi ab excessibus compescantur huiusmodi et ipsi de damnabili circa haec negligentia nequeant reprehendi."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p134","Constitution Concerning the Clothing of Clerics","Because he who, having cast aside the garments proper to his order, presumes to assume and to bear others in public without a reasonable cause ceasing, renders himself unworthy of the prerogative of the professors of that order, we sanction by the present constitution that any cleric who shall publicly use a striped or particolored garment, unless a reasonable cause underlie, if he shall be a beneficiary, be suspended ipso facto for six months from the perception of the fruits of the benefices which he obtains.","Quoniam qui abiectis vestibus proprio congruentibus ordini alias assumere et in publico portare rationabili causa cessante praesumit professorum illius ordinis praerogativa se reddit indignum praesenti constitutione sancimus quod quicunque clericus virgata vel partita veste publice utetur nisi causa rationabilis subsit si beneficiatus exstiterit per sex menses a perceptione fructuum beneficiorum quae obtinet sit eo ipso suspensus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p135","Constitution Concerning the Clothing of Clerics","But if he shall not be a beneficiary, yet constituted in sacred orders below the priesthood, let him be rendered incapable ipso facto for the same time of obtaining an ecclesiastical benefice.","Si vero beneficiatus non fuerit in sacris tamen ordinibus citra sacerdotium constitutus per idem tempus reddatur eo ipso inhabilis ad ecclesiasticum beneficium obtinendum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p136","Constitution Concerning the Clothing of Clerics","We also decree the same concerning other clerics publicly bearing such a garment together with the clerical tonsure.","Idem quoque censemus de clericis aliis vestem talem simul et tonsuram publice deferentibus clericalem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p137","Constitution Concerning the Clothing of Clerics","Moreover, one obtaining a dignity, personate, or other benefice to which the care of souls impends, and also others constituted in the priesthood, and any religious persons—whom it befits to show the inward honesty of morals by the decency of outward habit—if, except for a reasonable cause, they publicly bear such a garment, or publicly wear a fillet or a linen cap on their heads, be suspended ipso facto, namely beneficiaries, from the perception of the fruits of the benefices which they obtain for one year.","Dignitatem vero personatum seu beneficium aliud obtinens cui cura immineat animarum nec non ceteri in sacerdotio constituti ac religiosi quilibet quos oportet per decentiam habitus extrinseci morum intrinsecam honestatem ostendere si praeterquam ex causa rationabili publice vestem ferant huiusmodi aut infulam seu pileum lineum publice portent in capite sint eo ipso beneficiati videlicet a perceptione fructuum beneficiorum quae obtinent suspensi per annum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p138","Constitution Concerning the Clothing of Clerics","But other priests and any religious persons, let them be rendered incapable for the same time of obtaining any ecclesiastical benefice.","Ceteri vero sacerdotes et religiosi quilibet per idem tempus reddantur inhabiles ad quodcunque beneficium ecclesiasticum obtinendum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p139","Constitution Concerning the Clothing of Clerics","But also such persons and any other clerics whatsoever using an epitogium or furred tabard down to the hem and so short that the lower garment is notably seen—secular clerics and religious persons having administration shall be held to give that same epitogium to the poor within one month.","Sed et tales et ceteri quicunque clerici utentes epitogio seu tabardo foderato usque ad oram et ita brevi quod vestis inferior notabiliter videatur epitogium ipsum saeculares clerici et religiosi administrationem habentes teneantur infra mensem dare pauperibus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p140","Constitution Concerning the Clothing of Clerics","But other religious persons not having administration shall be held to assign it to their superiors within the same time to be converted into some pious uses; otherwise, let them know that they have incurred the aforesaid penalties of suspension or incapacity for the same time.","Ceteri vero religiosi administrationem non habentes infra idem tempus illud teneantur suis superioribus assignare in pios usus aliquos convertendum alioquin beneficiati suspensionis ceteri vero inhabilitatis poenas praedictas per idem tempus se noverint incurrisse."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p141","Constitution Concerning the Clothing of Clerics","To this sanction we also add that clerics, especially beneficiaries, not publicly use checked, red, or green leggings.","Huic insuper adicimus sanctioni ut clerici praesertim beneficiati caligis scacatis rubeis aut viridibus publice non utantur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p142","Constitution Super Cat Hedram","Long ago by Pope Boniface VIII, our predecessor, the under-written decree was issued; our predecessor Pope Benedict XI promulgated another revocatory one of it, which, because it has not—as experience has proved—brought the fruit of the hoped-for peace by its author, but rather has furnished no small fuel for discord for the calming of which it had proceeded, we, altogether annulling it, renew the other issued by the aforesaid Boniface, the sacred council pressing and approving, subjoining its tenor, which is known to be such:","Dudum a Bonifacio papa viii praedecessore nostro infra scripta edita decretali Benedictus papa xi praedecessor noster aliam illius revocatoriam promulgavit quae quia ut probavit effectus nedum pacis ab auctore ipsius speratae fructum non attulit quin immo discordiae pro qua sedanda processerat fomentum non modicum ministravit nos eam omnino cassantes aliam a praefato Bonifacio editam sacro instante et approbante concilio innovamus subicientes tenorem illius qui dignoscitur esse talis"],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p143","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","Boniface, bishop, servant of the servants of God, for perpetual remembrance of the matter.","Bonifacius episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p144","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","Placed upon the chair of pre-eminence of the pastoral office by the disposing clemency of divine providence, and although we are weighed down as if by a torrent with many and arduous negotiations which flow together on all sides into the wide channel of the Roman curia, and we are excited by innumerable cares, and we are distracted by very many thoughts, nevertheless we intend and insist upon this with fervent vows, and we impart the study of operative solicitude, that to the glory of the divine name, the exaltation of the catholic faith, and the progress of the salvation of faithful souls, the thorns of dissensions having been rooted up from the roots and the windings of litigations having been altogether removed, the tranquillity of peace may flourish among the prelates of churches—deputed to the care and governance of the Lord's flock—and among other persons whom the clerical order includes, the fervor of charity may burn, the unity of concord may grow strong, and the identity of minds may persist.","Super cathedram praeeminentiae pastoralis divina disponente clementia constituti etsi multis et arduis quae in amplum Romanae curiae alveum undique confluunt quasi torrens praegravemur negotiis curis excitemur innumeris cogitationibus plurimis distrahamur circa id tamen ferventibus votis intendimus vacamus instantius ac operosae studium sollicitudinis impertimur ut ad divini nominis gloriam exaltationem catholicae fidei et profectum fidelium animarum praecisis radicitus dissidiorum vepribus et litigiorum anfractibus omnino subductis inter ecclesiarum antistites ad curam et regimen gregis dominici deputatos ceteras que personas quas ordo clericalis includit pacis tranquillitas vigeat fervor caritatis exaestuet invalescat concordiae unitas animorum identitas perseveret."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p145","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","For we know and we gather from the evidence of the fact that the Author of peace is not well cultivated except in the time of peace, nor are we ignorant that dissensions and scandals prepare an entrance for evil acts, stir up rancors and hatreds, and provide boldness for illicit morals.","Scimus enim et ex evidentia facti colligimus quod non nisi in pacis tempore bene colitur pacis auctor nec ignoramus quod dissensiones et scandala pravis actibus aditum praeparant rancores et odia suscitant et illicitis moribus ausum praebent."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p146","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","Long ago, indeed, between prelates and rectors or priests and clerics of parochial churches constituted through the various provinces of the world on the one part, and the brothers of the orders of Preachers and Minor on the other part, with the sower of zizania, the enemy of peace, procuring, a grave and perilous discord has arisen concerning the preachings to be made to the faithful peoples, their confessions to be heard, penances to be enjoined upon them, and the bodies of the dead to be buried who are known to choose burial among the churches or places of the same brothers.","Ab olim siquidem inter praelatos et rectores seu sacerdotes ac clericos parochialium ecclesiarum per diversas mundi provincias constitutos ex una parte et praedicatorum et minorum ordinum fratres ex altera pacis aemulo satore zizaniae procurante gravis et periculosa discordia exstitit suscitata super praedicationibus fidelium populis faciendis eorum confessionibus audiendis poenitentiis iniungendis eisdem et tumulandis defunctorum corporibus qui apud fratrum ipsorum ecclesias sive loca noscuntur eligere sepulturam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p147","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","We, however, as pious fathers, bearing with a praiseworthy manner the inconveniences of the sons, reducing [the matter] to the examination of exact consideration, and solicitously revolving within the enclosures of the heart how full of perils, how burdened with expenses, and how hateful in the sight of the divine majesty the above-mentioned discord is, and therefore intending to root it out altogether and to remove it in every way by the study of paternal solicitude—never to be aroused in any future times, the Lord favoring—and also greatly desiring that such a negotiation, which especially resides in our heart, may obtain a healthful and swift end through the study of apostolic skill, having held a diligent deliberation with our brothers concerning this, for the honor of God and the exaltation of the catholic faith, the quiet state of the aforesaid parties, and the increase of the salvation of souls, by the counsel of the same brothers we decree and ordain by apostolic authority as follows.","Nos autem pii patres more laudabili moleste ferentes incommoda filiorum reducentes ad exactae considerationis examen ac infra pecoris claustra sollicite revolventes quam sit plena periculis quam onusta dispendiis quam que in divinae maiestatis conspectu reddatur exosa discordia supra dicta et propterea intendentes paternae sollicitudinis studio illam prorsus evellere ac omnimode submovere nullis unquam futuris temporibus favente domino suscitandam grandi quoque desiderio cupientes ut huiusmodi negotium quod potissime insidet cordi nostro finem salubrem et celerem per apostolicae solertiae studium consequatur diligenti cum fratribus nostris deliberatione praehabita super eo ad honorem dei et exaltationem catholicae fidei quietum statum partium praedictarum ac salutis animarum fidelium incrementum de ipsorum fratrum consilio auctoritate apostolica statuimus et ordinamus"],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p148","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","That the brothers of the said orders may freely preach and set forth the word of God to the clergy and people in their churches and places and in public squares—that hour alone excepted in which the prelates of the places shall have wished to preach or to cause solemn preaching to be done before them; in that hour they shall cease to preach, unless something else shall have proceeded from the will and special license of the prelates themselves.","ut dictorum ordinum fratres in ecclesiis et locis eorum ac in plateis communibus libere valeant clero et populo praedicare ac proponere verbum dei hora illa dumtaxat excepta in qua locorum praelati praedicare voluerint vel coram se facere solemniter praedicari in qua praedicare cessabunt praeterquam si aliud de praelatorum ipsorum voluntate processerit ac licentia speciali."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p149","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","In general studies, however, where sermons to the clergy are accustomed to be made according to custom, on those days on which it is customary to preach solemnly, also at funerals of the dead and on special or peculiar feasts of the same brothers, the same brothers may and it is lawful for them freely to preach, unless perhaps that hour in which the word of God is accustomed to be set forth to the clergy in the aforesaid places, the bishop or superior prelate should generally convoke the clergy to himself or should lead the clergy to be gathered for some reason or cause.","In studiis autem generalibus ubi sermones ad clerum ex more fieri solent diebus illis quibus praedicari solemniter consuevit ad funera etiam mortuorum et in festis specialibus sive peculiaribus eorundem fratrum possunt iidem fratres et liceat eis libere praedicare nisi forte illa hora qua solet ad clerum in praedictis locis dei verbum proponi episcopus vel praelatus superior clerum ad se generaliter convocaret aut ex aliqua ratione vel causa clerum ipsum duceret congregandum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p150","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","In parochial churches, however, those brothers may by no means dare or ought to preach or set forth the word of God unless the aforesaid brothers shall have been invited or called by the parochial priests, and [it shall have been] with their good pleasure and assent, or license sought and obtained, unless the bishop or superior prelate should command preaching to be done by the same brothers.","In ecclesiis autem parochialibus fratres illi nullatenus audeant vel debeant praedicare vel proponere verbum dei nisi fratres praedicti a parochialibus sacerdotibus invitati fuerint vel vocati et de ipsorum beneplacito et assensu seu petita licentia fuerit et obtenta nisi episcopus vel praelatus superior per eosdem fratres praedicari mandaret."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p151","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","We also decree and ordain by the aforesaid authority that in each of the cities and dioceses in which the places of the same brothers are known to exist, or in cities and dioceses neighboring to those places in which such places are not had, the masters, provincial priors of the Preachers or their vicars, and the general and provincial ministers and custodians of the said orders of Minors, betake themselves to the presence of the prelates of the same places, through themselves or through brothers whom they shall have thought suitable for this, humbly about to seek that the brothers who shall have been elected for this may be able freely to hear the confessions of their subjects wishing to confess to them in their cities and dioceses, and to enjoin salutary penances upon such confessing persons as they shall have known expedient according to God, and to impart the benefit of absolution to them, by the license, favor, and good pleasure of the same [prelates].","Statuimus etiam et ordinamus auctoritate praedicta ut in singulis civitatibus et dioecesibus in quibus loca fratrum ipsorum consistere dignoscuntur vel in civitatibus et dioecesibus locis ipsis vicinis in quibus loca huiusmodi non habentur magistri priores provinciales praedicatorum aut eorum vicarii et generales et provinciales ministri et custodes minorum ordinum praedictorum ad praesentiam praelatorum eorundem locorum se conferant per se vel per fratres quos ad hoc idoneos fore putaverint humiliter petituri ut fratres qui ad hoc electi fuerint in eorum civitatibus et dioecesibus confessiones subditorum suorum confiteri sibi volentium audire libere valeant et huiusmodi confitentibus prout secundum deum expedire cognoverint poenitentias imponere salutares atque eisdem absolutionis beneficium impendere de licentia gratia et beneplacito eorundem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p152","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","And then the aforesaid masters, provincial priors, and ministers of the aforesaid orders shall take care to choose persons sufficient, suitable, of approved life, discreet, modest, and skilled for carrying out such a healthful ministry and office; which thus chosen by them, let them present or cause to be presented to the prelates, so that by their license, favor, and good pleasure in their cities and dioceses, such persons thus chosen may hear the confessions of those wishing to confess to them, enjoin salutary penances, and impart the benefit of absolution in the future as is expressed above—outside the cities and dioceses in which they shall have been deputed, through which we wish them and not through provinces to be deputed, about to hear confessions by no means.","Ac deinde praefati magistri priores provinciales et ministri ordinum praedictorum eligere studeant personas sufficientes idoneas vita probatas discretas modestas atque peritas ad tam salubre ministerium et officium exsequendum quas sic ab ipsis electas repraesentent vel faciant praesentari praelatis ut de eorum licentia gratia et beneplacito in civitatibus et dioecesibus eorundem huiusmodi personae sic electae confessiones confiteri sibi volentium audiant imponant poenitentias salutares et beneficium absolutionis in posterum impendant prout superius est expressum extra civitates et dioeceses in quibus fuerint deputatae per quas eas volumus et non per provincias deputari confessiones nullatenus audituras."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p153","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","The number of persons to be assumed for exercising such an office ought to be as the whole body of clergy and people and their multitude or paucity demands.","Numerus autem personarum assumendarum ad huiusmodi officium exercendum esse debet prout universitas cleri et populi ac multitudo vel paucitas exigit eorundem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p154","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","And if the same prelates shall have granted the sought license of hearing such confessions, let the aforesaid masters, ministers, and others receive it with giving of thanks, and the said persons thus chosen shall carry out the office committed to them.","Et si iidem praelati petitam licentiam confessionum huiusmodi audiendarum concesserint illam praefati magistri ministri et alii cum gratiarum recipiant actione dictae que personae sic electae commissum sibi officium exsequantur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p155","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","But if perhaps the said prelates should be unwilling to have or should not deem it appropriate to admit anyone of the said brothers presented to them for such an office, he having been removed or subtracted from the place of him, another is able and ought to be subrogated, similarly to be presented to the prelates.","Quod Si forte iam dicti praelati quemquam ex dictis fratribus praesentatis eisdem ad huiusmodi officium nollent habere vel non ducerent admittendum eo amoto vel subtracto loco ipsius similiter eisdem praesentandus praelatis possit et debeat alius subrogari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p156","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","But if the same prelates shall have refused to exhibit such license to the aforesaid brothers chosen for hearing confessions as premised, we from now on grant to them of the plenitude of apostolic power that they may freely and lawfully hear the confessions of those wishing to confess to them, and enjoin salutary penances upon them, and graciously impart the benefit of absolution to them.","Si vero iidem praelati praefatis fratribus ad confessiones ut praemittitur audiendas electis huiusmodi exhibere licentiam recusarint nos exnunc ipsis ut confessiones sibi confiteri volentium libere licite que audire valeant et eisdem poenitentias imponere salutares atque eisdem beneficium absolutionis impertiri gratiose concedimus de plenitudine apostolicae potestatis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p157","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","Through such concession, however, we by no means intend to impart greater power in this to the persons or brothers thus deputed for this than that which is granted by law to the curates or parochial priests, unless perhaps the prelates of the churches should specially deem that more abundant favor be made to them in this matter.","Per huiusmodi autem concessionem nequaquam intendimus personis seu fratribus ipsis ad id taliter deputatis potestatem in hoc impendere ampliorem quam in eo curatis vel parochialibus sacerdotibus est a iure concessa nisi forsan eis ecclesiarum praelati uberiorem in hac parte gratiam specialiter ducerent faciendam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p158","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","We also add to this our statute and ordinances that the brothers of the said orders have free burial in their churches or places constituted anywhere as follows: that they may receive all to it who shall have chosen to be buried in the places and churches aforesaid.","Huiusmodi quoque statuto et ordinationibus nostris adicimus ut fratres dictorum ordinum in ecclesiis vel locis suis ubilibet constitutis liberam ut sequitur habeant sepulturam videlicet quod omnes ad eam recipere valeant qui sepeliri elegerint in locis et ecclesiis memoratis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p159","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","But lest parochial churches and their curates or rectors who have to administer ecclesiastical sacraments, to whom it is known to pertain by law to preach or set forth the word of God and to hear the confessions of the faithful, be defrauded of due and necessary benefits, since the exhibition of wages to the workers is owed, we constitute and ordain by the same apostolic authority that the brothers of the said orders be held integrally to bestow the fourth part—which we tax and also limit by apostolic authority—to the parochial priests and rectors or curates of churches, from all obventions, both funeral and any whatever and in whatever way left, distinctly or indistinctly, to any certain or determined uses, from which even the fourth or canonical portion has not been accustomed to be given or exacted nor ought it by law, and also from things given or in whatever way donated at death or at the article of death in the infirmity of the donor or giver from which he has died, in whatever way directly or indirectly to the brothers themselves or to others on their behalf.","Verum ne parochiales ecclesiae et ipsarum curati sive rectores qui ministrare habent ecclesiastica sacramenta quibus noscitur de iure competere praedicare seu proponere verbum dei et confessiones fidelium debitis et necessariis beneficiis defraudentur cum operariis mercedis exhibitio debeatur auctoritate apostolica constituimus et ordinamus eadem ut dictorum ordinum fratres de obventionibus omnibus tam funeralibus quam quibuscunque et quomodocunque relictis distincte vel indistincte ad quoscunque certos vel determinatos usus de quibus etiam quarta sive canonica portio dari sive exigi non consuevit vel non debet de iure nec non de datis vel qualitercunque donatis in morte seu mortis articulo in infirmitate donantis vel dantis de qua decesserit quomodocunque directe vel indirecte fratribus ipsis vel aliis pro eisdem quartam partem quam auctoritate apostolica taxamus et etiam limitamus parochialibus sacerdotibus et ecclesiarum rectoribus seu curatis largiri integre teneantur"],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p160","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","And they shall do and take care that neither by others nor to others, from whom such fourth part would by no means be due, be such things left or thus given or donated to them to the utility or benefit of the same brothers, or that what would be to be given or donated to the same brothers at death or by the infirm be procured to be given or donated to them in the health of the same givers or donors.","facturi et curaturi quod nec alii nec aliis a quibus quarta huiusmodi minime deberetur ad ipsorum fratrum utilitatem vel commodum huiusmodi fiant relicta aut in eos taliter data vel donata procedant seu quod in morte vel ab infirmis huiusmodi dandum vel donandum fratribus ipsis exsisteret in eorundem dantium vel donantium sanitate sibi dari vel donari procurent."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p161","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","In which things to be avoided by them we intend to burden their consciences, so that if—which may be absent—it shall happen that anything be done by the same brothers deceitfully or fraudulently in this matter, besides that we wish them to be held thereby to the said priests, rectors, and curates, even a strict account shall be required of them at the examination of the last judgment.","In quibus per ipsos vitandis eorum intendimus conscientias onerare ut si quod absit per fratres ipsos dolo vel fraude quicquam in hac parte agi fortasse contigerit praeter id quod eos propterea dictis sacerdotibus rectoribus et curatis teneri volumus etiam districta ratio in extremi iudicii examine requiratur ab eis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p162","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","Beyond such portion, however, let the aforesaid parochial rectors, curates, and prelates be able to exact nothing, nor are the said brothers bound to spend more to them, nor may they be coerced to it by anyone in any way.","Ultra portionem autem huiusmodi nihil valeant parochiales rectores curati et praelati exigere supra dicti neque illis dicti fratres amplius impendere sint adstricti neque ad id a quoquam possint aliqualiter coerceri."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p163","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","For we, so that in all things equally and peacefully, the Lord favoring, it may proceed, all privileges, graces, indulgences, by word or writing, under whatever form or expression or conception of words, granted by us or our predecessors the Roman pontiffs to whichever of the aforesaid orders, and also customs, conventions, statutes, and pacts, insofar as they are contrary to the premises or to any of the premises, we utterly revoke, annul, quash, and make void; nay more, we announce and decree them as void, annulled, and quashed, to be of no firmness whatsoever.","Nos etenim ut in cunctis aequaliter et pacifice favente domino procedatur universa privilegia gratias indulgentias verbo seu scripto sub quacunque forma vel expressione seu conceptione verborum a nobis vel praedecessoribus nostris Romanis pontificibus cuicunque ordinum praedictorum concessa nec non consuetudines conventiones statuta et pacta in quantum sunt praemissis vel alicui praemissorum contraria ea penitus revocamus vacuamus cassamus et irritamus quin immo cassa vacua et irrita nunciamus et decernimus nullius prorsus exsistere firmitatis."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p164","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","Moreover, we ask and exhort attentively by the tenor of the presents all prelates of churches, of whatever pre-eminence, state, or dignity they may exist, and the aforesaid parochial priests and curates or rectors, and nonetheless we command them by strictly enjoining, that for the reverence of God and of the apostolic see, having the aforesaid orders and their professors commended with benevolent affection, they take care to show themselves to the same brothers not difficult, grave, harsh, or hard, but rather favorable, propitious, and benign, and liberal with pious munificence; thus may they receive them with prompt benignity in the office of preaching and settings forth of the word of God and in all other things aforesaid as suitable co-workers with them and participants of their labors, and not omit affectionately to admit them, so that therefore for them the reward of eternal blessedness may be increased and the happy increments of the salvation of souls may be procured.","Ceterum universos ecclesiarum praelatos cuiuscunque praeeminentiae status vel dignitatis exsistant ac sacerdotes parochiales et curatos sive rectores praedictos praesentium tenore rogamus et hortamur attente nihilominus que eis districte praecipiendo mandamus quatenus pro divina et apostolicae sedis reverentia praedictos ordines et professores eorum habentes affectu benevolo commendatos fratribus ipsis non se difficiles graves duros aut asperos sed potius favorabiles propitios ac benignos pia que munificentia liberales se studeant exhibere sic eos in praedicationis officio et propositionibus verbi dei ac in omnibus aliis supra dictis tanquam cooperatores eorum idoneos et laborum suorum participes prompta benignitate recipiant ac affectuose admittere non omittant ut proinde illis aeternae beatitudinis praemium augeatur et animarum salutis incrementa felicia procurentur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p165","Boniface Viii: Super Cat Hedram","Nor let them be ignorant that if it shall happen to be done otherwise by them in this matter, the benignity of the apostolic see, which pursues the same orders and professors with bountiful favor and bears them in the bowels of charity, would not undeservedly be troubled against them, nor could it suffer it equanimously but would apply the remedy of opportune provision concerning this, and that the indignation of the heavenly Prince, who renders worthy things according to merits, whose services the sedulous care of the same brothers pursues, would by no means pass over them.","Nec ipsos lateat quod si secus ab eis agi fortasse contigerit in hac parte apostolicae sedis benignitas quae ordines et professores eosdem ubere favore prosequitur et gerit in visceribus caritatis contra eos non immerito turbaretur nec eadem aequanimiter pati posset quin super hoc provisionis opportunae remedium adhiberet ipsos que nihilominus coelestis indignatio principis digna pro meritis rependentis cuius obsequia fratrum ipsorum sedulitas curiosa prosequitur minime praeteriret."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p166","Exivi de Paradiso","\"I came forth from paradise,\" said that heavenly farmer, \"I said, I will water the garden of plantations,\" who truly is the fountain of wisdom, the Word of God abiding in the Father, begotten from the Father from eternity. Finally in these days, the Holy Spirit fashioning in the womb of the Virgin, He went forth made flesh, a man, to accomplish the arduous work of the redemption of the human race, giving Himself as an example to men by offering Himself as a pattern of heavenly life.","Exivi de paradiso dixi rigabo hortum plantationum ait ille coelestis agricola qui vere fons sapientiae verbum dei a patre in patre manens genitum ab aeterno novissime diebus istis fabricante spiritu in utero virginis Caro factum exivit homo ad opus arduum redemptionis humani generis peragendum exemplar se dando coelestis vitae praebens hominibus semetipsum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p167","Exivi de Paradiso","But because man, pressed down by the cares of mortal life, often turned the gaze of his mind away from the contemplation of such a pattern, our true Solomon, upon the throne of the militant church, made a certain garden of delight, among others, extended far from the stormy waves of the world, in which more quietly and more securely there might be leisure for contemplating and observing works of this kind of the pattern. He Himself entered into this world to water it with the fruitful waters of spiritual grace and doctrine.","Verum quia plerumque mortalis vitae sollicitudinibus pressus homo mentis adspectum ab exemplaris huiusmodi intuitu divertebat verus noster Salomon in solio militantis ecclesiae hortum voluptatis inter ceteros quendam fecit a procellosis mundi fluctibus elongatum in quo quietius ac securius vacaretur contemplandis servandis que huiusmodi operibus exemplaris in hunc mundum introivit ipse ut rigaret ipsum foecundis aquis spiritualis gratiae et doctrinae."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p168","Exivi de Paradiso","This garden, indeed, is the holy religion of the Friars Minor, which, firmly enclosed on every side with the walls of regular observance, within itself alone content with God, is abundantly adorned with the new plantations of sons.","Hic hortus siquidem est fratrum minorum sancta religio quae muris regularis observantiae firmiter undique circumclusa intra se solo contenta deo adornatur abunde novellis plantationibus filiorum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p169","Exivi de Paradiso","Coming to this garden, the beloved Son of God reaps the myrrh of mortifying penance with the spices, which surround all with wonderful sweetness the odor of attracting holiness.","Ad hunc veniens dilectus dei filius mortificantis paenitentiae myrrham metit cum aromatibus quae suavitate mira universis odorem attrahentis sanctimoniae circumfundunt."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p170","Exivi de Paradiso","This is that form and rule of heavenly life which that eminent confessor of Christ, holy Francis, described and taught to be observed by his sons equally by word and example.","Haec est illa coelestis vitae forma et regula quam descripsit ille confessor Christi eximius sanctus Franciscus ac servandam a suis filiis verbo docuit pariter et exemplo."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p171","Exivi de Paradiso","Because, however, the devoted professors and emulators of the said holy rule, as pupils and true sons of so great a father, aspired—just as they fervently aspire—firmly to observe the aforesaid rule purely and fully, attending to certain things which were able to bring doubtful meaning which are contained in the series of the rule itself, they prudently had recourse for their declaration long ago to the apex of apostolic dignity, so that, certified by it—to whose feet also through the rule itself they are subject—they might be able, all doubts having been expelled, to serve the Lord with full charity of conscience.","Quia vero dictae sanctae regulae professores ac aemulatores devoti ut et alumni et veri filii tanti patris affectabant sicut et ferventer affectant ad purum et ad plenum praemissam regulam firmiter observare attendentes quaedam quae dubium poterant afferre sensum in ipsius regulae serie contineri pro ipsorum declaratione habenda recurrerunt prudenter olim ad apicem apostolicae dignitatis ut certificati per ipsam cuius pedibus etiam per ipsam regulam sunt subiecti possent domino pulsis cunctis dubiis cum plena caritate conscientiae deservire."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p172","Exivi de Paradiso","To their pious and just supplications, several of our predecessors, the Roman pontiffs, successively, as was worthy, applying ear and mind, declared those things which seemed doubtful, issued some things, and granted some things as seemed expedient for the consciences of the brothers and the state of pure observance.","Horum autem piis ac iustis supplicationibus plures praedecessores nostri Romani pontifices successive sicut dignum erat applicantes aures et animum declaraverunt ea quae dubia videbantur ediderunt nonnulla et aliqua concesserunt sicut expedire videbant fratrum conscientiis ac purae observantiae status."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p173","Exivi de Paradiso","But because often where there is no fault, timorous consciences are accustomed to fear it—which dread any deviation in the way of God—the consciences of all the brothers were not fully quieted from the said declarations of the said [pontiffs], so that concerning some things pertaining to the rule and their state, some waves of doubts are generated and arise, just as has been reported to our ears many times and concerning very many things in public and private consistories.","Verum quia plerumque ubi culpa non est eam timere solent conscientiae timoratae quae in via dei quodcunque devium expavescunt non sunt ad plenum ex dictis declarationibus dictorum omnium fratrum conscientiae quietatae quin circa aliqua ad regulam ipsorum que statum pertinentia dubitationum in ipsis fluctus aliqui generentur et oriantur sicut ad aures nostras pluries et de quampluribus in publicis et privatis consistoriis est perlatum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p174","Exivi de Paradiso","Wherefore, it was humbly supplicated to us by the same brothers that we would take care to apply the opportune remedies of declaration to the aforesaid doubts which have occurred and which may occur in the future, out of the benignity of the apostolic see.","Quapropter per ipsos fratres nobis exstitit humiliter supplicatum quatenus praedictis dubiis quae occurrerunt et quae possunt occurrere in futurum adhibere opportuna declarationis remedia de benignitate sedis apostolicae curaremus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p175","Exivi de Paradiso","We, therefore, whose mind from tender age boiled with pious devotion to such professors of the rule and to the whole order itself—now, however, from the common care of pastoral governance which we sustain undeservedly, we are the more ardently provoked to foster them more sweetly and more attentively to be pursued with gracious favors, the more frequently we revolve with intent mind the abundant fruits which we continually see coming forth from their exemplary life and salutary doctrine to the whole universal church—moved by such pious intention of the supplicants, we have deemed to convert our studies to diligently carrying out what is sought, and we caused the same doubts to be diligently examined by several archbishops and bishops and masters in theology and other learned, prudent, and discreet men.","Nos igitur cuius animus ab aetate tenera pia devotione efferbuit ad huiusmodi professores regulae et ad ordinem ipsum totum nunc autem ex communi cura pastoralis regiminis quam immeriti sustinemus ad ipsos fovendos dulcius et attentius gratiosis favoribus prosequendos tanto provocamur ardentius quanto frequentius intenta mente revolvimus fructus uberes quos ex eorum exemplari vita et salutari doctrina toti universali ecclesiae continue cernimus provenire tam pia supplicantium intentione commoti ad peragendum diligenter quod petitur studia nostra duximus convertenda ipsa que dubia per plures archiepiscopos et episcopos et in theologia magistros et alios literatos providos et discretos examinari fecimus diligenter."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p176","First Doubt: Concerning the Obligation of the Evangelical Counsels","Since, therefore, in the first place from what is had at the beginning of the said rule: \"The rule and life of the Friars Minor is this: namely, to observe the holy gospel of our Lord Jesus Christ, living in obedience, without property, and in chastity\"—likewise, at the end of the approved year of probation, \"let them be received to obedience, promising always to observe this life and rule\"; likewise, near the end of the rule: \"poverty and humility and the holy gospel of our Lord Jesus Christ, which we have firmly promised to observe\"—it was doubted whether the brothers of the same order are bound by the profession of their rule to all things, both precepts and counsels, of the gospel, some saying that they are bound to all things, others on the contrary asserting that they are bound only to those three counsels, namely, to live in obedience, in chastity, and without property, and to those things which are placed in the rule under obligatory words—we, concerning this article, following in the footsteps of our predecessors and prosecuting the same article as to something more clearly, decreed to answer to the said doubt that, since the determined vow of anyone ought to fall under a certain [object], the one vowing the rule cannot be said to be bound by the force of such a vow to those evangelical counsels which are not placed in the rule.","Cum igitur in primis ex eo quod in dictae regulae principio habetur: regula et vita fratrum minorum haec est scilicet domini nostri Iesu Christi sanctum evangelium observare in oboedientia vivendo sine proprio et in castitate item infra finito vero anno probationis recipiantur ad oboedientiam promittentes vitam istam semper et regulam observare item circa finem regulae paupertatem et humilitatem et sanctum evangelium domini nostri Iesu Christi quod firmiter promisimus observemus fuit haesitatum an fratres eiusdem ordinis ad omnia tam praecepta quam consilia evangelii ex professione suae regulae teneantur quibusdam dicentibus quod ad omnia aliis autem asserentibus quod ad sola illa tria consilia videlicet vivere in oboedientia in castitate et sine proprio et ad ea quae sub verbis obligatoriis ponuntur in regula obligantur nos circa hunc articulum praedecessorum nostrorum vestigiis inhaerentes ipsum que articulum quoad aliquid clarius prosequentes dictae haesitationi duximus respondendum quod cum votum determinatum cuiuslibet habeat cadere sub certo vovens regulam non potest dici teneri ex vi voti huiusmodi ad ea consilia evangelica quae in regula non ponuntur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p177","First Doubt: Concerning the Obligation of the Evangelical Counsels","And indeed, the intention of blessed Francis, the founder of the rule, is proved to have been this from the fact that he placed certain evangelical counsels in the rule, others having been omitted. For if through that word: \"The rule and life of the Friars Minor is this,\" etc., he had intended to bind them to all the evangelical counsels, he would have superfluously and nugatorily expressed some of them in the rule, the others having been suppressed.","Et quidem beati Francisci conditoris regulae haec probatur fuisse intentio ex hoc quod quaedam evangelica consilia in regula posuit aliis praetermissis. Si enim per illud verbum: regula et vita fratrum minorum haec est et cetera intendisset eos ad omnia consilia evangelica obligare superflue et nugatorie quaedam eorum suppressis ceteris in regula expressisset."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p178","First Doubt: Concerning the Obligation of the Evangelical Counsels","Since, however, the nature of a restrictive term has this, that it thus excludes extraneous things from itself that it concludes all things pertaining to itself, we declare and say that the said brothers are bound not only to those three vows, taken nakedly and absolutely, by the profession of their rule, but also are held to fulfill all those things which are pertinent to these three aforesaid which the rule itself places.","Cum autem natura termini restrictivi hoc habeat quod sic excludit ab ipso extranea quod cuncta ad ipsum pertinentia concludit declaramus et dicimus quod dicti fratres non solum ad illa tria vota nude et absolute accepta ex professione suae regulae obligantur sed etiam tenentur ad ea omnia implenda quae sunt pertinentia ad haec tria praedicta quae regula ipsa ponit."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p179","First Doubt: Concerning the Obligation of the Evangelical Counsels","For if they were bound precisely and nakedly only to these three aforesaid, promising to keep the rule by living in obedience, chastity, and without property, and not also to all things contained in the rule which modify these three, the words \"I promise always to observe this rule\" would be said for nothing and in vain, since from these words no obligation would arise.","Nam si ad haec tria praedicta tantum praecise et nude promittentes se servare regulam vivendo in oboedientia castitate et sine proprio et non etiam ad omnia contenta in regula quae haec tria modificant arctarentur pro nihilo et vane proferrentur haec verba: promitto semper hanc regulam observare ex quo ex his verbis nulla obligatio nasceretur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p180","First Doubt: Concerning the Obligation of the Evangelical Counsels","Nor, however, is it to be thought that blessed Francis intended that the professors of this rule be equally bound as to all things contained in the rule modifying the three vows or to other things expressed in it; rather, he openly distinguished that as to some of them the transgression is mortal by force of the word, and as to some other things not, since to some of them he applies the word of precept or its equivalent, and as to some he is content with other words.","Nec tamen putandum est quod beatus Franciscus professores huius regulae quantum ad omnia contenta in regula modificantia tria vota seu ad alia in ipsa expressa intenderit aequaliter esse obligatos quin potius aperte discrevit quod quoad quaedam ipsorum ex vi verbi transgressio est mortalis et quoad quaedam alia non cum ad quaedam ipsorum verbum apponat praecepti vel aequipollentis eidem et quoad aliqua verbis aliis sit contentus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p181","Second Doubt: Concerning Things Having the Force of Precept","Likewise, because besides those things which are placed in the rule under the word of precept or exhortation or admonition, some things are inserted with the word of the imperative mode attached negatively or affirmatively, it has been doubted up to now whether they are held to those things as to things having the force of precept; and because, as we have understood, this doubt is not diminished but increased by this, that our predecessor Pope Nicholas III of happy memory is known to have declared that the same brothers are bound by the profession of their rule to those evangelical counsels which are expressed in the rule itself preceptively or prohibitively or under equivalent words, and nonetheless the aforesaid brothers have supplicated that we, for the sake of preserving their consciences, would deign to declare which of these things are to be regarded as equivalent to precepts and obligatory.","Item quia praeter ea quae expresse verbo praecepti ac exhortationis seu monitionis ponuntur in regula nonnulla verbo imperativi modi negative vel affirmative apposito inseruntur hactenus exstitit dubitatum an tenerentur ad ista ut ad habentia vim praecepti et quia ut intelleximus non minuitur hoc dubium sed augetur ex eo quod felicis recordationis Nicolaus papa iii praedecessor noster noscitur declarasse quod fratres ipsi ex professione suae regulae sunt adstricti ad ea consilia evangelica quae in ipsa regula praeceptorie vel inhibitorie seu sub verbis aequipollentibus exprimuntur et nihilominus ad eorum omnium observantiam quae ipsis in eadem regula sub verbis obligatoriis inducuntur supplicaverunt praedicti fratres ut ad ipsorum conscientias servandas declarare quae horum censeri debeant praeceptis aequipollentia ac obligatoria dignaremur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p182","Second Doubt: Concerning Things Having the Force of Precept","We, therefore, who delight in the sincere consciences of these persons, attending that in those things which look to the salvation of the soul, the safer part is to be held for avoiding grave remorse of conscience, say that although the brothers are not bound to the observance of all things which are placed in the rule under words of the imperative mode as to precepts or things equivalent to precepts, yet it is expedient for the same brothers for preserving the purity and rigor of the rule that they know themselves to be obliged to those things as to things equivalent to precepts which are noted below.","Nos itaque qui in sinceris horum conscientiis delectamur attendentes quod in his quae animae salutem respiciunt ad vitandos graves remorsus conscientiae pars securior est tenenda dicimus quod licet fratres non ad omnium quae sub verbis imperativi modi ponuntur in regula sicut ad praeceptorum seu praeceptis aequipollentium observantiam teneantur expedit tamen ipsis fratribus ad observandam puritatem regulae et rigorem quod ad ea sicut ad aequipollentia praeceptis se noverint obligatos quae hic inferius adnotantur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p183","Second Doubt: Concerning Things Having the Force of Precept","So that these things which can be seen as equivalent to precepts may be had in summary, by force of the word or at least by reason of the matter concerning which it is treated or from both, we declare that that which is placed in the rule concerning not having more tunics than one with a hood and another without a hood; likewise concerning not wearing shoes and concerning not riding except in case of necessity; likewise that the brothers clothe themselves with vile garments; likewise that they are held to fast from the feast of All Saints until the Nativity of the Lord and on the sixth ferias; likewise that clerics do the divine office according to the order of the holy Roman church; likewise that the ministers and custodians bear solicitous care for the necessities of the sick and for clothing the brothers; likewise that if any brother falls into infirmity, the other brothers ought to serve him; likewise that the brothers do not preach in the bishopric of any bishop when it has been contradicted by him; likewise that no one dare preach to the people at all unless he has been examined, approved, and instituted for this by the general minister or by others to whom it pertains according to the aforesaid declaration; likewise that brothers who would know themselves unable specially to observe the aforesaid rule ought and are able to have recourse to their ministers; likewise all things which are placed in the rule concerning the form of the habit both of novices and also of professors, and concerning the mode of reception and profession—unless it seems otherwise to the receivers concerning the habit of novices according to God as the rule says—these things, I say, all are to be observed by the brothers as obligatory.","Ut autem haec quae videri possunt aequipollentia praeceptis ex vi verbi vel saltem ratione materiae de qua agitur seu ex utroque sub compendio habeantur declaramus quod illud quod ponitur in regula de non habendo plures tunicas quam unam cum caputio et aliam sine caputio item de non portandis calceamentis et de non equitando extra casum necessitatis item quod fratres vilibus induantur item quod ieiunare a festo omnium sanctorum usque ad natale domini et in sextis feriis teneantur item quod clerici faciant divinum officium secundum ordinem sanctae Romanae ecclesiae item quod ministri et custodes pro necessitatibus infirmorum et fratribus induendis sollicitam curam gerant item quod si quis fratrum in infirmitatem ceciderit alii fratres debent ei servire item quod fratres non praedicent in episcopatu alicuius episcopi cum ab eo fuerit contradictum item quod nullus audeat penitus populo praedicare nisi a generali ministro vel aliis quibus secundum declarationem praedictam id competit fuerit examinatus approbatus et ad hoc institutus item quod fratres qui cognoscerent se non posse praemissam regulam specialiter observare debeant et possint ad suos ministros recurrere item quod omnia quae ponuntur in regula ad formam habitus tam novitiorum quam etiam professorum nec non ad receptionis modum ac professionem spectantia nisi recipientibus quoad habitum novitiorum sicut dicit regula secundum deum aliter videatur haec inquam omnia sunt a fratribus tanquam obligatoria observanda."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p184","Second Doubt: Concerning Things Having the Force of Precept","Likewise, the order commonly has sensed and holds and has held from ancient times that wherever this word \"teneantur\" (they are held) is placed in the rule, it obtains the force of precept and ought to be observed by the brothers as such.","Item ordo communiter sensit tenet et tenuit ab antiquo quod ubicunque ponitur in regula hoc vocabulum teneantur obtinet vim praecepti et observari debet a fratribus sicut tale."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p185","Third Doubt: Concerning the Goods of Those Entering","Moreover, because the aforesaid confessor of Christ, giving the mode of doing and observing concerning those to be received into the order, said in the rule: \"Let the brothers and their ministers be careful that they be not solicitous concerning their temporal goods, so that they may freely do with them whatever shall have been inspired to them by the Lord. However, let the ministers themselves have license to send them to some God-fearing persons, if counsel be required, by whose counsel their goods be distributed to the poor\"—many of the brothers have doubted and do doubt whether it is lawful for them to receive anything of the goods of those entering if it be given, and whether they may be able to induce them without fault to give to the persons and convents, and also whether the ministers or brothers ought to give counsel concerning the distribution of such things when suitable persons other than those from among themselves to whom those about to enter are sent can be found to counsel.","Ceterum quia Christi confessor praedictus agendorum ac servandorum circa recipiendos ad ordinem ministris et fratribus modum praebens dixit in regula quod caveant fratres et eorum ministri ne sint solliciti de rebus suis temporalibus ut libere faciant de eis quicquid ipsis a domino fuerit inspiratum licentiam tamen habeant ipsi ministri mittendi eos ad aliquos deum timentes si consilium requiratur quorum consilio sua bona pauperibus erogentur dubitaverunt et dubitant multi fratrum an liceat ipsis de bonis ingredientium quicquam recipere si donetur et si ad dandum personis et conventibus possint eos inducere sine culpa si etiam ad disponendum de distributione rerum talium debeant ipsi ministri seu fratres dare consilium ubi ad consulendum alii quam ex ipsis ad quos ingressuri mittantur possint idonei inveniri."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p186","Third Doubt: Concerning the Goods of Those Entering","We, however, considering attentively that holy Francis intended his professors of the rule, whom he had founded in the greatest poverty, by the said words to be specially and totally lengthened away from the affection of temporal things of those entering, so that, as far as it pertains to the brothers, the reception into the order might appear holy and most pure, and that they might by no means seem to have an eye to their temporal goods but only to themselves to be handed over to divine service, we say that both ministers and the other brothers ought henceforth to abstain from the said inductions to giving to them and from suasion, and also from giving counsel concerning distribution, since for this they ought to be sent to God-fearing persons of another state, not to the brothers, so that they may truly appear to all to be zealous, studious, and perfect cultivators of such a salutary paternal institute.","Nos autem considerantes attente intendisse sanctum Franciscum suae regulae professores quos fundaverat in maxima paupertate ab affectu temporalium rerum ipsorum ingredientium per dicta verba specialiter et totaliter elongare ut quantum est ex parte fratrum ipsorum receptio ad ordinem sancta et purissima appareret et ne aliquo modo oculum viderentur habere ad bona eorum temporalia sed ad ipsos tantum divino servitio mancipandos dicimus de cetero debere tam ministros quam fratres ceteros a dictis inductionibus ad sibi dandum et suasionibus nec non et dandis circa distributionem consiliis abstinere cum per hoc ad timentes deum status alterius mitti debeant non ad fratres ut vere cunctis pateant esse tam salubris instituti paterni studiosi zelatores seduli et perfecti."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p187","Third Doubt: Concerning the Goods of Those Entering","Since, however, the rule itself wishes that those entering be free to do with their goods what the Lord will inspire, it does not seem that it is not lawful for them to receive, their necessities and the moderations of the aforesaid declaration having been considered, if the one entering wishes to give freely anything of his goods, just as to other poor persons, by way of alms.","Cum vero facere de rebus suis quod dominus inspirabit ipsamet regula ingredientibus liberum velit esse non videtur quin liceat eis recipere consideratis scilicet eorum necessitatibus et moderationibus declarationis iam dictae si quid de bonis suis intrans sicut et ceteris pauperibus per modum eleemosynae libere velit dare."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p188","Third Doubt: Concerning the Goods of Those Entering","Yet it is fitting that the brothers be careful in accepting such oblations lest from the notable quantity of things received a sinister eye be able to be presumed against them.","Cavere tamen in acceptatione oblatorum talium decet fratres ne ex receptorum quantitate notabili praesumi possit sinister oculus contra ipsos."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p189","Fourth Doubt: Concerning the Number of Tunics and the Meanness of Garments","Moreover, since it is said in the rule: \"Those who have already promised obedience shall have one tunic with a hood and another without a hood, if they wish to have it\"; likewise: \"All the brothers shall clothe themselves with vile garments\"; and we have declared that the aforesaid words are equivalent to precepts, wishing these things to be more fully determined, we say concerning the number of tunics that it is not lawful to use more except in necessities which can be had from the rule, according to what the said predecessor of ours more fully declared concerning this passage.","Praeterea cum dicatur in regula quod illi qui iam promiserunt oboedientiam habeant unam tunicam cum caputio et aliam sine caputio qui habere voluerint item quod fratres omnes vestimentis vilibus induantur nos que praedicta verba declaraverimus aequipollere praeceptis volentes haec determinari plenius dicimus quantum ad numerum tunicarum quod pluribus uti non licet nisi in necessitatibus quae haberi possunt ex regula secundum quod hunc passum memoratus praedecessor noster plenius declaravit."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p190","Fourth Doubt: Concerning the Number of Tunics and the Meanness of Garments","The meanness of garments, both of the habit and of the inner tunics, we say ought to be understood as that which, according to the custom or condition of the region, ought deservedly to be reputed as meanness concerning the color of the cloth and the price. For one determined mode cannot be assigned concerning such things with respect to all regions.","Vilitatem autem vestium tam habitus quam interiorum tunicarum illam intelligi debere dicimus quae secundum consuetudinem vel conditionem patriae debeat quantum ad colorem panni et pretium vilitatis merito reputari. Non enim quoad regiones omnes potest determinatus unus modus in talibus assignari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p191","Fourth Doubt: Concerning the Number of Tunics and the Meanness of Garments","We have deemed to commit the judgment of such meanness to the ministers and custodians or guardians, burdening their consciences concerning this, yet in such a way that they preserve meanness in garments.","Huiusmodi etiam vilitatis iudicium ministris et custodibus seu guardianis duximus committendum eorum super hoc conscientias onerantes ita tamen quod servent in vestibus vilitatem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p192","Fourth Doubt: Concerning the Number of Tunics and the Meanness of Garments","We also leave to the judgment of the same ministers, custodians, and guardians, in the same manner, for what necessity the same brothers may be able to wear shoes.","Quorum etiam ministrorum custodum et guardianorum iudicio eodem modo relinquimus pro qua necessitate possint ipsi fratres calceamenta portare."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p193","Fifth Doubt: Concerning Fasting","Next, since in the rule there are inserted two times, namely from the feast of All Saints until the Nativity of the Lord and especially the season of Lent, in which they are held to fast, and in the same rule it is inserted that at other times they are not held to fast except on the sixth feria, and from this some wished to say that the brothers of the said order are not held except out of congruity to other fasts beyond these, we declare that it ought to be understood that they are not held to fasting at other times except in the fasts established by the church. For it is not likely that either the institutor of the rule or even the confirmer intended to absolve them from observing those fasts to which other Christians are bound by the common statute of the church.","Deinde cum duobus temporibus adnotatis in regula scilicet a festo omnium sanctorum usque ad nativitatem domini et maxime quadragesimae in quibus ieiunare tenentur inseratur in eadem regula aliis autem temporibus non teneantur nisi sexta feria ieiunare et ex hoc voluerunt aliqui dicere quod dicti ordinis fratres non tenentur nisi ex condecentia ad alia ieiunia quam ad ista declaramus debere intelligi eos non teneri ad ieiunium aliis temporibus praeterquam in ieiuniis ab ecclesia constitutis. Non est enim verisimile quod vel institutor regulae vel etiam confirmator absolvere ipsos intenderit a servandis illis ieiuniis ad quae de communi statuto ecclesiae obligantur ceteri christiani."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p194","Sixth Doubt: Concerning the Reception of Money","Moreover, since the said saint, wishing his brothers to be totally alien from denarii or money above all things, firmly commanded all the brothers that they by no means receive denarii or money through themselves or through an interposed person; and our same predecessor, declaring this article, set down the cases and modes in which, if observed, the brothers cannot and ought not to be said to be receivers of money against the rule or the purity of their order—we say that the brothers are bound to be extremely careful that for other causes and under other modes than those placed by the declaration of our said predecessor, they do not have recourse to those giving money or to deputed nuncios, lest if something else be attempted by them, they may deservedly be able to be called transgressors of the precept and the rule. For where something is generally prohibited to anyone which is not expressly conceded, it is understood to be denied.","Porro cum dictus sanctus volens fratres suos super omnia a denariis seu pecunia esse totaliter alienos praecepit firmiter fratribus universis ut nullo modo denarios vel pecuniam recipiant per se vel per interpositam personam istum que articulum declarans idem praedecessor noster casus et modos posuerit quibus servatis a fratribus non possunt dici nec debent per se vel per alium pecuniae receptatores contra regulam vel sui ordinis puritatem dicimus fratres teneri cavere summopere quod pro aliis causis et sub modis aliis quam ponat dicti praedecessoris nostri declaratio ad dantes pecunias sive deputatos nuncios non recurrant ne si secus ab ipsis attentatum fuerit transgressores praecepti et regulae merito possint dici. Nam ubi aliquid alicui generaliter prohibetur quod expresse non conceditur intelligitur denegatum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p195","Sixth Doubt: Concerning the Reception of Money","Wherefore, every gain of money, and the reception of oblations of money in church or elsewhere, and chests or trunks arranged for putting the offerings or donations of money, and also any other recourse to monies or those having them which is not conceded by the aforesaid declaration—these things, I say, are all simply forbidden to them.","Quocirca quaestus omnis pecuniae ac oblationum pecuniarum receptio in ecclesia vel alibi cippi vel trunci ordinati ad offerentium seu donantium pecunias reponendas nec non et quicunque recursus alius ad pecunias seu habentes ipsas qui per declarationem praedictam non conceditur haec inquam omnia sunt eis simpliciter interdicta."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p196","Sixth Doubt: Concerning the Reception of Money","Although, moreover, recourse to special friends is expressly conceded according to the rule only in two cases, namely for the necessities of the sick and for clothing the brothers, and our oft-said predecessor deemed it to be extended for that same thing, piously and reasonably, the necessity of life having been considered, to other necessities of the brothers occurring for the time, alms failing or also pressing—let the aforesaid brothers attend that for no other causes than the aforesaid or similar ones in the way or elsewhere is it lawful for them to have recourse to such friends, whether they be those giving money or deputed by them or nuncios or depositaries or called by any other name, even if the modes concerning money conceded by the same declaration be integrally preserved.","Cum etiam recursus ad amicos speciales expresse tantum in duobus casibus secundum regulam concedatur videlicet pro necessitatibus infirmorum et fratribus induendis id que pie et rationabiliter considerata necessitate vitae ad alias necessitates fratrum pro tempore occurrentes cessantibus eleemosynis seu etiam ingruentes saepe dictus praedecessor noster duxerit extendendum attendant fratres praefati quod pro nullis causis aliis quam praedictis vel similibus in via vel alibi recurrere licet eis ad amicos huiusmodi sive sint dantes pecunias seu deputati per ipsos sive nuncii vel depositarii seu alio quovis nomine appellentur etiamsi concessi per eandem declarationem modi circa pecuniam integre servarentur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p197","Sixth Doubt: Concerning the Reception of Money","Finally, since the same confessor supremely aspired that the professors of his rule be totally abstracted from the affection and desire of earthly things, and especially wholly inexperienced from money and its use, as the prohibition concerning receiving money, often repeated in the rule, proves—it is necessary that the brothers carefully take care that when they have to have recourse for the aforesaid causes and modes to those having money deputed for their necessities—to those holding it, whoever these may be, principals or nuncios—they so conduct themselves in all things that they show to all that they have nothing at all in the said monies, just as they do not have [them].","Denique cum idem confessor summe affectaverit suae regulae professores totaliter esse abstractos ab affectu et desiderio terrenorum et specialiter a pecunia et eius usu totaliter inexpertos sicut probat prohibitio de recipienda pecunia in regula saepius repetita curare fratres vigilanter necesse est quod cum e causis praedictis et modis ad habentes pecunias deputatas pro ipsorum necessitatibus recurrere oportebit ad tenentes ipsas quicunque hi fuerint principales vel nuncii in omnibus sic se gerant quod se cunctis ostendant in dictis pecuniis sicut nec habent penitus nil habere."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p198","Sixth Doubt: Concerning the Reception of Money","Wherefore, concerning prescribing how and to what extent the money be spent, and demanding an account of the expense, and in any way reclaiming it, and depositing or causing to be deposited a money box or its key—let the brothers know that these acts and similar ones are unlawful for them. For the aforesaid acts pertain only to the lords who gave [the money] and to those whom they have deputed for this same thing.","Quapropter praecipere quod et qualiter pecunia expendatur computum que exigere de expensa eam quomodocunque repetere sive deponere aut deponi facere capsulam pecuniae vel eius clavem deferre hos actus et consimiles sibi fratres illicitos esse sciant. Praedicta enim facere ad solos dominos pertinet qui dederunt et eos quos ipsi deputaverunt ad hoc ipsum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p199","Doubts Concerning Practices Apparently Contrary to the Vow of Poverty","Hence, since the holy man, expressing the mode of the aforesaid poverty in the rule, said in the same: \"Let the brothers appropriate nothing to themselves, neither a house nor a place nor any thing; but as pilgrims and strangers in this age, serving the Lord in poverty and humility, let them go for alms confidently\"; and it was declared by several of our predecessors, the Roman pontiffs, that this expropriation ought to be understood both in special and also in common, on account of which they received into themselves the ownership and dominion of all things granted, offered, and donated to the brothers, which and the use of which, namely of the fact having been made to the order or to the brothers themselves, it is lawful for them to have; but the use of the fact alone was left to the same brothers—there were brought to our examination the things which were said and seen to be done in the order, which were said to be repugnant to the aforesaid vow and purity of the order; namely, that they not only endure but procure themselves to be instituted as heirs; likewise that they receive annual rents, sometimes in such notable quantity that the convents having them live entirely from them; likewise that when their negotiations, even concerning temporal things, are agitated in courts, they assist advocates and proctors and present themselves personally there for instigating them; likewise that they undertake the executions of last wills and conduct themselves, and sometimes intermeddle concerning the dispositions or restitutions to be made of usuries or of things badly taken away; likewise that somewhere they have not only excessive gardens but also large vineyards, from which both vegetables and wine are much gathered for selling; likewise that at the times of harvests or vintage, grain and wine are gathered by the brothers by begging or buying elsewhere in such great quantity and stored in cellars and granaries that they can pass the remainder of the year and their life without their begging; likewise that they build or cause to be built churches or other buildings in quantity and curiosity of figure and form and notably excessive sumptuousness, so that they do not seem the dwellings of the poor but of magnates.","Proinde cum vir sanctus paupertatis praemissae in regulam modum exprimens dixerit in eadem: fratres nihil sibi approprient nec domum nec locum nec aliquam rem sed tanquam peregrini et advenae in hoc saeculo in paupertate et humilitate domino famulantes vadant pro eleemosyna confidenter sic que declaratum exstitit per nonnullos praedecessores nostros Romanos pontifices hanc expropriationem intelligi debere tam in speciali quam etiam in communi propter quod et rerum omnium concessarum oblatarum et donatarum fratribus quas et quarum usum facti scilicet ordini vel ipsis fratribus licet habere proprietatem et dominium in se et Romanam ecclesiam receperunt dimisso ipsis fratribus in eis tantummodo usu facti simplicis ad nostrum fuerunt deducta examen quae in ordine fieri dicebantur et videbantur praedicto voto et puritati ordinis repugnare videlicet ut ea prosequamur ex ipsis quae remedio credimus indigere quod se institui heredes non solum sustinent sed procurant item quod redditus annuos recipiunt interdum in tam notabili quantitate quod conventus habentes totaliter inde vivunt item quod cum ipsorum negotia etiam pro rebus temporalibus in curiis agitantur assistunt advocatis et procuratoribus et ad instigandum eosdem se ibidem personaliter repraesentant item quod exsecutiones ultimarum suscipiunt voluntatum et gerunt se que intromittunt quandoque de usurarum vel male ablatorum dispositionibus seu restitutionibus faciendis item quod alicubi non solum excessivos hortos sed etiam vineas magnas habent de quibus tam de oleribus quam de vino multum colligitur ad vendendum item quod temporibus messium vel vindemiarum sic copiose granum et vinum mendicando vel aliunde emendo colliguntur a fratribus et in cellariis et granariis reconduntur quod per anni residuum et absque eorum mendicatione possunt transigere vitam suam item quod ecclesias vel alia aedificia faciunt vel procurant fieri in quantitate et curiositate figurae et formae ac sumptuositate notabiliter excessiva sic quod non videntur habitacula pauperum sed magnatum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p200","Doubts Concerning Practices Apparently Contrary to the Vow of Poverty","They also have in very many places so many and so notably precious ecclesiastical paraments that they exceed in these things great cathedral churches.","Paramenta etiam ecclesiastica in plerisque locis tam multa habent et tam notabiliter pretiosa quod excedunt in his magnas ecclesias cathedrales."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p201","Doubts Concerning Practices Apparently Contrary to the Vow of Poverty","Moreover, they receive without distinction horses and arms offered to them in funerals.","Equos insuper et arma eis oblata in funeribus recipiunt indistincte."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p202","Doubts Concerning Practices Apparently Contrary to the Vow of Poverty","Nevertheless, the community of the brothers and especially the rectors of the same order asserted that the aforesaid things or many of them were not done in the order, and that if some are found guilty in such things they are rigidly punished, and also against such things, lest they be done, very strict statutes have been made in the order many times from ancient times.","Tamen communitas fratrum et specialiter rectores ipsius ordinis asserebant quod praedicta seu plura ex ipsis in ordine non fiebant quod et si qui reperiuntur rei in talibus rigide puniuntur nec non contra talia ne fiant sunt facta pluries ab antiquo statuta in ordine multum stricta."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p203","Doubts Concerning Practices Apparently Contrary to the Vow of Poverty","Wishing therefore to provide for the consciences of the same brothers and to remove all doubts from their hearts as much as is possible to us, we answer to the aforesaid things in the way that follows.","Cupientes igitur nos ipsorum fratrum providere conscientiis et cuncta dubia quantum possibile nobis est de ipsorum pectoribus removere ad praedicta modo qui sequitur respondemus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p204","Response Concerning Successions","For since it pertains to the truth of life that what is done outwardly represents the inward disposition and habit of the mind, the brothers who have withdrawn themselves by so great an expropriation from temporal things need to abstain from everything that would be or could be seen as contrary to such expropriation.","Cum enim ad veritatem vitae pertineat ut id quod exterius agitur interiorem mentis dispositionem et habitum repraesentet necesse habent fratres qui se expropriatione tanta a temporalibus abstraxerunt ab omni eo quod dictae expropriationi esset vel posset videri contrarium abstinere."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p205","Response Concerning Successions","Because, therefore, in successions there passes not only the use of the thing but also the dominion in time to the heirs, the aforesaid brothers, who can acquire nothing for themselves in special or even for their order in common, by declaring we say that they are by no means capable of such successions, which also from their nature extend indifferently to money and also to other movable and immovable things, the purity of their vow having been considered.","Quia igitur in successionibus transit non solum usus rei sed et dominium suo tempore in heredes fratres autem praefati nihil sibi in speciali acquirere vel eorum ordini possunt etiam in communi declarando dicimus quod successionum huiusmodi quae etiam ex sui natura indifferenter ad pecuniam et etiam ad alia mobilia et immobilia se extendunt considerata sui puritate voti nullatenus sunt capaces."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p206","Response Concerning Successions","Nor is it lawful for them to cause the value of such inheritances, or so great a part of them that it could be presumed that this be done in fraud, to be left to themselves as if under the mode and form of a legacy, or to receive such left things; rather, we simply forbid such things to be done by them.","Nec licet eis valorem hereditatum talium vel tantam earum partem quod praesumi posset hoc in fraudem fieri quasi sub modo et forma legati sibi dimitti facere vel sic dimissa recipere quin potius ista sic fieri ab ipsis simpliciter prohibemus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p207","Response Concerning Annual Rents","And since annual rents are considered among immovables by law, and to obtain such rents is repugnant to poverty and begging, there is no doubt that it is not lawful for the aforesaid brothers to receive or have any rents, just as also possessions or even their use, since it is not found conceded to them, their condition having been considered.","Cum que annui redditus inter immobilia censeantur a iure ac huiusmodi redditus obtinere paupertati et mendicitati repugnet nulla dubitatio est quod praedictis fratribus redditus quoscunque sicut et possessiones vel eorum etiam usum cum eis non reperiatur concessus recipere vel habere conditione considerata ipsorum non licet."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p208","Response Concerning Assistance in Courts","Furthermore, since not only what is known to be evil but also everything that has the appearance of evil is to be especially avoided by perfect men, from such assistances in courts and instigations when it is treated concerning things to be converted to their own benefit, it is believed likely from those things which are outwardly open, concerning which men outside are accustomed to judge, that the brothers assisting in those things are seeking something as their own—by no means ought professors of such a vow and rule to mix themselves with such courts and litigious acts, so that they may have testimony also from those who are outside, and satisfy the purity of their vow, and the scandal of neighbors be avoided.","Amplius cum non solum quod malum esse dignoscitur sed et omne quod speciem habet mali sit a viris perfectis specialiter evitandum ex talibus autem assistentiis in curiis et instigationibus cum de rebus agitur in ipsorum commoda convertendis creduntur verisimiliter ex his quae foris patent de quibus habent homines foris iudicare in ipsis rebus fratres assistentes aliquid quaerere tanquam suum nullo modo debent huiusmodi voti et regulae professores se talibus curiis et litigiosis actibus immiscere ut et testimonium habeant ab his qui foris sunt et puritati satisfaciant voti sui ac evitetur per hoc scandalum proximorum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p209","Response Concerning Executions of Wills","Moreover, since the brothers of the said order are not only alien from the reception, ownership, dominion, or use of money itself, but also from any handling of it and from it, just as our oft-said predecessor plainly said in the declaration of such rule; and since the professors of the said order cannot sue in judgment for any temporal thing—it is not lawful nor does it pertain to the aforesaid brothers; rather, considering the purity of their state, they ought to know that it is forbidden to them to expose themselves to such executions and dispositions, since these things cannot often be expedited without litigation and handling or administration of money.","Verum etiam cum dicti ordinis fratres non solum a receptione proprietate dominio sive usu ipsius pecuniae verum etiam a contrectatione qualibet ipsius et ab ea sint penitus alieni quemadmodum saepe dictus praedecessor noster in declaratione huiusmodi regulae plane dixit cum que dicti ordinis professores pro nulla re temporali possint in iudicio experiri praedictis fratribus non licet nec competit quin potius considerata sui puritate status debent sibi scire interdictum quod huiusmodi exsecutionibus et dispositionibus se exponant cum haec saepius absque litigio et contrectatione vel administratione pecuniae nequeant expediri."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p210","Response Concerning Executions of Wills","Nevertheless, in carrying out these things, giving counsel does not stand against their state, since from this no jurisdiction or action in judgment or dispensation concerning temporal goods is granted to them.","Verumtamen in his exsequendis dari consilium ipsorum statui non obsistit cum ex hoc ipsis circa bona temporalia nulla iurisdictio sive actio in iudicio sive dispensatio tribuatur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p211","Response Concerning Gardens and Vineyards","Although it is not only lawful but also very suitable to reason that brothers who are sedulously occupied in spiritual labors of prayer and study have competent gardens and areas for their recollection or recreation, and sometimes for physically leading themselves after such labors, and also for having necessary garden things for themselves—nevertheless, to have gardens to be cultivated and vegetables and other garden things to be sold at a price, and also vineyards, is repugnant to their rule and the purity of the order.","Licet vero non solum sit licitum sed et multum conveniens rationi quod fratres qui in laboribus spiritualibus orationis et studii sedulo occupantur hortos et areas habeant competentes ad recollectionem vel recreationem sui et interdum ad se ipsos post labores huiusmodi corporaliter deducendos nec non ad habenda necessaria hortalitia pro se ipsis habere tamen hortos aliquos ut colantur ac olera ac alia hortalitia pretio distrahantur nec non et vineas repugnat suae regulae et ordinis puritati."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p212","Response Concerning Gardens and Vineyards","According to what the said predecessor declared and also ordained, that if such things for the next-said uses—for example, a field or a vineyard for cultivating and similar things—should be bequeathed to the brothers, by every mode the brothers abstain from the reception of such things, since even to have the premises so that the price of fruits be had in its times approaches to the nature and form of proceeds.","Secundum quod dictus praedecessor declaravit ac etiam ordinavit quod si talia ad usus proxime dictos ut puta agrum vel vineam ad colendum et consimilia fratribus legarentur per omnem modum fratres a receptione talium abstinerent cum etiam praemissa habere ut pretium fructuum suis temporibus habeatur ad naturam et formam proventuum appropinquet."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p213","Response Concerning Granaries","Again, since the aforesaid saint, both in the examples of his life and in the words of the rule, showed that he wished that his brothers and sons, leaning on divine providence, cast their thoughts upon God, who feeds the birds of heaven which do not gather into barns nor sow nor reap, it is not likely that he wished them to have granaries or cellars where they should hope to be able to pass their life with daily mendications.","Rursus cum praedictus sanctus tam in exemplis vitae quam verbis regulae ostenderit se velle quod fratres sui et filii divinae providentiae innitentes suos in deum iacerent cogitatus qui volucres coeli pascit quae non congregant in horrea nec seminant nec metunt non est verisimile voluisse ipsum eos habere granaria vel cellaria ubi quotidianis mendicationibus deberent sperare posse transigere vitam suam."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p214","Response Concerning Granaries","And therefore, they ought not to relax themselves to such congregations and conservations out of light fear, but only when it would be very credible from things already experienced that they could not otherwise find the necessities of life.","Et idcirco non ex timore levi relaxare se debent ad congregationes et conservationes huiusmodi faciendas sed tunc tantum cum esset multum credibile ex iam expertis quod non possent vitae necessaria aliter invenire."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p215","Response Concerning Granaries","We have deemed this to be left to the judgment of the ministers and custodians together and separately in their administrations and custodies, with the counsel and assent of the guardians and of two discreet priests of the convent and ancient brothers in the order, specially burdening their consciences concerning this.","Hoc autem ministrorum et custodum simul et separatim in suis administrationibus et custodiis cum guardiani et duorum de conventu loci discretorum sacerdotum et antiquorum in ordine fratrum consilio et assensu duximus iudicio relinquendum eorum super hoc specialiter conscientias onerantes."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p216","Response Concerning Buildings","Hence also it is that since the holy man wished to found his brothers in the highest poverty as to affection equally and effect—as almost the whole rule cries out—it is fitting for them that by no means henceforth cause to be built or permit to be built churches or any other buildings which, the number of the inhabiting brothers having been considered, ought to be reputed as excessive in multitude and magnitude.","Hinc est etiam quod cum vir sanctus fratres suos in paupertate summa ac humilitate fundare voluerit quoad affectum pariter et effectum sicut fere regula tota clamat convenit ipsis quod nullo modo deinceps fieri faciant vel fieri sustineant ecclesias vel alia quaecumque aedificia quae considerato fratrum inhabitantium numero excessiva in multitudine et magnitudine debeant reputari."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p217","Response Concerning Buildings","Therefore we wish that everywhere in his order henceforth they be content with temperate and humble buildings, lest what is contrary to such great promised poverty cry out outwardly to the eyes.","Ideo que volumus quod ubique in suo ordine deinceps temperatis et humilibus aedificiis sint contenti ne huic tantae paupertati promissae quod patet oculis contrarium foris clamet."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p218","Response Concerning Ecclesiastical Paraments","Although also paraments and ecclesiastical vessels are ordained for the honor of the divine name, on account of which God Himself made all things, yet He who is the knower of hidden things looks principally to the mind of those ministering to Him, not to the hand, nor does He wish to be served through those things which would be dissonant to the condition and state of His servants. For which reason, decent ecclesiastical vessels and paraments, sufficient in number and in magnitude conveniently, ought to suffice for them.","Quamvis etiam paramenta et vasa ecclesiastica ad honorem divini nominis ordinentur propter quem omnia fecit ipse deus tamen qui absconditorum est cognitor ad animum sibi ministrantium respicit principaliter non ad manum nec per illas sibi vult serviri quae suorum servitorum conditioni et statui dissonarent propter quod sufficere debent eis vasa et paramenta ecclesiastica decentia in numero et in magnitudine sufficientia competenter."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p219","Response Concerning Ecclesiastical Paraments","Superfluity, however, or excessive preciousness, or any curiosity in these or in any other things, cannot be fitting to their profession or state. For since these things savor of hoarding or abundance, to such great poverty, as to human judgment, they manifestly derogate.","Superfluitas autem aut nimia pretiositas vel quaecumque curiositas in his seu aliis quibuscunque non potest ipsorum professioni vel statui convenire. Cum enim haec sapiant thesaurizationem seu copiam paupertati tantae quoad humanum iudicium derogant manifeste."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p220","Response Concerning Ecclesiastical Paraments","Wherefore, we wish and command the premises to be observed by the brothers.","Quapropter praemissa servari a fratribus volumus et mandamus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p221","Response Concerning Offerings of Horses and Arms","Concerning the offerings of horses and arms, we decree that to be observed in all and through all which is known to have been defined in the aforesaid declaration concerning alms of money.","Circa equorum vero et armorum oblationes illud decernimus in omnibus et per omnia observandum quod per declarationem praedictam in pecuniariis noscitur eleemosynis diffinitum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p222","Doubt Concerning the Poor Use of Things","From the premises, moreover, a not small scrupulous question has arisen among the brothers, namely, whether by the profession of their rule they are bound to the strict and meager or poor use of things, some of them believing and saying that just as they have the strictest renunciation as to the dominion of things, so the greatest strictness and meagerness as to use is imposed upon them, others on the contrary asserting that by their profession they are bound to no poor use which is not expressed in the rule, although they are held to a moderate use of temperance, just as by congruity more than other Christians.","Ex praemissis autem succrevit non parum scrupulosa quaestio inter fratres videlicet utrum ex suae professione regulae obligentur ad arctum et tenuem sive pauperem usum rerum quibusdam ex ipsis credentibus et dicentibus quod sicut quoad dominium rerum habent ex voto abdicationem arctissimam ita ipsis quoad usum arctitudo maxima et exilitas est indicta aliis in contrarium asserentibus quod ex professione sua ad nullum usum pauperem qui non exprimatur in regula obligantur licet teneantur ad usum moderatum temperantiae sicut et magis ex condecenti quam ceteri christiani."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p223","Doubt Concerning the Poor Use of Things","Wishing therefore to provide for the quiet of the consciences of the aforesaid brothers and to put an end to these altercations, by declaring we say that the Friars Minor are specially bound by the profession of their rule to the strict or poor uses which are contained in their rule, and in that mode of obligation under which the rule contains or places the said uses.","Volentes itaque conscientiarum praedictorum fratrum providere quieti et his altercationibus finem dare declarando dicimus quod fratres minores ex professione suae regulae specialiter obligantur ad arctos usus seu pauperes qui in ipsorum regula continentur et eo obligationis modo sub quo continet seu ponit regula dictos usus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p224","Doubt Concerning the Poor Use of Things","To say, however, as some are reported to assert, that it is heretical to hold that the poor use is included or not included under the vow of evangelical poverty, we judge to be presumptuous and temerarious.","Dicere autem sicut aliqui asserere perhibentur quod haereticum sit tenere usum pauperem includi vel non includi sub voto evangelicae paupertatis praesumptuosum et temerarium iudicamus."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p225","Concerning the Election of Provincial Ministers","Finally, because the said rule, handing down by whom and where the election of the general minister ought to be made, makes no mention at all concerning the election or institution of provincial ministers, a doubt could arise concerning this among the brothers. We, wishing that they be able to proceed clearly and securely in all their acts, declare, and also decree and ordain in this constitution to be valid in perpetuity, that when a province is to be provided with a minister, the election of that minister resides with the provincial chapter, which the same chapter is held to make on the next day after it has been gathered. The confirmation of that election, however, pertains to the general minister.","Demum quia ex eo quod dicta regula per quos et ubi fieri debeat ministri generalis electio tradens nullam facit de ministrorum provincialium electione vel institutione penitus mentionem oriri super hoc poterat dubitatio inter fratres nos volentes posse ipsos clare ac secure procedere in omnibus factis suis declaramus statuimus etiam et ordinamus in hac constitutione in perpetuum valitura ut cum alicui provinciae de ministro fuerit providendum ipsius ministri electio penes capitulum provinciale resideat quam idem capitulum die sequenti qua fuerit congregatum facere teneatur ipsius autem electionis confirmatio ad ministrum pertineat generalem."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p226","Concerning the Election of Provincial Ministers","And if indeed it be proceeded to such election through the form of scrutiny, and it happen that, the votes being divided into diverse [parts], several elections be celebrated in discord, let that which has been celebrated by the greater part of the chapter in number—no comparison or consideration of zeal or merit having been had—be confirmed or also be invalidated by the said general minister with the counsel of discreet men of the order, a diligent examination having been held beforehand from his office as pertains to him, as shall have seemed expedient to them according to God; and if it shall have been invalidated, let such election revert to the provincial chapter.","Et si quidem ad electionem huiusmodi per formam scrutinii procedatur et votis in diversa divisis electiones plures in discordia celebrari contingat illa quae a maiori parte capituli numero nulla zeli vel meriti collatione aut consideratione habita fuerit celebrata exceptione seu contradictione quacunque alterius partis non obstante per dictum generalem ministrum de consilio discretorum de ordine prius tamen ex officio prout spectat ad ipsum diligenti examinatione praemissa confirmetur vel etiam infirmetur prout eis secundum deum visum fuerit expedire. Et si fuerit infirmata ad capitulum provinciale electio huiusmodi revertatur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p227","Concerning the Election of Provincial Ministers","Moreover, if the aforesaid chapter shall have omitted to elect a minister on the said day, then the provision of the provincial minister is freely devolved to the general minister.","Ceterum si capitulum memoratum die praedicta ministrum eligere praetermittat extunc ministri provincialis provisio ad generalem ministrum libere devolvatur."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p228","Concerning the Election of Provincial Ministers","But if to the said general minister and general chapter, for a certain, manifest, and reasonable cause, it should seem expedient at some time in the overseas provinces, Ireland, or Greece, or Rome—in which up to now another mode of providing is said to have been preserved for a certain and reasonable cause—that a provincial minister be placed over the province by the general minister with the counsel of upright men of the order rather than by the election of the aforesaid chapter, let this be observed irrefragably in the provinces of Ireland and also overseas; but in the Roman or Greek [province], when the minister of the said province should die or be absolved on this side of the sea, for that turn let it be observed without deceit, partiality, and fraud—concerning which we burden their consciences—and let the said minister ordain concerning this with the counsel of the said upright men as shall have seemed [expedient].","Verum si ministro praedicto et capitulo generalibus ex certa manifesta ac rationabili causa videretur aliquando in provinciis ultramarinis Hyberniae seu Graeciae seu Romae in quibus hactenus alius providendi modus dicitur ex causa certa et rationabili fuisse servatus expedire ministrum provincialem per ministrum generalem cum proborum ordinis consilio potius quam per capituli praedicti electionem praefici in provinciis Hyberniae etiam ultramarinis irrefragabiliter in Romana vero vel Graeca quando minister dictae provinciae moreretur vel absolveretur citra mare illa vice servetur absque dolo partialitate et fraude super quo eorum conscientias oneramus quod super hoc dictus minister cum dictorum proborum consilio duxerit ordinandum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p229","Concerning the Election of Provincial Ministers","In the destitution, however, of the said provincial ministers, we wish to be observed what has been observed up to now concerning this in the order itself.","In destitutione vero dictorum ministrorum provincialium servari volumus quod super hoc hactenus de ipso ordine exstiterit observatum."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p230","Concerning the Election of Provincial Ministers","Moreover, if it should happen that they lack a general minister, what is to be done by the same minister shall be done by the vicar of the order, until provision has been made concerning a general minister.","Ceterum si contingeret eosdem ministro generali carere per vicarium ordinis fiat super hoc quod faciendum fuerit per eundem ministrum usque quo provisum fuerit de generali ministro."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p231","Concerning the Election of Provincial Ministers","Furthermore, if anything to the contrary concerning such a provincial minister shall happen to be attempted, let it be ipso facto invalid and void.","Porro si quid de huiusmodi ministro provinciali secus attentari forte contigerit illud ipso facto sit irritum et inane."],["vol2/vienne/index.html","p232","Concerning the Election of Provincial Ministers","Let no one therefore ... If anyone ....","Nulli igitur .... Si quis autem ...."]];